k': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rotenberg, Marc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Electronic Privacy Information Center', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Research Center', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-01T15:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f2b2e22-67e5-58bd-957f-c1decd598697', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f2b2e22-67e5-58bd-957f-c1decd598697'}, {'abstract': 'Television listings for Sunday, June 1.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television listings for Sunday, June 1.', 'lead_paragraph': '10 P.M. (AMC) HALT AND CATCH FIRE Set in Texas during the 1980s tech boom, this new series stars Lee Pace (“Lincoln,” “Pushing Daisies”) as Joe McMillan, a former IBM salesman who enlists the help of a has-been computer engineer (Scoot McNairy, above left, with Mackenzie Davis and Mr. Pace) and a talented computer science student (Ms. Davis) to reverse-engineer an IBM PC. The story is fiction, but based on the actual technology race of that decade, in which companies were trying to create their own version of a PC. With AMC’s “Mad Men,” focusing on the ad executive Don Draper, ending next year, the network “seems to be looking for another period drama that can seduce viewers with old-fashioned customs and atavistic sins,” Alessandra Stanley wrote in The New York Times. “Joe, like Don, is handsome, suave and moodily enigmatic, a salesman who pitches his product with passionate intensity.” Unfortunately, “Halt and Catch Fire” doesn’t seem to carry the same panache. “The period music is fun, but there is nothing particularly glamorous about high-tech Dallas. Most of all, the early 1980s are not a distant enough memory to cast a retro glow.” ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-01T15:19:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/53ff0cbd-86d8-5217-ad13-9bb5c4cb23ac', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53ff0cbd-86d8-5217-ad13-9bb5c4cb23ac'}, {'abstract': 'With a defeat against Ernests Gulbis at the French Open, Roger Federer has now lost before the quarterfinals in three of his last four Grand Slam events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/02/sports/tennis/Federer-Ousted-at-French-Open.html', 'snippet': 'With a defeat against Ernests Gulbis at the French Open, Roger Federer has now lost before the quarterfinals in three of his last four Grand Slam events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Roger Federer and Ernests Gulbis are both running out of time.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/sports/clarey1/clarey1-artic