'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glazer, Elizabeth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-02T02:19:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6907e940-5bd3-526a-9dca-73a2f07394ce', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6907e940-5bd3-526a-9dca-73a2f07394ce'}, {'abstract': 'The Broadway musical “A Gentleman’s Guide to Love & Murder” led the trophy tally with seven on Sunday night at the Drama Desk Awards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/02/theater/drama-desk-embraces-a-gentlemans-guide.html', 'snippet': 'The Broadway musical “A Gentleman’s Guide to Love & Murder” led the trophy tally with seven on Sunday night at the Drama Desk Awards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Broadway musical “A Gentleman’s Guide to Love & Murder” led the trophy tally with seven on Sunday night at the Drama Desk Awards, which honor Broadway and Off Broadway productions. “Gentleman’s Guide,” a strong contender in next weekend’s Tonys competition, won for outstanding musical, outstanding actor (Jefferson Mays), featured actress (Lauren Worsham), director (Darko Tresnjak), lyrics, book and projection design. “Beautiful: The Carole King Musical” followed with three awards, including for actress in a musical (Jessie Mueller as Carole King), sound design and featured actress (Anika Larsen, who tied with Ms. Worsham). Robert Schenkkan’s “All the Way” won for outstanding play and actor (Bryan Cranston as Lyndon B. Johnson), with other top awards for plays going to Audra McDonald (“Lady Day at Emerson’s Bar & Grill”) for best actress and “Twelfth Night” for outstanding revival and director (Tim Carroll). In the musical category, “Hedwig and the Angry Inch” took honors for outstanding revival and actor (Neil Patrick Harris as Hedwig, who shared the award with Mr. Mays). Featured acting awards went to Reed Birney (“Casa Valentina”), Celia Keenan-Bolger (“The Glass Menagerie”) and James Monroe Iglehart (“Aladdin”).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/theater/02guide/02guide-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 431, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/02/theater/02guide/02guide-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 431}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/theater/02guide/02guide-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 735, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/theater/02guide/02guide-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1470, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/theater/02guide/02guide-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/02/theater/02guide/02guide-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/theater/02guide/02guide-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name