me': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SCOTUSblog', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Denniston, Lyle', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldstein, Thomas C', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-02T14:43:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47e050ff-ef85-50f6-ae9d-0286e4c3312c', 'word_count': 1007, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47e050ff-ef85-50f6-ae9d-0286e4c3312c'}, {'abstract': 'Over the course of 15 years, Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif of Pakistan has courted and been at odds with neighboring India.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/02/pakistani-premier-in-delicate-dance-with-india/', 'snippet': 'Over the course of 15 years, Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif of Pakistan has courted and been at odds with neighboring India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pakistan’s prime minister, Nawaz Sharif, returned home from New Delhi to\xa0faint praise\xa0and\xa0considerable\xa0criticism, the latter suggesting that his 30-hour sojourn in Delhi to attend the swearing-in ceremony of Prime Minister Narendra Modi last week had been his riskiest gamble yet in improving his country’s relations with India.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistani Premier in Delicate Dance With India', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharif, Nawaz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lashkar-e-Taiba', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-02T14:46:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By JYOTI MALHOTRA', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jyoti', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MALHOTRA', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd43cbe3-94d6-5016-b659-ae2ec8c1a1f9', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd43cbe3-94d6-5016-b659-ae2ec8c1a1f9'}, {'abstract': 'Blame the weather. The city of eternal sunshine has become a haven for many a creative Swede.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/02/swedish-takeover-of-los-angeles-austere-ayd-acne-icona-pop/', 'snippet': 'Blame the weather. The city of eternal sunshine has become a haven for many a creative Swede.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Throngs of Swedes eager to escape the land of the midnight sun are traveling to the land of eternal sunshine. Blame the obvious (weather), the logistical (Norwegian Air Shuttle’s new direct flight from Stockholm) or the sartorial (Acne Studios’ opening of a West Coast outpost); whatever the reason, Los Angeles has become a new home away from ‘Holm for many Swedish creatives. “Without fail, every single day there’s a bunch of Swedes in the lobby,” says Fredrik Carlstrom, who ensconced himself at the newish Ace Hotel while overseeing the recent launch of Austere, a nearby Scandi-centric retail showroom and event space. Come late next year, downtown L.A. will also house AYD (short for “All Your Dreams”), a Nordic-influenced members club designed by Andreas Fornell (formerly Acne’s in-house architect). AYD will boast Swedish chefs (Adam & Albin of Matstudio in Stockholm) and club “ambassadors” like the pop icon Robyn. “A lot of people are skipping New York and going straight to L.A.,” says Patrik Berger, who co-produced the Swedish duo Icona Pop’s massive 2012 hit “I Love It.” “It’s all in L.A. when it comes to pop music.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/t-magazine/15culture-look-swede/15culture-look-swede-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/t-magazine/15culture-look-swede/15culture-look-swede-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/t-magazine/15culture-look-swede/15culture-look-swede-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/t-magazine/15culture-look-swede/15culture-look-swede-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1354, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/t-magazine/15culture-look-swede/15culture-look-swede-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/t-magazine/15culture-look-swede/15culture-look-swede-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/t-magazine/15culture-look-swede/15culture-look-swede-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main':