{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-02T17:24:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e91240f-fb32-5ed6-ab2e-761c357f0ece', 'word_count': 1312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e91240f-fb32-5ed6-ab2e-761c357f0ece'}, {'abstract': 'It’s a newly discovered annual migration of several thousand zebra in Botswana and Namibia, one of the longest animal journeys in Africa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/02/science/whats-black-and-white-and-travels-310-miles.html', 'snippet': 'It’s a newly discovered annual migration of several thousand zebra in Botswana and Namibia, one of the longest animal journeys in Africa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Given all the attention that has been paid to migrations of wildlife in Africa it’s a surprise to find a new one, not to mention one that is the longest of any on that continent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Black and White and Travels 310 Miles?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Animal Migration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Zebras', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Botswana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Namibia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-02T17:25:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/974359e3-3205-5f0a-97fd-b2effa8b18f0', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/974359e3-3205-5f0a-97fd-b2effa8b18f0'}, {'abstract': 'Why the cultural right shouldn’t reflexively disagree with the feminist left.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/02/the-right-and-misogyny/', 'snippet': 'Why the cultural right shouldn’t reflexively disagree with the feminist left.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My column yesterday joined the crowded debate over the cultural significance (if any) of the Santa Barbara killer’s radioactive, misogynistic, sexually-frustrated rage. That debate has gone in any number of directions, but it’s often slipped into some well-worn right-left grooves, with writers on the feminist left tilting at capital-p Patriarchy and “traditional masculinity”\xa0and conservative commentators dismissing their arguments\xa0as victimology\xa0and ideological overreach.\xa0And as in another recent controversy, on rape and campus life, I’m a little dissatisfied with my own side’s approach: In\xa0their haste to critique too-sweeping indictments of masculinity/male privilege/”rape culture”/etc., it seems to me, some conservatives risk foreclosing the possibility that misogyny might be a real cultural problem, to which a certain amount of moral outrage and a certain kind of structural critique might both be healthy, legitimate responses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Right and Misogyny', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Max, Tucker', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spiegel, Evan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Snapchat Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Santa Barbara (Calif)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-02T17:38:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b41c1e4f-33f2-59f5-9e56-fa8016fdba8e', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b41c1e4f-33f2-59f5-9e56-fa8016fdba8e'}, {'abstract': 'King Juan Carlos of Spain said that he would abdicate the throne after nearly 40 years, and that his son, Crown Prince Felipe, would succeed him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/europe/100000002915006/spain-king-juan-carlos-abdicates.html', 'snippet': 'King Juan Carlos of Spain said that he would abdicate the throne after nearly 40 years, and that his son, Crown Prince Felipe, would succeed him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'King Juan Carlos of Spain said that he would abdicate the throne after nearly 40 years, and that his son, Crown Prince Felipe, would succeed him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/multimedia/king-spain-abdication/king-spain-abdication-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 445, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/02/multimedia/king-spain-a