Tiananmen massacre argues that it was crucial to understanding today’s China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Louisa Lim’s new book, “The People’s Republic of Amnesia: Tiananmen Revisited,” describes how government policy and fast economic growth have conspired to make most Chinese ignorant about the June 4, 1989, massacre in Beijing and crackdowns in scores of cities across China. But in an interview, Ms. Lim, a former Beijing correspondent for National Public Radio who recently completed a fellowship in the United States, argued that the massacre was crucial to understanding today’s China. Excerpts follow:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/03/world/03sino-LOUISA/03sino-LOUISA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/03/world/03sino-LOUISA/03sino-LOUISA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 899}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/03/world/03sino-LOUISA/03sino-LOUISA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/03/world/03sino-LOUISA/03sino-LOUISA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/03/world/03sino-LOUISA/03sino-LOUISA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/03/world/03sino-LOUISA/03sino-LOUISA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't