n_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Rappeport', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rappeport', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7896dcfa-6750-5644-a8e9-404b2e700035', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7896dcfa-6750-5644-a8e9-404b2e700035'}, {'abstract': 'When I announced the pregnancy at a family dinner, my father reminded my younger brother that I was “helping out” friends by being their “donor.” “But David’s gay,” he helpfully reminded the table. “How can they make a baby if they’re not having sex?”', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/03/sperm-donation-is-full-of-euphemisms-and-an-11-year-old-is-full-of-questions/', 'snippet': 'When I announced the pregnancy at a family dinner, my father reminded my younger brother that I was “helping out” friends by being their “donor.” “But David’s gay,” he helpfully reminded the table. “How can they make a baby if they’re not having s...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since agreeing to become a sperm donor for my good friends, Tori and Kelly, I’ve noticed an impressive variety of euphemisms are used when discussing the subject in polite company. My mother regularly calls to inquire how “that thing” is going “with the girls,” for example, and acquaintances will praise me for my decision to “donate,” as if I’ve just dropped off some gently used housewares at my local Salvation Army.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/us/motherlode-private-sign/motherlode-private-sign-jumbo.png', 'height': 105, 'width': 196, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/us/motherlode-private-sign/motherlode-private-sign-superJumbo.png', 'height': 105, 'width': 196, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/02/us/motherlode-private-sign/motherlode-private-sign-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/02/us/motherlode-private-sign/motherlode-private-sign-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sperm Donation is Full of Euphemisms, and an 11-Year-Old is Full of Questions', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARTIFICIAL INSEMINATION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'In Vitro Fertilization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infertility', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-03T14:43:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Dodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ca19dc4-4afc-5f9a-ae13-9a3c856e5ae6', 'word_count': 775, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ca19dc4-4afc-5f9a-ae13-9a3c856e5ae6'}, {'abstract': 'Opinions on Obama’s proposed carbon-emissions rules, the European Union elections, the Veterans Affairs issues and Afghan interpreters.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/03/morning-views-2/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on Obama’s proposed carbon-emissions rules, the European Union elections, the Veterans Affairs issues and Afghan interpreters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama’s proposed emissions restrictions on power plants may be insufficient by themselves to forestall the severest impacts of climate change. But they are ultimately “good for the nation’s health, good for technological innovation, good for President Obama’s credibility abroad, and, in time, good for the health of the planet and future generations,” says  The New York Times editorial board.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/03/opinion/optalk-bug/optalk-bug-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 200, 'width': 200, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/03/opinion/optalk-bug/optalk-bug-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 200, 'width': 200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/03/opinion/optalk-bug/optalk-bug-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/03/opinion/optalk-bug/optalk-bug-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/03/opinion/optalk-bug/optalk-bug-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-03T14:59:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3823041-a358-53fa-98c4-b391be7fec18', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3823041-a358-53fa-98c4-b391be7fec18'}, {'abstract': 'Before Hollywood and a name change, Norma Jeane Dougherty worked in a war plant that manufactured an early version of today’s remote-controlled pilotless aircraft.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/04/upshot/marilyn-monroes-world-war-ii-drone-program.html', 'snippet': 'Before Hollywood and a name change, Norma Jeane Dougherty worked in a war plant that manufactured an early version of today’s remote-controlled pilotless aircraft.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Working 10 hours a day for $20 a week in a World War II defense plant 70 years ago was 18-year-old Norma Jeane Dougherty, wife of a young United States merchant seaman assigned overseas. Her company, called Radioplane, in Burbank, Calif., founded by the British character actor Reginald Denny, made small remote-controlled pilotless aircraft, intended to help United States Army and Navy anti-aircraft gunners refine their targeting skills.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/business/04UP-MARILYN/04UP-MARILYN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/04/business/04UP-MARILYN/04UP-MARILYN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 731}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/business/04UP-MARILYN/04UP-MARILYN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 841, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'