ashberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6f1f37f-1264-538c-8bf0-57ab2b86128d', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6f1f37f-1264-538c-8bf0-57ab2b86128d'}, {'abstract': 'Last week, President Obama, speaking at West Point, made clear that the end of combat in Afghanistan is near. An Air Force officer who deployed to Iraq and Afghanistan struggles to find clarity about what it all meant.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/03/my-wars-are-ending/', 'snippet': 'Last week, President Obama, speaking at West Point, made clear that the end of combat in Afghanistan is near. An Air Force officer who deployed to Iraq and Afghanistan struggles to find clarity about what it all meant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Dec. 31, the Air Force shut down rescue operations at Bastion Air Base in Afghanistan. It closed five years of lifesaving in the Helmand Province by “Pedro” and “Guardian,” as the HH-60G Pave Hawks and Pararescue/Combat Rescue officers were known, and became just the latest in the list of places I’ve gone, to which I will go no more. The first ones were Balad Air Base and Camp Victory, in Iraq. Then Kandahar, and most recently, Camp Bastion in Afghanistan. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Wars Are Ending', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AIR NATIONAL GUARD', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALASKA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Camp Victory (Iraq)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Helmand Province (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kandahar (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-03T21:39:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Komatsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Komatsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f95c926d-1baf-5fc1-8116-93f7c94400d7', 'word_count': 682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f95c926d-1baf-5fc1-8116-93f7c94400d7'}, {'abstract': 'American Ballet Theater’s revival of Kenneth MacMillan’s 1974 “Manon” features new sets and costumes and dramatic and delirious dancing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/04/arts/dance/julie-kent-stars-in-american-ballet-theaters-manon.html', 'snippet': 'American Ballet Theater’s revival of Kenneth MacMillan’s 1974 “Manon” features new sets and costumes and dramatic and delirious dancing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The dramatic fortitude required to pull off a good Manon — the troubled courtesan drawn between greed and love — is fairly attainable. Kenneth MacMillan’s earthy, naturalistic choreography reads like text. But for a great Manon, more is at stake. A ballerina doesn’t so much dance the role as let it consume her body, bit by bit. It’s like a fever.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/arts/manon/manon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/04/arts/manon/manon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 