ion’s two largest markets meet, some wonder if this is the formula for record viewership.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Stanley Cup Finals, Big Markets Don’t Mean Big Viewership', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t Expect Coast-to-Coast Fervor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stanley Cup', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings (Audience Measurement)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Kings', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flood, Sam', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T01:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc499597-950f-57ed-a242-a8bffeeefd25', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc499597-950f-57ed-a242-a8bffeeefd25'}, {'abstract': 'Is Jim Hilger playing with us?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/03/let-sleeping-dogs-lie/', 'snippet': 'Is Jim Hilger playing with us?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEDNESDAY’S PUZZLE — It’s game night here at Wordplay, and Jim Hilger has gotten out some of his favorites for us.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Let Sleeping Dogs Lie', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scrabble (Game)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T02:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51bfd1be-071e-5fde-bcf3-bfab230001a3', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51bfd1be-071e-5fde-bcf3-bfab230001a3'}, {'abstract': 'Jim Dale’s one-man show, “Just Jim Dale,” revisits the highs and pratfalls of his long and varied career in show business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/04/theater/just-jim-dale-says-it-all-and-does-it-all.html', 'snippet': 'Jim Dale’s one-man show, “Just Jim Dale,” revisits the highs and pratfalls of his long and varied career in show business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his light-footed and lovable new solo show, “Just Jim Dale,” the man of the hour and a half recalls an early turning point in his path toward a long career in show business. When the young Mr. Dale was taken to his first West End musical, “Me and My Girl,” he watched a comic dazzle the audience with a tumble into the orchestra pit and felt a sudden, churning yearning to be up there, basking in the laughter. He turned to his father and asked how he might make a go of it. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/arts/04JUSTJIM/04JUSTJIM-articleLarge.jpg