-minors-on-their-own-rush-to-cross-southwest-border.html', 'snippet': 'A perception that the United States is treating children of migrants better than in past years is fueling much of the new migration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN PEDRO SULA, Honduras — After a decade apart, 13-year-old Robin Tulio was finally heading to the border to be with his mother. A maid, living illegally in Baltimore, she had decided the time was right to smuggle her son into the United States.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wave of Minors on Their Own Rush to Cross Southwest Border', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wave of Minors on Their Own Rush to Cross Southwest Border', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robles, Frances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deportation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Customs and Border Protection (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Homeland Security Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central America', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Honduras', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T04:50:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frances Robles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frances', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f356f064-aa34-5971-a2a5-bc0085135b7b', 'word_count': 1245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f356f064-aa34-5971-a2a5-bc0085135b7b'}, {'abstract': 'Egypt’s elections commission on Tuesday formally declared the former defense chief, Abdel Fattah el-Sisi, the winner of last week’s pro forma presidential election with nearly 97 percent of the vote.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/04/world/middleeast/egypt-sisi-wins-with-97-percent.html', 'snippet': 'Egypt’s elections commission on Tuesday formally declared the former defense chief, Abdel Fattah el-Sisi, the winner of last week’s pro forma presidential election with nearly 97 percent of the vote.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Egypt’s elections commission on Tuesday formally declared the former defense chief, Abdel Fattah el-Sisi, the winner of last week’s pro forma presidential election with nearly 97 percent of the vote. Turnout was about 47 percent of eligible voters, far short of what he had sought to prove he had a mandate to lead and less than the 52 percent who voted in the 2012 election won by Mohamed Morsi, whom Mr. Sisi overthrew last year. Foreign observers have said the election fell short of international standards. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt: Sisi Wins With 97 Percent', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt: Sisi Wins With 97 Percent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheikh, Mayy El', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sisi, Abdel Fattah el-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T04:52:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mayy El Sheikh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mayy', 'middlename': 'El', 'lastname': 'Sheikh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/68f00b1b-9726-54a8-91ec-7fb6247cdf84', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68f00b1b-9726-54a8-91ec-7fb6247cdf84'}, {'abstract': 'The most recent flare-up in the long-running fight between the Yemeni Army and Shiite insurgents in the country’s north has killed hundreds of people in the past three weeks, local officials said Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/04/world/middleeast/yemen-deadly-fighting-flares.html', 'snippet': 'The most recent flare-up in the long-running fight between the Yemeni Army and Shiite insurgents in the country’s north has killed hundreds of people in the past three weeks, local officials said Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The most recent flare-up in the long-running fight between the Yemeni Army and Shiite insurgents in the country’s north has killed hundreds of people in the past three weeks, local officials said Tuesday. The clashes, in Amran Province, began when the rebels sought to claim new territory closer to the capital, said Ahmad al-Bakry, the province’s deputy governor. He said more than 500 people had been killed, including 100 rebels and 20 soldiers in the past two days. The toll could not be independently verified. On Monday, the government sent jets to bomb rebel areas after rebels came close to taking over a government checkpoint, Mr. Bakry said. A Houthi rebel leader, Ali al-Bikhiti, linked the violence to grievances over governmental neglect. “What is happening in Amran is a revolt against injustice,” Mr. Bikhiti said. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yemen: Deadly Fighting Flares', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yemen: Deadly Fighting Flares', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T04:52:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Hubbard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hubbard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba0b00ed-1114-59b6-99d7-41ba894196f5', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba0b00ed-1114-59b6-99d7-41ba894196f5'}, {'abstract': 'Saudi Arabia revised sharply upward the number of cases of the new MERS virus within its borders and said it had been fatal in more than 40 percent of the cases.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/04/health/saudi-arabia-mers-toll-revised.html', 'snippet': 'Saudi Arabia revised sharply upward the number of cases of the new MERS virus within its borders and said it had been fatal in more than 40 percent of the cases.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Saudi Arabia revised sharply upward the number of cases of the new MERS virus within its borders and said it had been fatal in more than 40 percent of the cases. After a review of medical records, the country’s total number of cases of Middle East Respiratory Syndrome since the virus was isolated in 2012 is now 688, up from 575; the death toll is 282, not 190. The country has had a surge of cases since March — many apparently originating in hospitals — but they seem to be falling off, an adviser to the Health Ministry said. That surge led to the dismissal of the health minister, and on Monday, his replacement dismissed the deputy health minister who has been the public face of the country’s response. The deputy, Dr. Ziad Memish, was recently  profiled in The Lancet for his preparations for the annual visit to Mecca by up to three million Muslims. Most of the 133 new cases are from March and April, suggesting that many were either not tested or not reported. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saudi Arabia: MERS Toll Revised', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saudi Arabia: MERS Toll Revised', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'MERS (Middle East Respiratory Syndrome)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNeil, Donald G Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T04:52:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe072878-1cbe-52ec-9efd-a0d7d00aeab8', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe072878-1cbe-52ec-9efd-a0d7d00aeab8'}, {'abstract': 'Maps of where Bowe Bergdahl was stationed and the initial operations to find him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2014/06/03/world/asia/where-bergdahl-disappeared.html', 'snippet': 'Maps of where Bowe Bergdahl was stationed and the initial operations to find him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maps of where Bowe Bergdahl was stationed and the initial operations to find him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'capt