ride Corp", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pinnacle Foods Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tyson Foods Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T17:52:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b18ef5a-6a82-5aab-b3b5-7f2c3b402d25', 'word_count': 788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b18ef5a-6a82-5aab-b3b5-7f2c3b402d25'}, {'abstract': 'The pop star with the bouffant hairdo and the sci-fi look, who performs a concert tonight in Brooklyn, offers a thoughtful set of creative inspirations.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/04/janelle-monae-electric-lady-inspirations-prospect-park/', 'snippet': 'The pop star with the bouffant hairdo and the sci-fi look, who performs a concert tonight in Brooklyn, offers a thoughtful set of creative inspirations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Janelle Monáe is both a major pop star and a bona fide style icon. With her signature bouffant hairdo and mildly androgynous tailored suits in sharp monochrome patterns, she is simultaneously retro and futuristic, a strikingly recognizable (and vaguely alien) figure in the cultural landscape. Originally from Kansas City, Kan., Monáe first gained recognition for her 2007 concept EP “Metropolis: Suite 1 (The Chase)” (loosely inspired by the Fritz Lang film “Metropolis”) and burst into pop stardom in 2010 with “The ArchAndroid,” then followed that up with last year’s “The Electric Lady.” Mixing references with abandon, her music channels the otherworldliness of the funk icons Curtis Mayfield, Parliament and Earth, Wind and Fire and the icy cool of Grace Jones, David Bowie and Prince, while incorporating her contemporary collaborators Big Boi (of Outkast) and Erykah Badu. Yet her influences reveal a warmth and depth beneath her stylized public persona. She shared them in advance of a concert for Celebrate Brooklyn tonight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04monae-miller-main/04monae-miller-main-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 749, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04monae-miller-main/04monae-miller-main-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 749}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04monae-miller-main/04monae-miller-main-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 821, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04monae-miller-main/04monae-miller-main-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1682, 'width': 1348, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04monae-miller-main/04monae-miller-main-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04monae-miller-main/04monae-miller-main-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04monae-miller-main/04monae-miller-main-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Janelle Monáe on Daily Devotionals, Electric Bicycles and Afro-Futurism', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Monae, Janelle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T18:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9dd85886-7e3f-5389-b2ca-82a2d7ae7a95', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9dd85886-7e3f-5389-b2ca-82a2d7ae7a95'}, {'abstract': 'The cartoonist Bob Eckstein takes readers through each of the Rangers’ 10 trips to the Stanley Cup finals, beginning in 1928.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2014/06/05/sports/hockey/05eckstein-cup.html', 'snippet': 'The cartoonist Bob Eckstein takes readers through each of the Rangers’ 10 trips to the Stanley Cup finals, beginning in 1928.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cartoonist Bob Eckstein takes readers through each of the Rangers’ 10 trips to the Stanley Cup finals, beginning in 1928.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/05/sports/hockey/05eckstein-cup-slide-DTOZ/05eckstein-cup-slide-DTOZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/05/sports/hockey/05eckstein-cup-slide-DTOZ/05eckstein-cup-slide-DTOZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 708}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/05/sports/hockey/05eckstein-cup-slide-DT