], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T18:59:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94661efd-42e0-5560-b97f-d40ccec73ae7', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94661efd-42e0-5560-b97f-d40ccec73ae7'}, {'abstract': 'The Williamsburg stronghold is installing an artistic pop-up church on its sixth floor terrace, where L.G.B.T. couples can come to tie the knot throughout New York’s upcoming Pride Week.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/04/happenings-wythe-hotel-pride-week-tom-fruin-andrew-tarlow-peter-lawrence-lgbt/', 'snippet': 'The Williamsburg stronghold is installing an artistic pop-up church on its sixth floor terrace, where L.G.B.T. couples can come to tie the knot throughout New York’s upcoming Pride Week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Church, like home, is where you find it. During New York’s Pride Week at the end of this month, gay couples looking to get married will find a church in the unlikeliest of places: on the rooftop of the Wythe Hotel in Williamsburg, Brooklyn. Andrew Tarlow, one of the hotel’s founders, came up with the idea of putting a pop-up wedding chapel on the sixth-floor terrace as a tribute to the members of the L.G.B.T. community who live in the neighborhood, especially those who had worked with him for many years at his nearby restaurant Diner.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04wythe-ashlock/04wythe-ashlock-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 526, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04wythe-ashlock/04wythe-ashlock-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 526}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04wythe-ashlock/04wythe-ashlock-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04wythe-ashlock/04wythe-ashlock-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1711, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04wythe-ashlock/04wythe-ashlock-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04wythe-ashlock/04wythe-ashlock-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/t-magazine/04wythe-ashlock/04wythe-ashlock-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Happenings | Love and Marriage on the Roof of the Wythe Hotel', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Happenings: Pride and Love', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wythe Hotel (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T19:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Ashlock', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ashlock', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbdb2f85-a8e3-56ed-bced-82e1e2a8fda7', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbdb2f85-a8e3-56ed-bced-82e1e2a8fda7'}, {'abstract': 'A historian traces the desire for information that led eventually to newspapers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/books/review/the-invention-of-news-by-andrew-pettegree.html', 'snippet': 'A historian traces the desire for information that led eventually to newspapers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a fundamental human impulse to want to know more than you can hear over the back fence. Curiosity about people, places and events that matter but cannot be seen from where you stand has civic, commercial and prurient origins. In his new book, Andrew Pettegree argues that this need to know led to the establishment of postal routes, private back channels and, eventually, newspapers. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-bks-CARR/0608-bks-CARR-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-bks-CARR/0608-bks-CARR-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 710}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-bks-CARR/0608-bks-CARR-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 866, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp