'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/d91e12f0-e346-58e4-838c-85452333300a', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d91e12f0-e346-58e4-838c-85452333300a'}, {'abstract': 'Gunmen in the central Libyan city of Surt fatally shot a Swiss man working for the Red Cross on Wednesday, the humanitarian group said in a statement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/05/world/africa/libya-swiss-red-cross-worker-is-killed.html', 'snippet': 'Gunmen in the central Libyan city of Surt fatally shot a Swiss man working for the Red Cross on Wednesday, the humanitarian group said in a statement.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Gunmen in the central Libyan city of Surt fatally shot a Swiss man working for the Red Cross on Wednesday, the organization said in a statement. The man, Michael Greub, 42, was leaving a meeting with colleagues when their vehicle came under attack, it said. No possible motives were disclosed, but foreign workers and diplomats have come under increasing threat from armed groups that have operated with impunity in Libya since its revolt in 2011. The killing came amid intensifying violence in the eastern city of Benghazi, where a self-proclaimed army leader, Gen. Khalifa Hifter declared war on Islamist militias almost three weeks ago. He was lightly wounded Wednesday when a suicide bomber drove an explosives-laden truck into a base where the general was staying east of Benghazi. Four of his guards died in the attack, according to one of the general’s aides. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Libya: Swiss Red Cross Worker Is Killed', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Libya: Swiss Red Cross Worker Is Killed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fahim, Kareem', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Workers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Surt (Libya)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greub, Michael', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Red Cross', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Switzerland', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Committee of the Red Cross', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T23:32:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/689b1ac3-7812-5aa1-8f20-bf20f0a16d50', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/689b1ac3-7812-5aa1-8f20-bf20f0a16d50'}, {'abstract': 'The government of Canada introduced legislation on Wednesday that would criminalize buying sexual services but would not, in many situations, make selling them illegal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/05/world/americas/canada-prostitution-bill-targets-clients.html', 'snippet': 'The government of Canada introduced legislation on Wednesday that would criminalize buying sexual services but would not, in many situations, make selling them illegal.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The government of Canada introduced legislation on Wednesday that would criminalize buying sexual services but would not, in many situations, make selling them illegal. The bill follows a Canadian Supreme Court ruling in December that overturned most laws governing the sex trade on the basis that they violated prostitutes’ constitutional right to security. While Justice Minister Peter MacKay said the bill aimed to focus on pimps and the customers of prostitutes, he added that prostitutes who offered their services in public areas where children might be present would, in most cases, be fined. The bill would also make advertising sexual services a crime. Some lawyers for prostitutes argued that the measure did not address the court’s concern about prostitutes’ safety. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Canada: Prostitution Bill Targets Clients', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Canada: Prostitution Bill Targets Clients', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostitution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Ian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court of Canada', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-04T23:32:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cde6c089-3bcc-55f5-88e7-8d9bb5013a3b', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cde6c089-3bcc-55f5-88e7-8d9bb5013a3b'}, {'abstract': 'The Syrian president’s opponents mocked his campaign slogan, “Together,” featured in a series of upbeat advertisements posted on his YouTube channel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/05/world/middleeast/assads-soft-focus-vision-for-syria-as-seen-on-youtube.html', 'snippet': 'The Syrian president’s opponents mocked his campaign slogan, “Together,” featured in a series of upbeat advertisements posted on his YouTube channel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As unexpected as it might have seemed three summers ago, when hundreds of thousands of Syrians jammed the streets demanding that President Bashar al-Assad just “leave,” his landslide re-election, confirmed shortly after polls closed on Wednesday, surprised no one. Given that his supporters, in regions of the deeply divided nation now under government control, openly boasted of voting early and often, and that his opponents largely ignored the contest, the huge margin of victory was entirely believable.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/world/middleeast/04open_instagram/04open_instagram-articleLarge.png', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/04/world/middleeast/04open_instagram/04open_instagram-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/world/middleeast/04open_instagram/04open_instagram-jumbo.png', 'height': 607, 'width': 606, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/world/middleeast/04open_instagram/04open_instagram-superJumbo.png', 'height': 607, 'width': 606, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/04/world/middleeast/04open_instagram/04open_instagram-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/04/world/middleeast/04open_instagram/04open_instagram-thumbSt