rench transplants on the 12th floor of the Pacific Mutual Building, offers a well-curated selection of furniture, objects, leather goods and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The puzzlingly named Please Do Not Enter is the newest intriguing venture to launch in downtown Los Angeles, that burgeoning mecca of all things cool and compelling. A cross between a gallery and a boutique, Please Do Not Enter offers a collection of contemporary goods that have been carefully curated by its French founders Nicolas Libert and Emmanuel Renoird for an audience of “progressive minds.” Both are art collectors with a special affection for design and architecture, and the space is like an extension of their lives.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Downtown L.A., a Stylish Design Gallery for Special Guys', 'kicker': 'By Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-05T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ff05541-a454-53e0-9020-54e641d597bd', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ff05541-a454-53e0-9020-54e641d597bd'}, {'abstract': 'In an upcoming exhibition, Brea Souders introduces two distinct bodies of work that share the same tone but never the same meanings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/05/t-magazine/brea-souders-surrealism-film-electric-counterforms.html', 'snippet': 'In an upcoming exhibition, Brea Souders introduces two distinct bodies of work that share the same tone but never the same meanings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this weekly series, T’s photo editors share the most compelling visual projects they’ve discovered.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Surrealist Photographs of Life’s In-Between Moments', 'kicker': 'Viewfinder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-05T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TCulture', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jamie Sims', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jamie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sims', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c2e66e8-ea3d-5dfb-b811-300f7abfda3f', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c2e66e8-ea3d-5dfb-b811-300f7abfda3f'}, {'abstract': 'FriendsWithYou, purveyors of all things happy and cute, are releasing a monograph with Rizzoli and a cloud-shaped light with Case Studyo and Paul Kasmin Gallery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/05/t-magazine/arturo-sandoval-iii-and-samuel-albert-borkson-little-cloud-lamp.html', 'snippet': 'FriendsWithYou, purveyors of all things happy and cute, are releasing a monograph with Rizzoli and a cloud-shaped light with Case Studyo and Paul Kasmin Gallery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first image in the debut monograph of the Los Angeles-based artist duo of Arturo Sandoval III and Samuel Albert Borkson, a.k.a FriendsWithYou — called “We Are FriendsWithYou” (Rizzoli, $55) — is a group of cute little clouds with black beady eyes and tiny red smiles. The characters are from their short film “Cloudy.” “It’s like a very simplistic, reduced form that everybody can relate to,” Borkson explains. “It reminds you of childhood, it reminds you of your creativity, how people can look at the clouds and dream up whatever they want.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/05/t-mag