levision/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '4:15 P.M. (Starz Cinema) INCENDIES (2011) The adult children (Mélissa Désormeaux-Poulin and Maxim Gaudette) of an Arab woman, Nawal (Lubna Azabal, above), living in Canada travel to an unnamed country that closely resembles Lebanon to seek out a brother they never knew existed on the instructions of Nawal’s last will and testament. In the process, they learn that their mother, a Christian, had fallen in love with a Muslim and fled her hometown, becoming in succession a student, an activist, a clandestine militant, a political prisoner — and more. The director Denis Villeneuve “tells Nawal’s story in a way that is both subtle and emphatic, and Ms. Azabal, portraying Nawal from hopeful youth to despairing middle age, gives a performance that is all the more powerful for the restrained, unshakable sense of dignity she brings to it,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times about this Oscar nominee for best foreign-language film, based on a play by Wajdi Mouawad. “The depth and complexity of her anger is both a product and a mirror of her native country’s self-destructive pathology, and as the full horror of her life is disclosed, she becomes, in her children’s eyes and the audience’s, as grand and tragic as the heroine of an opera.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-05T05:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'ty