icker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-05T20:19:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Media', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a17392c-4154-5d74-95e0-0d8d1fb23daa', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a17392c-4154-5d74-95e0-0d8d1fb23daa'}, {'abstract': 'The sergeant is being attacked in the absence of actual facts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/06/opinion/demonizing-sergeant-bergdahl.html', 'snippet': 'The sergeant is being attacked in the absence of actual facts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four months ago, Senator John McCain said he would support the exchange of five hard-core Taliban leaders for the release of Sgt. Bowe Bergdahl. “I would support,” he told CNN. “Obviously I’d have to know the details, but I would support ways of bringing him home and if exchange was one of them I think that would be something I think we should seriously consider.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rush to Demonize Sgt. Bergdahl', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Rush to Demonize Sgt. Bergdahl ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bergdahl, Bowe R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisoners of War', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCain, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desertion (Military)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-05T20:19:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5c0c95a-4cb2-55d3-b517-6942c0c85157', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5c0c95a-4cb2-55d3-b517-6942c0c85157'}, {'abstract': 'With one eye on Philip Marlowe, Stephen King takes a crack at the detective novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/books/review/stephen-kings-mr-mercedes.html', 'snippet': 'With one eye on Philip Marlowe, Stephen King takes a crack at the detective novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Halfway through “Mr. Mercedes,” billed as Stephen King’s first hard-boiled detective novel, a fedora makes an appearance. It’s a winking gift to the retired cop hero, Bill Hodges, from his first client, Janey Patterson. “Every private dick should have a fedora he can pull down to one eyebrow,” she tells him. Hodges tries it on for size, as do Jerome (his teenage Watson) and even Janey herself. No one seems to hang on to it for long. Then, at a critical point in the story, it disappears in a flash.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-bks-Abbott/0608-bks-Abbott-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'sub