eaturing Tom Cruise and Emily Blunt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06EDGE1/06EDGE1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06EDGE1/06EDGE1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06EDGE1/06EDGE1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06EDGE1/06EDGE1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06EDGE1/06EDGE1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06EDGE1/06EDGE1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06EDGE1/06EDGE1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Clip: ‘Edge of Tomorrow’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-05T21:54:30+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Warner Bros. Pictures', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Warner', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bros', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/bdf058b8-5fc5-5a14-b89f-0e28761f783a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/bdf058b8-5fc5-5a14-b89f-0e28761f783a'}, {'abstract': 'In “Edge of Tomorrow,” Tom Cruise plays a military man caught in a time loop, destined to battle invading aliens day after day in a repeating bid to save Earth from Armageddon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/06/movies/tom-cruise-battles-invaders-in-edge-of-tomorrow.html', 'snippet': 'In “Edge of Tomorrow,” Tom Cruise plays a military man caught in a time loop, destined to battle invading aliens day after day in a repeating bid to save Earth from Armageddon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tell me if you’ve heard this one before. A man wakes up and quickly realizes that he’s repeating yesterday, down to the last meal, salutation and conversation. He’s trapped in a kind of time loop. He can’t escape, but, he realizes, he can change. That may not make sense, given the logic of the space-time continuum, but it works just fine in fiction because, well, it’s fiction. To put it another way, “There are no paradoxes in time travel, there can’t be.” Or so says a character in Robert A. Heinlein’s 1964 novel, “Farnham’s Freehold,” about space, time and the apocalypse. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Killed in Action by Aliens, Over and Over Again', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Killed in Action by Aliens, Over and Over Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Edge of Tomorrow (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruise, Tom', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blunt, Emily', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liman, D