y of Northern Virginia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-05T23:50:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allen C. Guelzo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allen', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Guelzo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f93b775-0ea2-551c-9994-4beeef71ff24', 'word_count': 1450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f93b775-0ea2-551c-9994-4beeef71ff24'}, {'abstract': '“Willow Creek” is a work of restraint rarely seen from its writer and director, Bobcat Goldthwait.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/06/movies/willow-creek-a-good-old-hunt-for-sasquatch.html', 'snippet': '“Willow Creek” is a work of restraint rarely seen from its writer and director, Bobcat Goldthwait.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a comedian, Bobcat Goldthwait can seem like a colicky baby, spitting punch lines punctuated by random screams. As a filmmaker, however, he’s more focused and less predictable, channeling his choleric style into pointed satires on American society that sometimes hit their marks with uncomfortable directness.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06WILLOW/WILLOW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06WILLOW/WILLOW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06WILLOW/WILLOW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'sup