terial': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5b35036-eeef-5bd7-8de8-951d51958cc8', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5b35036-eeef-5bd7-8de8-951d51958cc8'}, {'abstract': 'In “2 Autumns, 3 Winters,” a stylistic homage to French New Wave cinema, three young adults come together by chance and stay together because of something more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/06/movies/2-autumns-3-winters-homage-to-the-new-wave.html', 'snippet': 'In “2 Autumns, 3 Winters,” a stylistic homage to French New Wave cinema, three young adults come together by chance and stay together because of something more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Life doesn’t proceed as methodically as in the screenplay of a skillfully plotted commercial film. Unforeseen events interrupt the flow and destroy the symmetry. The randomness of life and the capriciousness of human nature were recurrent motifs in French New Wave movies of the 1960s and ’70s. And in “2 Autumns, 3 Winters,” the Gallic filmmaker Sébastien Betbeder’s breezy, lighthearted homage to the films of Jean-Luc Godard and others, fate keeps stepping in to alter the destinies of its three principal characters, Arman (Vincent Macaigne), Benjamin (Bastien Bouillon) and Amélie (Maud Wyler). ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06TWOAUTUMNS/06TWOAUTUMNS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06TWOAUTUMNS/06TWOAUTUMNS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/arts/06TWOAUTUMNS/06TWOAUTUMNS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 