4:44:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Hoinski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoinski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7ddb856-0c53-5cc8-bd5b-40065544322e', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7ddb856-0c53-5cc8-bd5b-40065544322e'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/06/arts/television/whats-on-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (TCM) FRIDAY NIGHT SPOTLIGHT: PIRATE PICTURES The comedian Greg Proops takes his humor to the high seas as host of this monthlong festival, which begins with Frank Lloyd’s silent version of “The Sea Hawk” (1924), about a British noble (Milton Sills) sold into slavery who escapes and turns himself into a pirate king. Henry King’s “The Black Swan” (1942), starring Tyrone Power as a swashbuckler intent on cleaning up the Caribbean after he’s named governor of Jamaica, follows at 10:15; Frank Borzage’s “The Spanish Main” (1945), in which a Dutch ship’s captain (Paul Henreid) turns Caribbean pirate and kidnaps the future bride (Maureen O’Hara) of a corrupt Spanish governor (Walter Slezak), at midnight; Felix Feist’s “Pirates of Tripoli” (1955), with Henreid as a buccaneer trying to help a deposed Arab princess (Patricia Medina) reclaim her throne, at 2 a.m.; Sidney Salkow’s “The Golden Hawk” (1952), with Rhonda Fleming and Sterling Hayden as pirates who join forces against yet another corrupt Caribbean governor, at 3:30; and Lew Landers’s “Hurricane Island” (1951), about a captain (Jon Hall) searching for the fountain of youth alongside Ponce de León (Edgar Barrier), at 5.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Fri