 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/books/review/michael-korytas-those-who-wish-me-dead-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Michael Koryta’s “Those Who Wish Me Dead,” two killers start a forest fire to flush out the only witness to their crimes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Koryta is a fearless stylist who has put his hand to ghost stories, historical novels, killer-thrillers, revenge tragedies, morality tales and detective stories. He’s now swinging from the high wire with THOSE WHO WISH ME DEAD (Little, Brown, $26), a heart-thumping backwoods adventure that sends two creatively sadistic killers into Montana’s Beartooth Mountains, where they spark a monster forest fire to flush out the only witness to their crimes: Jace Wilson, a 14-year-old boy. Jace was just trying to escape his reputation as class coward when he dived into a quarry near his Indiana home. But when he swam into a dead man — and spotted the killers flinging a second victim into the water — Jace knew he was going to die. Reborn with a new name, he finds himself among the “bad kids” in a grueling wilderness survival course, parked there when his parents couldn’t be persuaded to put him in a traditional witness protection program. Jace’s hunters, the Blackwell brothers, are heartless sociopaths whose professional detachment is etched in their unnervingly precise grammar and careful diction. (“The way they say things. Like they’re alone in the world. Like it was built for the two of them and they’re lords over it.”) There will be no mercy from that quarter.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08bksCrime/08bksCrime-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 595, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08bksCrime/08bksCrime-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 595}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08bksCrime/08bksCrime-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1016, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08bksCrime/08bksCrime-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2033, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08bksCrime/08bksCrime-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08bksCrime/08bksCrime-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08bksCrime/08bksCrime-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fire on the Mountain', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fire on the Mountain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koryta, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Tom Rob', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Finder, Joseph', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grecian, Alex', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Link, Charlotte', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T14:41:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/163c16cf-c75b-5278-a621-3f4b948cccf1', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/163c16cf-c75b-5278-a621-3f4b948cccf1'}, {'abstract': 'The women in these stories seek new passions and geographies, from Italy to India.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/books/review/the-other-language-by-francesca-marciano.html', 'snippet': 'The women in these stories seek new passions and geographies, from Italy to India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “The Italian System,” a wry, knowing story in Francesca Marciano’s first collection, “The Other Language,” the unnamed protagonist is planning a lifestyle guide for Americans. Seven years earlier — a period that evokes the scent of a fairy tale — she had moved to New York from Rome on a whim, having secured a place in a creative writing workshop at the New School. She fell in love with the city and so has never left, despite the fact that this place she adores hasn’t exactly opened its arms to welcome her. She found work as a language teacher, initially telling herself this is simply “a way to pay the rent.” But, as is often the way with the quotidian, “teaching became her real job and she forgot about other aspirations.” And yet those aspirations don’t quite disappear. In the fresh new days of spring, she determines to put together a book, “The Italian System,” about how to live a Roman life in New York. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08WAGNER/08WAGNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08WAGNER/08WAGNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 715}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08WAGNER/08WAGNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 860, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08WAGNER/08WAGNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1527, 'width': 1282, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08WAGNER/08WAGNER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08WAGNER/08WAGNER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08WAGNER/08WAGNER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Border Crossings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Border Crossings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Other Language (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marciano, Francesca', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T14:41:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erica Wagner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wagner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/832638c1-a2fc-5158-8c5e-f10b1a3d920f', 'word_count': 1197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/832638c1-a2fc-5158-8c5e-f10b1a3d920f'}, {'abstract': 'Jeffrey Masson examines the human propensity for violence and cruelty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/books/review/beasts-by-jeffrey-moussaieff-masson.html', 'snippet': 'Jeffrey Masson examines the human propensity for violence and cruelty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We humans often like to trumpet our uniqueness among all the world’s biodiversity. We pride ourselves on our exalted, big-brained status and our resulting separation from everything else we lump together as “nature.” For Jeffrey Moussaieff Masson, the salient feature of our distinction is neither the power of language nor the potential for extreme acts of creativity nor even an opposable thumb. It is, rather, our “singular capacity for violence.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-BKS-ROSNER/0608-BKS-ROSNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 301, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-BKS-ROSNER/0608-BKS-ROSNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 301}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-BKS-ROSNER/0608-BKS-ROSNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 514, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-BKS-ROSNER/0608-BKS-ROSNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 535, 'width': 1066, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-BKS-ROSNER/0608-BKS-ROSNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-BKS-ROSNER/0608-BKS-ROSNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/0608-BKS-ROSNER/0608-BKS-ROSNER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Acting the Brute', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Acting the Brute', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosner, Hillary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Masson, Jeffrey Moussaieff', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T14:41:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hillary Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hillary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/98fb188d-2935-58a1-8290-482638c94606', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98fb188d-2935-58a1-8290-482638c94606'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NO EASY DAY: The Autobiography of a Navy SEAL — The Firsthand Account of the Mission That Killed Osama bin Laden,  by Mark Owen with Kevin Maurer. (New American Library, $16.) The May 2011 raid on Abbottabad, Pakistan, is the heart of this boots-on-the-ground account, but Owen (a pseudonym for Matt Bissonnette, a former member of the SEALs who served 13 consecutive combat deployments) also details the formation and evolution of one of the military’s most elite units.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T14:41:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7e346b0-2365-563a-9d96-1cc5301e9e8f', 'word_count': 478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7e346b0-2365-563a-9d96-1cc5301e9e8f'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAN’T WE TALK ABOUT SOMETHING MORE PLEASANT?, written and illustrated by Roz Chast. (Bloomsbury, $28.) This scorchingly honest, achingly wistful graphic memoir looks at the last years of Chast’s nonagenarian parents.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T14:42:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c91edd22-8bde-59a1-942d-6078d29ee34d', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c91edd22-8bde-59a1-942d-6078d29ee34d'}, {'abstract': 'Floyd Skloot describes his experience with vertigo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/books/review/revertigo-a-memoir-by-floyd-skloot.html', 'snippet': 'Floyd Skloot describes his experience with vertigo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One recent morning, I helped my son search for his misplaced shoes. They were nowhere to be found, so I took a peek under the couch. This was a rash move. The bend and twist required for \xadunder-couch viewing set off a wicked spin of dizziness, as I feared it would. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08DEDERER/08DEDERER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 877, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08DEDERER/08DEDERER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 877}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08DEDERER/08DEDERER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 701, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08DEDERER/08DEDERER-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1401, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08DEDERER/08DEDERER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08DEDERER/08DEDERER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08DEDERER/08DEDERER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As the World Turns', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As the World Turns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vertigo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skloot, Floyd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T14:46:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Dederer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dederer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3106b5ee-2c18-5767-9fa1-8a4425774651', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3106b5ee-2c18-5767-9fa1-8a4425774651'}, {'abstract': 'A political scientist assesses the climbing costs of higher education and the rise of for-profit universities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/books/review/degrees-of-inequality-by-suzanne-mettler.html', 'snippet': 'A political scientist assesses the climbing costs of higher education and the rise of for-profit universities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re having the wrong conversation about higher education in our country. So argues Suzanne Mettler in her \xadprovocative new book, “Degrees of Inequality,” based on the eight years she spent studying a college system that she argues works well for those born into well-off families but few others. Tuition rates of $50,000 or more at private four-year colleges? The trillion-plus dollars Americans collectively owe on college loans? In \xadMettler’s \xadtelling, those should be the least of our worries when considering the “caste system” that is now higher education in the United States. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08RIVLIN/0608-bks-Rivlin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08RIVLIN/0608-bks-Rivlin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08RIVLIN/0608-bks-Rivlin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08RIVLIN/0608-bks-Rivlin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1064, 'width': 1408, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08RIVLIN/0608-bks-Rivlin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/08/books/review/08RIVLIN/0608-bks-Rivlin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',