k': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Pamela', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pettegree, Andrew', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sehgal, Parul', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, John', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Worthen, Molly', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T17:27:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f000c7ea-5d8b-569e-ac27-b95ee045f3ce', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f000c7ea-5d8b-569e-ac27-b95ee045f3ce'}, {'abstract': 'This weekend, the restaurant is operating out of a large art studio on Greenwich Street. Last night, the wunderkind artist Korakrit Arunanondchai hosted a party for the art and fashion set there.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/06/happenings-a-beloved-bangkok-eatery-bo-lan-bo-songvisava-dylan-jones-korakrit-arunanondchai/', 'snippet': 'This weekend, the restaurant is operating out of a large art studio on Greenwich Street. Last night, the wunderkind artist Korakrit Arunanondchai hosted a party for the art and fashion set there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So many pop-ups happen solely for marketing purposes these days that it’s refreshing to find one that reflects the original intent behind the concept: fleeting, spontaneous pleasure. This weekend, New Yorkers can rejoice in the news that, apropos of nothing, one of Bangkok’s most beloved eateries, Bo.Lan, is operating an outpost in a massive, tumbledown art studio opposite the UPS hangar on Greenwich Street. On Saturday and Sunday, the wife-and-husband chef duo of Bo Songvisava and Dylan Jones (their restaurant’s moniker is a Kimyeification of their names) will dish up a tasting menu of regional Thai specialties for an intimate group of strangers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Happenings | The Beloved Bangkok Eatery Bo.Lan Pops Up in New York', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T17:30:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGarry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d195ff8-ffe8-5a32-b747-267a0d232dd7', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d195ff8-ffe8-5a32-b747-267a0d232dd7'}, {'abstract': 'Nuts and seeds like sunflower, pumpkin, chia and sesame are loaded with nutrients and give dishes heft and a satisfying texture.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/06/cooking-with-nuts-and-seeds/', 'snippet': 'Nuts and seeds like sunflower, pumpkin, chia and sesame are loaded with nutrients and give dishes heft and a satisfying texture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years, nutritionists have been urging us to eat nuts in place of high-carb and sugary snacks. Regulators allow purveyors of peanuts, almonds, walnuts, pistachios, hazelnuts and pecans to make claims that these nuts, as part of a diet low in saturated fats and cholesterol, may help reduce the risk of heart disease. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 