d, 11-year-old boy on suspicion of neglect and child cruelty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/07/world/europe/in-britain-childs-weight-leads-to-parents-arrest.html', 'snippet': 'The police called it a rare step when they arrested the parents of a 210-pound, 11-year-old boy on suspicion of neglect and child cruelty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The parents of an 11-year-old boy were arrested in Britain on suspicion of neglect and child cruelty after authorities grew alarmed about the child’s weight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Britain, Child’s Weight Leads to Parents’ Arrest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Health Service', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T20:12:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kimiko de Freytas-Tamura', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kimiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Freytas-Tamura', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbad1eed-dc53-520f-8683-6be5c64c708c', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbad1eed-dc53-520f-8683-6be5c64c708c'}, {'abstract': 'Should egalitarian liberals favor child tax credits for the upper class?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/06/inequality-fertility-and-the-rich/', 'snippet': 'Should egalitarian liberals favor child tax credits for the upper class?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In my post earlier today on the distributional impact of a new child tax credit, I noted that the benefits to richer families under a plan like Senator Mike Lee’s would diminish if, in pursuit of revenue neutrality, the plan phased out its expanded tax credit at higher income levels. (The current child tax credit has a phase-out that begins at $110,000 for married joint filings.) But as a coda, it’s worth noting that to the extent that family-friendly policy actually boosts fertility\xa0— a highly uncertain question, to be sure\xa0— egalitarian liberals might want to think twice about\xa0a child tax credit phase-out, because then they wouldn’t reap the potentially egalitarian benefits of higher fertility in the upper class.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inequality, Fertility and the Rich', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Piketty, Thomas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T20:14:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9fe8203-ee33-5430-9edc-e2a068a805e5', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9fe8203-ee33-5430-9edc-e2a068a805e5'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Strauss, born in Munich 150 years ago, gained fame with scandalous works before taking his experimentalism into the mainstream.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/arts/music/a-look-at-richard-strausss-legacy-after-150-years.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Strauss, born in Munich 150 years ago, gained fame with scandalous works before taking his experimentalism into the mainstream.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Richard Strauss, born in Munich 150 years ago this Wednesday, must have laughed over the condemnations, even from many critics, of his opera “Salome” after its 1905 premiere in Dresden. Before long, of course, this succès de scandale was playing to eager audiences across Europe. The royalties Strauss earned paid for his bucolic villa in Garmisch. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08STRAUSS1/08STRAUSS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08STRAUSS1/08STRAUSS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08STRAUSS1/08STRAUSS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08STRAUSS1/08STRAUSS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1372, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08STRAUSS1/08STRAUSS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08STRAUSS1/08STRAUSS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08STRAUSS1/08STRAUSS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drifting Back to the Real World', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drifting Back to the Real World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tommasini, Anthony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss, Richard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T20:17:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fa33a83-09ab-55a4-b8af-dc045873a133', 'word_count': 1496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fa33a83-09ab-55a4-b8af-dc045873a133'}, {'abstract': 'A rigorous composer, clarinetist and tenor saxophonist, Jimmy Giuffre broke from the jazz mainstream in the 1960s, but by today’s standards, his music sounds modern.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/arts/music/jimmy-giuffres-music-finds-new-appreciation.html', 'snippet': 'A rigorous composer, clarinetist and tenor saxophonist, Jimmy Giuffre broke from the jazz mainstream in the 1960s, but by today’s standards, his music sounds modern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s anyone’s guess what Jimmy Giuffre was thinking when he improvised the stark, intriguing solo clarinet pieces intended for his 1962 Columbia album, “Free Fall.” Along with the five that made the cut, there were five others that saw the light of day some 35 years later, as bonus tracks on an overdue reissue. Small gems of oblique investigation, they bear titles that seem to hint at Giuffre’s state of mind; among them is one with a lonesome air, played in shadowy subtones, that he called “Time Will Tell.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08GIUFFRE/08GIUFFRE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08GIUFFRE/08GIUFFRE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08GIUFFRE/08GIUFFRE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08GIUFFRE/08GIUFFRE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08GIUFFRE/08GIUFFRE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08GIUFFRE/08GIUFFRE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/08/arts/08GIUFFRE/08GIUFFRE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Booed in the ’60s, but Time Will Tell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Booed in the ’60s, but Time Will Tell', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'perso