 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T21:54:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c90a74da-7959-5176-baf4-e8846a942c9a', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c90a74da-7959-5176-baf4-e8846a942c9a'}, {'abstract': 'The short-lived CBS comedy “The Class,” which was recently released on DVD, is worth remembering for a cast filled with actors who have gone on to populate a bumper crop of hits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/08/arts/television/cast-of-the-class-survived-its-cancellation.html', 'snippet': 'The short-lived CBS comedy “The Class,” which was recently released on DVD, is worth remembering for a cast filled with actors who have gone on to populate a bumper crop of hits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a sitcom about Philadelphia-area third graders who reunited in their late 20s, and it was spun from the fertile minds of David Crane, a creator of “Friends,” and Jeffrey Klarik, a writer-producer of “Mad About You.” In the fall of 2006, “The Class” was the new CBS show with the season’s highest hopes. It was not to be dismissed. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graduating to Hit Shows', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Graduating to Hit Shows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Class (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T21:56:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original':