 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/blogs/20140606POD-slide-QVWE/20140606POD-slide-QVWE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/06/blogs/20140606POD-slide-QVWE/20140606POD-slide-QVWE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/blogs/20140606POD-slide-QVWE/20140606POD-slide-QVWE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: France and Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central African Republic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T22:17:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/054a19a1-0f0d-50cc-8b63-f2de9c850d7b', 'word_count': 14, 'uri': 'nyt://article/054a19a1-0f0d-50cc-8b63-f2de9c850d7b'}, {'abstract': 'A federal appeals court issued a potentially far-reaching decision Friday that allows the victims of Nazi-related looting to press ahead with efforts to reclaim stolen artwork.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/07/arts/design/nazi-art-looting-case-may-proceed-court-rules.html', 'snippet': 'A federal appeals court issued a potentially far-reaching decision Friday that allows the victims of Nazi-related looting to press ahead with efforts to reclaim stolen artwork.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal appeals court in California issued a potentially far-reaching decision\xa0Friday\xa0that allows the victims of Nazi-related looting and their heirs to press ahead with legal efforts to reclaim stolen artwork. \xa0The case involves two large portrayals of Adam and Eve by the 14th-century master Lucas Cranach the Elder that were taken from the prominent Jewish Dutch dealer Jacques Goudstikker in a forced sale and are now in the collection of the Norton Simon Museum in California. Goudstikker’s daughter-in-law, Marei Von Saher, has been trying to reclaim the dyptich for years but has repeatedly run into legal stumbling blocks. \xa0In a 2-to-1 decision, though, the United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit reversed a lower court’s decision and permitted Ms. Von Saher’s lawsuit to proceed.\xa0 The diptych was recovered and returned to the Netherlands after World War II, when the Dutch negotiated a partial settlement with Goudstikker’s widow, which did not cover the work.\xa0The Norton Simon bought the paintings in 1971 from a collector. In 2012, a district judge ruled against Ms. Von Saher, saying that the United States had fulfilled its obligations to restitute the art, and that any effort to alter the Dutch resolution of the case would interfere with the federal government’s sole authority over foreign policy. But the appeals panel noted that the United States had signed two international agreements — one in Washington in 1998, and another in Terezin, Czech Republic, in 2009 — intended to remove obstacles to the recovery of possessions stolen by the Nazis. \xa0The Norton Simon released a statement saying it “remains confident that it holds complete and proper title” to the paintings and that it would “continue to pursue, consistent with its fiduciary duties, all appropriate legal options.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nazi Art-Looting Case May Proceed, Court Rules', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nazi Art-Looting Case May Proceed, Court Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arts and Antiquities Looting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goudstikker, Jacques', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-06T22:18:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/27948c8c-76ce-5e0d-b292-6897a66eeda5', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27948c8c-76ce-5e0d-b292-6897a66eeda5'}, {'abstract': 'Recommended reads from New York Times reporters and editors, highlighting great stories from around the web.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/06/06/what-were-reading-9/', 'snippet': 'Recommended reads from New York Times reporters and editors, highlighting great stories from around the web.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recommended reads from New York Times reporters and editors, highlighting great stories from around the web. What We’re Reading is an exclusive benefit for home delivery and digital subscribers that is sent twice a week as an email. Sign up »', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/blogs/cappa/cappa-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 218, 'width': 300, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank