': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/f4cffff1-f8b1-5de8-85b2-c8486647d608', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/f4cffff1-f8b1-5de8-85b2-c8486647d608'}, {'abstract': 'There is no shortage of places to watch soccer games in the city, especially during the World Cup. Tell us your favorite hangout.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/06/where-should-you-watch-the-world-cup-in-new-york-city/', 'snippet': 'There is no shortage of places to watch soccer games in the city, especially during the World Cup. Tell us your favorite hangout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Outside Brazil, there is probably no better place to watch the World Cup than the world’s city — New York. The usual spots, like The Football Factory, in Manhattan, and Woodwork, in Brooklyn, will be showing the games, of course, but every four years, the list of places to watch grows longer. Tapas bars are transformed into cheering sections for La Roja. Italian spaghetti joints become filled with passionate Azzurri fans. And bars typically crowded with American football loyalists draw fans of that other brand of football.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/nyregion/06worldcupcityroomweb/06worldcupcityroomweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/06/nyregion/06worldcupcityroomweb/06worldcupcityroomweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/06/nyregion/06worldcupcityroomweb/06worldcupcityroomweb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type