'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fff79f3e-da99-56d4-aab7-4ec054100e14', 'word_count': 1175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fff79f3e-da99-56d4-aab7-4ec054100e14'}, {'abstract': 'General Motors is recalling vehicles because a warning chime is inoperative and because air bags may not deploy in a crash.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/07/business/general-motors-recalls-105500-vehicles.html', 'snippet': 'General Motors is recalling vehicles because a warning chime is inoperative and because air bags may not deploy in a crash.', 'lead_paragraph': 'General Motors is recalling about 105,500 vehicles in two actions — one because a warning chime is inoperative and the other because air bags may not deploy in a crash, the automaker said. So far this year, the automaker has issued 34 recalls covering nearly 15.9 million vehicles across North America, including 13.9 million in the United States. The largest recall announced on Friday involves the warning-chime issue and includes about 69,500 of the automaker’s newest pickup trucks and sport utility vehicles, including 57,500 in the United States. The models affected by the recall are the 2014 Chevrolet Silverado and GMC Sierra light-duty pickups, the 2015 Silverado and Sierra heavy-duty pickups and the 2015 Chevrolet Tahoe, Suburban and GMC Yukon and Yukon XL S.U.V.s. The air bag problem affects 36,000 vehicles in North America, including 31,500 in the United States. The models covered by the recall are the 2012 Buick Verano and the Chevrolet Camaro, Cruze and Sonic. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'General Motors Recalls 105,500 Vehicles', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'General Motors Recalls 105,500 Vehicles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recalls and Bans of Products', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jensen, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-07T01:18:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen and Danielle Ivory', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ivory', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f069a11e-3007-587d-b683-a893c07bb9ae', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f069a11e-3007-587d-b683-a893c07bb9ae'}, {'abstract': 'An antitrust trial begins Monday that could upend big-time college sports.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/07/opinion/nocera-day-of-reckoning-for-ncaa.html', 'snippet': 'An antitrust trial begins Monday that could upend big-time college sports.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I began writing about the N.C.A.A. two and a half years ago, more or less by accident. Assigned by The New York Times Magazine to imagine a scheme in which athletes in the revenue sports — football and men’s basketball — get paid, I was awakened for the first time to inequities in the world of big-time college sports. Of course I knew that the coaches were getting rich while the players were getting nothing; everybody knew that. But I didn’t think that much about it. Neither did most fans, I would venture to guess.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Day of Reckoning for N.C.A.A.', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Day of Reckoning for N.C.A.A.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Bannon, Ed", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Collegiate Athletic Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vaccaro, Sonny', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball (College)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-07T01:18:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/11fdc6b7-ca40-5b1a-99ab-daf3914b932d', 'word_count': 814, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11fdc6b7-ca40-5b1a-99ab-daf3914b932d'}, {'abstract': 'Lawmakers on the local level who support a higher minimum wage show Congress where it needs to go.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/07/opinion/seattle-leads-the-way.html', 'snippet': 'Lawmakers on the local level who support a higher minimum wage show Congress where it needs to go.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new $15-an-hour minimum wage approved this week in Seattle does more than guarantee a raise to tens of thousands of workers in the city. As the highest minimum in the nation, it changes the terms of the minimum-wage debate and expands the realm of the possible in setting new minimums. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seattle Leads the Way', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seattle Leads the Way', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seattle (Wash)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-07T01:24:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4812be74-8774-5e23-b3cf-eff31390047d', '