': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecf1f37d-565b-52df-83db-ef2e59e9c415', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecf1f37d-565b-52df-83db-ef2e59e9c415'}, {'abstract': 'In Herman Koch’s “Summer House With Swimming Pool,” there is lechery, jealousy and filth, and they all come to play during a vacation on the Mediterranean coast.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/09/books/herman-koch-returns-with-summer-house-with-swimming-pool.html', 'snippet': 'In Herman Koch’s “Summer House With Swimming Pool,” there is lechery, jealousy and filth, and they all come to play during a vacation on the Mediterranean coast.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reading “The Dinner,” by the Dutch author and misanthrope Herman Koch, was rather like being stabbed in the eyeball with a hot needle. The book was such a huge international best seller that Mr. Koch is demonstrably not alone in his fascination with all things loathsome about humanity, and “The Dinner” did tell a propulsive tale. It is a hard act to follow, but say this about his new novel, “Summer House With Swimming Pool”: This is a book in which someone actually does stick a hot needle in his own eyeball. The blood, pus and pain that follow are lovingly described.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/09/arts/book/book-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/09/arts/book/book-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 700}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/09/arts/book/book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 878, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/09/arts/book/book-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1996, 'width': 1711, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/09/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/09/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/09/arts/book/book-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Good With the Scalpel, but Lacks People Skills', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Good With the Scalpel, but Lacks People Skills', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, Herman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-08T22:02:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bda0c94-34c6-5be8-ae19-dbe034549530', 'word_count': 898, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bda0c94-34c6-5be8-ae19-dbe034549530'}, {'abstract': 'As the host ensemble for the annual conference of the League of American Orchestras, Ludovic Morlot led the Seattle Symphony in wildly contrasting programs at the Benaroya Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/0