ion': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d9d30cd-3f28-5563-b787-d68ee07d073e', 'word_count': 190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d9d30cd-3f28-5563-b787-d68ee07d073e'}, {'abstract': 'A deadly assault on an airport in Karachi showed how, despite the Pakistani Taliban’s challenges and deepening divisions, their reach has extended into Pakistan’s biggest city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/10/world/asia/karachi-pakistan-airport-attack-taliban.html', 'snippet': 'A deadly assault on an airport in Karachi showed how, despite the Pakistani Taliban’s challenges and deepening divisions, their reach has extended into Pakistan’s biggest city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Only a week ago, the Pakistani Taliban appeared to be on the ropes. Violent rivalries had split the insurgency in two. Peace talks with the government had collapsed. Military jets had pounded militant hide-outs in the tribal belt.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Assault on Pakistan Airport Signals Taliban’s Reach and Resilience', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Assault Signals Taliban’s Reach and Resilience', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Karachi (Pakistan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tehrik-e-Taliban', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jinnah International Airport (Karachi, Pakistan)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic Movement of Uzbekistan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-09T10:29:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Declan Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Declan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26a17bd1-2548-559e-9dbd-a89defe89813', 'word_count': 1303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26a17bd1-2548-559e-9dbd-a89defe89813'}, {'abstract': 'A mountain neighborhood of the Afghan capital is a microcosm of a country divided, by ethnicity and status.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/10/opinion/kabuls-city-on-the-hill.html', 'snippet': 'A mountain neighborhood of the Afghan capital is a microcosm of a country divided, by ethnicity and status.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Just south of the city center is the most famous of Kabul’s many dun-colored mountains. It is called TV Hill, after the telecom and broadcast antennas that crowd its peak like needles in a pincushion. One of the most visible signs of the Western presence in Afghanistan, it is hailed as a symbol of post-Taliban progress.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/10/opinion/10jeung/10jeung-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/10/opinion/10jeung/10jeung-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/10/opinion/10jeung/10jeung-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'captio