ss', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Tierney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tierney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71c83602-c58a-5fe1-a753-73f4a29ad293', 'word_count': 983, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71c83602-c58a-5fe1-a753-73f4a29ad293'}, {'abstract': 'Writing can be an artificial arena where we mash the world into a shape we can stand to look at.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/09/controlling-the-narrative/', 'snippet': 'Writing can be an artificial arena where we mash the world into a shape we can stand to look at.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I used to be a polemicist. I was an editorial cartoonist, and wrote what I called “artist’s statements” to accompany my cartoons each week, which over the two terms of the George W. Bush administration lengthened and sharpened from rants into something more like essays. I became practiced at using language as a weapon. My role models were hilarious, elegant and brutal humorists from Mark Twain and H.L. Mencken through Hunter S. Thompson and Matt Taibbi, who raised American invective to an art form. This is a fine and honorable tradition when practiced with a certain amour-propre and panache, but in the last couple of decades it’s become our dominant mode of public discourse, degraded by hacks and amateurs who ape its cruelty but are rhetorically illiterate and tone-deaf to humor. They’re just parroting talking points with profanity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draft/opinionator-pog-draft-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draft/opinionat