olitics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-10T19:07:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicola Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicola', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5167588-69db-5cec-a1d5-6ae8d3de9b1f', 'word_count': 564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5167588-69db-5cec-a1d5-6ae8d3de9b1f'}, {'abstract': 'Two Southern states haven’t posted official primary vote totals yet, because the elections are run by the parties, not the state.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/11/upshot/alabama-and-mississippi-voted-last-week-no-official-results-yet.html', 'snippet': 'Two Southern states haven’t posted official primary vote totals yet, because the elections are run by the parties, not the state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A week after primary elections in eight states, two of them — Alabama and Mississippi — have not posted any results on an official state government website. The two stand alone among states that held primaries on June 3 in not publishing any results in a central location. California, Iowa, Montana, South Dakota, New Mexico and New Jersey had unofficial election-night results sites, in many cases updated live on election night.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/11/upshot/11up-results/11up-results-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/11/upshot/11up-results/11up-results-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i