5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-10T21:44:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3ab86b0-0d2c-5f31-887c-ef0b0faa1ccc', 'word_count': 1333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3ab86b0-0d2c-5f31-887c-ef0b0faa1ccc'}, {'abstract': 'The great moments in Frederick Ashton’s “Cinderella,” which joined American Ballet Theater’s repertory on Monday, were the ensembles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/11/arts/dance/frederick-ashtons-cinderella-at-american-ballet-theater.html', 'snippet': 'The great moments in Frederick Ashton’s “Cinderella,” which joined American Ballet Theater’s repertory on Monday, were the ensembles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Frederick Ashton’s three-act “Cinderella” (1948) is a story of hope fulfilled, a comedy of contrasting characters and a classical ballet dense with sensuous movement. Though New York has been having a Cinderellathon in 2014 — the Rodgers and Hammerstein musical on Broadway, and Rossini’s “La Cenerentola” at the Metropolitan Opera and Massenet’s “Cendrillon” at Juilliard Opera, both in April — this treatment of the old fairy tale takes the prize for dramatic poetry. Still, it looked a pale echo of itself on Monday when it joined American Ballet Theater’s repertory. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/11/arts/CINDERELLA/CINDERELLA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/11/arts/CINDERELLA/CINDERELLA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight