ges/2014/06/11/multimedia/eu-tax-investigations/eu-tax-investigations-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 651, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/11/multimedia/eu-tax-investigations/eu-tax-investigations-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1301, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/11/multimedia/eu-tax-investigations/eu-tax-investigations-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/11/multimedia/eu-tax-investigations/eu-tax-investigations-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/11/multimedia/eu-tax-investigations/eu-tax-investigations-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'European Union Investigates Tax Rulings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Almunia, Joaquin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Luxembourg', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ireland', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Netherlands', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Starbucks Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiat Finance and Trade Ltd SA', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Shelters', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-11T16:13:43+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/26b9dbef-99ae-521f-af6c-83b3f960b628', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/26b9dbef-99ae-521f-af6c-83b3f960b628'}, {'abstract': 'On the possible implications of a shocking primary defeat.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/11/after-eric-cantor/', 'snippet': 'On the possible implications of a shocking primary defeat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the government shutdown last year, I wrote a series of posts trying to explain why anyone interested in a healthy conservatism, a viable and serious Republican Party, should be somewhat ambivalent about the fight between the party’s insurgents and its congressional leadership.\xa0The insurgents pushed for the shutdown and the establishment resisted it, which meant that on the major question of the moment that establishment was right and the insurgents were not only wrong but utterly disconnected from reality. But in the bigger picture, the insurgents’ critique of the establishment’s lazy, K Street-oriented, “repeal the Medical device tax!” approach to policymaking was quite frequently correct, and it was noteworthy that some of the most prominent figures supporting the shutdown (Mike Lee, most notably, but also Marco Rubio and Rand Paul) were also sources of much of the party’s post-2012 policy innovation.\xa0So the post-shutdown scenario favored by many centrist observers in the media, in which John Boehner and Mitch McConnell just found a way to crush the grassroots and bring their base to heel, was not really a long-term solution to the G.O.P.’s lack of vision and seriousness. Instead —\xa0somehow, somewhere —\xa0the party would need to find leadership that could\xa0channel\xa0grassroots populism into a constructive agenda, without being captured by that populism’s follies and excesses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Eric Cantor', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brat, David A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cantor, Eric', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaus, Mickey', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klein, Ezra', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Rand', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubio, Marco', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-11T16:20:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34430b8d-bb59-5094-a578-47e0ef399bf5', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34430b8d-bb59-5094-a578-47e0ef399bf5'}, {'abstract': 'An indictment describes a criminal ring whose members drugged wealthy men and took them to establishments where thousands of dollars were charged to their credit cards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/12/nyregion/5-charged-with-robbing-men-in-strip-club-scheme.html', 'snippet': 'An indictment describes a criminal ring whose members drugged wealthy men and took them to establishments where thousands of dollars were charged to their credit cards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seemed, at the time, to be an unlikely story.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/12/nyregion/SCORESweb2/SCORESweb2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/12/nyregion/SCORESweb2/SCORESweb2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/12/nyregion/SCORESweb2/SCORESweb2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/12/nyregion/SCORESweb2/SCORESweb2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/12/nyregion/SCORESweb2/SCORESweb2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/12/nyregion/SCORESweb2/SCORESweb2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/12/nyregion/SCORESweb2/SCORESweb2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '5 Accused of Luring Men to Strip Clubs for a Night They Wouldn’t Remember', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '5 Accused of Plot to Lure Men to Strip Clubs for a Night They Wouldn’t Remember', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strip Clubs and Strippers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flushing (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-11T16:23:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d8468d8-c426-5d1c-b38f-4a2bac5ad9ce', 'word_count': 863, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d8468d8-c426-5d1c-b38f-4a2bac5ad9ce'}, {'abstract': 'The Republican outsider who unseated Eric Cantor did not take a position on the minimum wage Wednesday. Then again, most economists can’t find a consensus on the issue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/12/upshot/an-honest-answer-from-david-brat.html', 'snippet': 'The Republican outsider who unseated Eric Cantor did not take a position on the minimum wage Wednesday. Then again, most economists can’t find a consensus on the issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When an MSNBC interviewer asked David Brat, the economics professor at Randolph-Macon College who toppled Eric Cantor in a primary challenge Tuesday, whether he opposed the minimum wage, he responded on Wednesday, “Um, I don’t have a well-crafted response on that one.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Honest Answer From David Brat', 'kicker': 'Everyday Economics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brat, David A', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-11T16:25:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Wolfers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolfers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f93d9a81-7381-5d0e-b3dc-87b39e4ab353', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f93d9a81-7381-5d0e-b3dc-87b39e4ab353'}, {'abstract': '“God acted through people on my behalf,” David Brat told Fox News after his primary upset.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/11/so-who-exactly-is-david-brat-the-guy-who-beat-eric-cantor/', 'snippet': '“God acted through people on my behalf,” David Brat told Fox News after his primary upset.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m not the only one asking that question this morning after David Brat, an economics professor from Randolph-Macon College, defeated the House Majority Leader, Eric Cantor, in the primary in the 7th District of Virginia — with a tiny campaign budget and an unlimited willingness to represent Mr. Cantor’s record as too moderate for the Republican Party.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'So, Who, Exactly, Is David Brat, the Guy Who Beat Eric Cantor?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brat, David A', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cantor, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-11T16:31:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17b13619-058f-5d68-a091-29fd73fb114a', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17b13619-058f-5d68-a091-29fd73fb114a'}, {'abstract': 'Machiya, traditional Japanese townhouses, are resurfacing as places for travelers to shop and sleep.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/travel/in-kyoto-tradition-meets-today.html', 'snippet': 'Machiya, traditional Japanese townhouses, are resurfacing as places for travelers to shop and sleep.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Accommodations rarely get more atmospheric than this: a 100-plus-year-old Kyoto townhouse that once housed maiko, or apprentice geisha, now thoroughly renovated to 21st-century standards trimmed in 1,000-year-old temple beams, antique art and tatami mats. On the banks of the Kamogawa River, the two-bedroom named  Kamogawa-tei (Kiyamachi Street; 81-75-354-7770) is one of six traditional townhouses known as machiya renovated by Aoi Kyoto Stay.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/travel/15HEADS2/15HEADS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/travel/15HEADS2/15HEADS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'