title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/3203023e-6869-566a-8b5a-f65f800c26b6', 'word_count': 800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3203023e-6869-566a-8b5a-f65f800c26b6'}, {'abstract': 'There are smart new ways to lock your front door, but are they really better than that old lump of brass?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/12/garden/losing-the-key.html', 'snippet': 'There are smart new ways to lock your front door, but are they really better than that old lump of brass?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this age of rapid transformation, the house key has been surprisingly resistant to change. Cars have mostly switched to key fobs. Hotels and office buildings favor the pass card. And yet the little metal keys we carry around — part security device, part domestic totem — aren’t that different from the ones carried by our parents, their parents or their parents, going back to the Civil War, when Linus Yale Jr. invented the cylinder lock, modifying an ancient Egyptian design.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/12/garden/12SUBKEY/12SUBKEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 970, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/12/garden/12SUBKEY/12SUBKEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 970}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/12/garden/12SUBKEY/12SUBKEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 634, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/12/garden/12SUBKEY/12SUBKEY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1268, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo',