: 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d309c5bb-9158-5f1e-bc73-808029ba37e0', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d309c5bb-9158-5f1e-bc73-808029ba37e0'}, {'abstract': 'The United Nations General Assembly chose Sam Kutesa, Uganda’s foreign minister, as its next president, bypassing concerns of rights advocates and Western officials over Uganda’s law criminalizing homosexuality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/12/world/africa/ugandan-official-to-be-next-general-assembly-chief.html', 'snippet': 'The United Nations General Assembly chose Sam Kutesa, Uganda’s foreign minister, as its next president, bypassing concerns of rights advocates and Western officials over Uganda’s law criminalizing homosexuality.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The United Nations General Assembly chose Sam Kutesa, Uganda’s foreign minister, as its next president on Wednesday, bypassing concerns of rights advocates and Western officials over Uganda’s  law criminalizing homosexuality, which took effect four months ago. Mr. Kutesa was selected by acclamation, without a vote. The one-year post is largely ceremonial but carries significance because the president leads General Assembly meetings. Mr. Kutesa said nothing about gay rights in his acceptance speech but vowed to promote gender equality and women’s rights. Asked by reporters afterward about his views on gays, he said, “As long as they respect privacy, I have no problem with it at all.” Philippe Bolopion, the United Nations director at Human Rights Watch, said the Uganda law “makes him a questionable choice.” Ban Ki-moon, the secretary general, supported Mr. Kutesa’s presidency but has expressed concern about the law, urging its repeal.  ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ugandan Official to Be Next General Assembly Chief', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ugandan Official to Be Next General Assembly Chief', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Uganda', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kutesa, Sam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kutesa, Sam', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-12T03:09:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1037eaab-9b03-583c-93ad-33dac3d73825', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1037eaab-9b03-583c-93ad-33dac3d73825'}, {'abstract': 'An antiterrorism court in Punjab Province on Wednesday sentenced two brothers accused of cannibalism each to 11 years and four months in prison.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/12/world/asia/pakistan-brothers-sentenced-again-for-cannibalism.html', 'snippet': 'An antiterrorism court in Punjab Province on Wednesday sentenced two brothers accused of cannibalism each to 11 years and four months in prison.', 'lead_paragraph': ' An antiterrorism court in Punjab Province on Wednesday sentenced two brothers accused of cannibalism each to 11 years and four months in prison. The brothers, Muhammad Arif and Farman Ali, were arrested in April after the police found a baby’s head at their home in remote Bhakkar. They were convicted after a similar discovery in 2011 but were released after 18 months because, lawyers said, Pakistani law has no specific provisions against cannibalism. This time they were convicted of terrorism, desecrating a corpse and concealing evidence, said Mian Muhammad Rizwan, the public prosecutor in the case. Mr. Arif said that his older brother had disinterred a baby’s body and that the two had cooked and eaten the remains. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan: Brothers Sentenced Again for Cannibalism', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ASIA Pakistan: Brothers Sentenced Again for Cannibalism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannibalism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Punjab (Pakistan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-12T03:09:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Waqar Gillani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Waqar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c875a1e-b844-58f4-a15e-7591996ff693', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c875a1e-b844-58f4-a15e-7591996ff693'}, {'abstract': 'The workers at Rio de Janeiro’s airports said they would go on a 24-hour strike on Thursday, the opening day of the World Cup soccer tournament.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/12/world/americas/brazil-with-world-cup-near-airport-staff-plans-strike.html', 'snippet': 'The workers at Rio de Janeiro’s airports said they would go on a 24-hour strike on Thursday, the opening day of the World Cup soccer tournament.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The workers at Rio de Janeiro’s airports said they would go on a 24-hour strike on Thursday, the opening day of the World Cup soccer tournament. Other strikes have recently affected Brazilian cities as unions exert leverage at a time when the government is trying to avert major problems. The workers at Rio’s international airport and two domestic airports said they were seeking better conditions and a new collective bargaining agreement. It was not clear how the strike would affect the thousands of visitors flowing into Rio, which will host several games, including the World Cup final. Union officials said that as much as 80 percent of the airport work force would keep working during the strike, Brazilian news organizations reported. In São Paulo, subway workers voted not to resume their strike.\xa0 ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brazil: With World Cup Near, Airport Staff Plans Strike', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brazil: With World Cup Near, Airport Staff Plans Strike', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romero, Simon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2014 (Soccer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collective Bargaining', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-12T03:09:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Romero', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romero', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported',