 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Broadway League', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-12T16:58:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8ea8547-b1c0-5b55-9619-92a26adbb63f', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8ea8547-b1c0-5b55-9619-92a26adbb63f'}, {'abstract': 'In “22 Jump Street,” Jonah Hill and Channing Tatum reprise their role as police partners, this time going undercover on a college campus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/13/movies/22-jump-street-puts-cops-on-campus.html', 'snippet': 'In “22 Jump Street,” Jonah Hill and Channing Tatum reprise their role as police partners, this time going undercover on a college campus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More is more and is, at times, just right in “22 Jump Street,” an exploding piñata of gags, pratfalls, winking asides, throwaway one-liners and self-reflexive waggery. This is, of course, the sequel to “21 Jump Street,” the 2012 hit that borrowed its title and undercover brother shtick from the old television show best known for its hair-gel hottie, Johnny Depp. For a second time, Jonah Hill and Channing Tatum play lovable undercover cops who — in between dodging danger and jokes that fly almost as fast as they do on “The Simpsons” — insinuate themselves into a juvenile population, whose resistance to these charmers has been rendered completely futile, much like that of the movie’s audience.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/13/arts/13TWENTYTWO/13TWENTYTWO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'wi