me': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-12T19:29:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. C. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e7dc1d0-ded3-5b04-82f1-3fd4032bf661', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e7dc1d0-ded3-5b04-82f1-3fd4032bf661'}, {'abstract': 'The stylish sibling duo talk reggae influences, keeping it in the family and dressing like rock stars.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/12/q-and-a-wild-belle-spreads-its-island-vibes/', 'snippet': 'The stylish sibling duo talk reggae influences, keeping it in the family and dressing like rock stars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reggae-based pop music has unlikely torchbearers in Elliot and Natalie Bergman, a.k.a. Wild Belle. The brother-sister duo grew up around Chicago, a city that’s more Buddy Guy than Bob Marley, and spent much of their childhood like modern-day Von Trapps, singing songs around the piano with their parents and two other siblings. Elliot, who plays keyboard and saxophone and, at 32, is seven years Natalie’s senior, would go on to study jazz and play in an Afrobeat band, Nomo, at the University of Michigan. And the siblings’ travels, both literal and sonic, helped expand their musical purview. The resulting mix of sounds — indie pop tinged with reggae and ’70s soul — can be heard throughout Wild Belle’s debut album, “Isles,” released last year. And though they insist the title is more about the album’s structure than its influences (“each song is its own island,” Natalie has said), it might just as easily be a nod to Chris Blackwell’s Island Records, whose catalogue of ska and reggae hits surely left an impression.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedi