ne, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T14:25:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d5c6097-3735-574e-928f-5bd119b35a2b', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d5c6097-3735-574e-928f-5bd119b35a2b'}, {'abstract': 'Charity, once removed; rogue editing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/magazine/highly-highly-recommended.html', 'snippet': 'Charity, once removed; rogue editing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As part of a typical hiring process, I asked a job applicant for a reference. It turned out that I already knew the reference, who was very supportive of the candidate. The man got the job. After I extended the offer, I heard through the grapevine that the applicant and the reference were romantically linked. The hire has performed very well, but I felt that they both should have been more forthright about their relationship. Should they have been? JEFF H., SAN DIEGO', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15ethicist/mag-15Ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 341, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15ethicist/mag-15Ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 341}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15ethicist/mag-15Ethicist-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 582, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15ethicist/mag-15Ethicist-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1164, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15ethicist/mag-15Ethicist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15ethicist/mag-15Ethicist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15ethicist/mag-15Ethicist-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Highly, Highly Recommended', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Highly, Highly Recommended', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T14:25:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/74af39f1-3c2f-5a8b-9a20-25c9a9f1ae34', 'word_count': 818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74af39f1-3c2f-5a8b-9a20-25c9a9f1ae34'}, {'abstract': 'Underwater photographs of infants learning to swim.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/magazine/before-learning-to-crawl-you-must-learn-to-swim.html', 'snippet': 'Underwater photographs of infants learning to swim.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rachael Ubiera, a 25-year-old in Kissimmee, Fla., considers herself “one of the relaxed moms.” But the first time she saw her 5-month-old daughter, Zoe, carefully dropped underwater in a pool for infant survival swim training, it was nerve-racking. “She looked surprised,” Ubiera says. “Like, ‘What are you doing to me?’ ” Zoe was being introduced to “self-rescue,” in which babies are taught to hold their breath underwater, kick their feet, turn over to float on their backs and rest until help arrives. The technique, photographed here by Seth Casteel, who is working on a related book that will come out next spring, was pioneered in the late ’60s by Harvey Barnett, who at 18 became determined to teach infants to swim after the drowning of a neighbor’s child. His methods have spread around the world — taught in clinics and imitated widely. “It gives the baby the best possible chance,” he says. Julie Bosman', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Before Learning to Crawl, You Must Learn to Swim', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Little Nemos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drownings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T14:25:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Photographs by Seth Casteel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Photographs', 'middlename': 'Seth', 'lastname': 'Casteel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f8adad2-d30b-53ca-8d97-88429a5487cb', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f8adad2-d30b-53ca-8d97-88429a5487cb'}, {'abstract': 'There were a few things about me that my girlfriend liked. And plenty she didn’t.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/magazine/the-breakup-list.html', 'snippet': 'There were a few things about me that my girlfriend liked. And plenty she didn’t.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was summertime in Brooklyn, so hot that the trees were shedding leaves and the Realtors were sweating through their starched shirts and stopping on street corners to wipe down their Bluetooth earpieces. My girlfriend — we’d been together, more or less, since our first date in February — was staying with me for a couple of weeks while she sublet her apartment. She was between trips abroad. She was always traveling for work. I’d set her up at my kitchen table with the wireless password. I got her a partner membership at my gym. We cranked up the air-conditioner in the bedroom at night and lay across the comforter to let our sweat dry. In the mornings, the tired air-conditioner wheezed out a spray of mist.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15lives/mag-15Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15lives/mag-15Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/magazine/15lives/mag-15Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType'