e something important but long forgotten.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dragon Seed', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dragon Seed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hahn, Rebecca', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:23:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer A. Nielsen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Nielsen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d1c7218-8ab6-5719-9ca1-5f5ec35f8532', 'word_count': 722, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d1c7218-8ab6-5719-9ca1-5f5ec35f8532'}, {'abstract': 'In a comfortingly familiar vacation community, a girl is now mature enough to understand what’s been kept from her before.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/books/review/this-one-summer-by-mariko-tamaki-and-jillian-tamaki.html', 'snippet': 'In a comfortingly familiar vacation community, a girl is now mature enough to understand what’s been kept from her before.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first frames of “This One Summer,” a graphic novel written by Mariko Tamaki and illustrated by her cousin Jillian Tamaki, a father carries his sleeping daughter down a driveway to a cottage in the dark. This is Awago Beach, where Rose Wallace’s family has been coming “since . . . like . . . forever.” Or, more precisely, since Rose, who is now either an old tween or a young teenager (we don’t learn her exact age), was 5.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/15burton/15burton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 834, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/books/15burton/15burton-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 834}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/15burton/15burton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/15burton/15burton-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1473, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/15burton/15burton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/books/15burton/15burton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/15burton/15burton-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drawn to the Shore', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drawn to the Shore', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tamaki, Mariko', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tamaki, Jillian', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:23:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Burton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/24365f69-94fd-52ee-b482-7e4959aa85d2', 'word_count': 783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24365f69-94fd-52ee-b482-7e4959aa85d2'}, {'abstract': 'A teenage girl searches for her kidnapped uncle in a world where time and geography have been shuffled.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/books/review/the-glass-sentence-by-s-e-grove.html', 'snippet': 'A teenage girl searches for her kidnapped uncle in a world where time and geography have been shuffled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The publishers of S. E. Grove’s novel, an ambitious and quixotic high fantasy called “The Glass Sentence,” smartly slapped upon the dust jacket an assessment by Megan Whalen Turner: “Not since Philip Pullman’s ‘The Golden Compass’ have I seen such an original and compelling world built inside a book.” A sensible precaution; it’s easy to suppose that “The Glass Sentence” was written in reaction to Pullman’s magisterial “His Dark Materials.” I am hardly one to lodge a complaint about that. Great works create their own gravitational field; Pullman wrote under the influence of Milton. The question \xadremains: Does this admirer pull off something worthwhile of her own?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Global Repositioning', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Global Repositioning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grove, S E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:24:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Maguire', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maguire', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5c2fa22-6724-5266-870c-d0e0bafc231c', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5c2fa22-6724-5266-870c-d0e0bafc231c'}, {'abstract': 'Neil Gaiman, whose “Ocean at the End of the Lane” is No. 8 on the paperback fiction list, wrote his first published book — “solely for the money,” he says — about Duran Duran.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Neil Gaiman, whose “Ocean at the End of the Lane” is No. 8 on the paperback fiction list, wrote his first published book — “solely for the money,” he says — about Duran Duran.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Notorious: After spending two and a half months on the hardcover fiction list last year, Neil Gaiman’s fantasy novel “The Ocean at the End of the Lane” slips onto the trade paperback list this week at No. 8. The paperback follows on the heels of another book that should appeal to Gaiman’s fans — Hayley Campbell’s winningly scrapbookish tribute, “The Art of Neil Gaiman,” which Audrey Niffenegger describes in the foreword as “a sort of midcareer retrospective.” The book recounts Gaiman’s early days as a hungry arts journalist and his rise to cult success as a writer of comic books, notably the “Sandman” series, before he turned his hand to novels. It also reminds readers that Neil himself (as he’s known on Twitter) once wrote a scrapbookish tribute of his own: His first published book — “written solely for the money,” he says — was “Duran Duran: The First Four Years of the Fab Five” (1984). “I got a phone call from a lady,” Gaiman explains in an interview cited in the book, “and she said would you like to write a rock ’n’ roll book for us? A rock ’n’ roll biography? And I thought, ‘Oh God, yes! I’m in!’ ” Gaiman suggested a handful of subjects, including the Velvet Underground, David Bowie and Elvis Costello, before the editor interrupted: “Do you want to write the Duran Duran book, the Barry Manilow book or the Def Leppard book?” Pragmatism led him to choose the relative newcomers Duran Duran. “With Barry Manilow,” he said, “I figured I was going to have to listen to, you know, 40 Barry Manilow albums.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22list-gaiman/22list-gaiman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22list-gaiman/22list-gaiman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22list-gaiman/22list-gaiman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22list-gaiman/22list-gaiman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22list-gaiman/22list-gaiman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:26:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39d80408-36df-5ed9-9b2b-bafee7874381', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39d80408-36df-5ed9-9b2b-bafee7874381'}, {'abstract': 'An account of office design and technology since the Civil War offers insights into the changing nature of work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/books/review/cubed-by-nikil-saval.html', 'snippet': 'An account of office design and technology since the Civil War offers insights into the changing nature of work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How you work depends in large part on the spaces in which you work. This big theme is taken up by Nikil Saval in “Cubed: A Secret History of the Workplace.” There is in fact nothing “secret” about this history; from the Civil War on, as the white-collar world grew, managers and designers thought out loud about where workers should sit, the furniture they should use, the walls and windows that should surround them. Instead of a secret history, Saval, an editor of n+1, has written something more interesting. He has infused a straightforward factual account with all sorts of literary, cultural and political insights; these make the story he tells more dark than dry.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SENNETT/0615-bks-SENNETT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 555, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SENNETT/0615-bks-SENNETT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 555}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SENNETT/0615-bks-SENNETT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 947, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SENNETT/0615-bks-SENNETT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1202, 'width': 1300, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SENNETT/0615-bks-SENNETT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SENNETT/0615-bks-SENNETT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SENNETT/0615-bks-SENNETT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Office Max', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Office Max', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saval, Nikil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:27:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sennett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sennett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/94c1070f-dad3-5a20-ad21-a80491f38878', 'word_count': 1202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94c1070f-dad3-5a20-ad21-a80491f38878'}, {'abstract': 'A novel traces a solitary farmer’s attempt to escape her past on a remote British island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/books/review/all-the-birds-singing-by-evie-wyld.html', 'snippet': 'A novel traces a solitary farmer’s attempt to escape her past on a remote British island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Evie Wyld’s second novel opens with its narrator, a woman named Jake Whyte, discovering one of her sheep killed and gutted. Carrion birds are feasting: “Crows, their beaks shining, strutting and rasping, and when I waved my stick they flew to the trees and watched, flaring out their wings, singing, if you could call it that.” Jake shouts, “What are you laughing at?” and throws a rock at them. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15MELOY/15MELOY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 762, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15MELOY/15MELOY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 762}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15MELOY/15MELOY-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 807, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15MELOY/15MELOY-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1242, 'width': 978, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 