k of another 19th-century novelist — Bram Stoker’s “Dracula.” “For Cat,” we are told, “schooled only in contemporary vampire romance, it was a curious and unsettling experience. It reminded her of the first time she’d tasted an olive. It was unlike everything that had crossed her palate before; strange and not quite pleasant, yet gilded with the promise of sophistication.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15BAKER/0615-bks-Baker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15BAKER/0615-bks-Baker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 643}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15BAKER/0615-bks-Baker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 956, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15BAKER/0615-bks-Baker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1737, 'width': 1620, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15BAKER/0615-bks-Baker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15BAKER/0615-bks-Baker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15BAKER/0615-bks-Baker-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It Was a Dark and Stormy Night', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It Was a Dark and Stormy Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDermid, Val', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Jane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Northanger Abbey (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:27:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jo Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/402393c1-7980-58bf-a158-bfce0685877d', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/402393c1-7980-58bf-a158-bfce0685877d'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir centers on life in Concord, Mass., its cultural legacy and changing ways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/books/review/perfectly-miserable-by-sarah-payne-stuart.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir centers on life in Concord, Mass., its cultural legacy and changing ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Concord, Mass., is a sacred spot in the mythology of New England Protestants, once the Brahmins of American society, now fast becoming just another ethnic group. With this change comes new material for our venerable tradition of ethnic comedy. The territory is not altogether virgin, of course, but Sarah Payne Stuart comes to it with freshness and flair in this memoir.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15LESSARD/15LESSARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 447, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15LESSARD/15LESSARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 447}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15LESSARD/15LESSARD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 764, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15LESSARD/15LESSARD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1527, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15LESSARD/15LESSARD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15LESSARD/15LESSARD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15LESSARD/15LESSARD-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Puritans’ Progress', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Puritans’ Progress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lessard, Suzannah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stuart, Sarah Payne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Concord (Mass)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:28:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzannah Lessard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lessard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/486e4353-6edf-54ad-a1ba-1e26db737da1', 'word_count': 891, 'uri': 'nyt://article/486e4353-6edf-54ad-a1ba-1e26db737da1'}, {'abstract': 'The front-runner in Afghanistan’s presidential election, Mr. Abdullah has pushed ahead by excelling at the central skill of Afghan political leadership: making deals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/14/world/asia/abdullah-moves-closer-to-center-of-power-in-afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'The front-runner in Afghanistan’s presidential election, Mr. Abdullah has pushed ahead by excelling at the central skill of Afghan political leadership: making deals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — AFTER decades roaming the margins of power — as a close aide to the revered resistance leader Ahmed Shah Massoud, as a foreign minister and later as Afghanistan’s perennial opposition leader — Abdullah Abdullah may finally be arriving at the center of it all.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/world/ABDULLAH-1/ABDULLAH-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/world/ABDULLAH-1/ABDULLAH-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/world/ABDULLAH-1/ABDULLAH-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/world/ABDULLAH-1/ABDULLAH-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/world/ABDULLAH-1/ABDULLAH-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/world/ABDULLAH-1/ABDULLAH-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/world/ABDULLAH-1/ABDULLAH-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Abdullah Abdullah Moves Toward Center of Afghan Power', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Afghan Who Never Strayed Far Closes In on Center of Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ahmed, Azam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABDULLAH, ABDULLAH', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:29:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Biography', '_id': 'nyt://article/588d9b8a-591c-5142-805d-6629790f5b6f', 'word_count': 1390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/588d9b8a-591c-5142-805d-6629790f5b6f'}, {'abstract': 'The author of “Under the Tuscan Sun” reflects on her early life in a small Georgia town.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/books/review/under-magnolia-by-frances-mayes.html', 'snippet': 'The author of “Under the Tuscan Sun” reflects on her early life in a small Georgia town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Throughout Frances Mayes’s idylls about “the sweet life in Italy” — the subtitle of one of her best-selling memoirs — there are glimpses of a childhood, far less blissful, in the American South. In “Bella Tuscany,” she mentions her “tyrant” grandfather, Daddy Jack. In “Under the Tuscan Sun,” she touches on her “resigned disappointed ancestors” back home. Even as she enjoys a “pure surge of pleasure” while restoring Bramasole, a villa near Cortona, Italy, and discovering what it means to “surprise your own life,” she must keep the shadows cast by the town of Fitzgerald, Ga., at bay. “To put it mildly,” she remarks in passing of her father, Garbert, “he was a difficult man and to complicate that, he died at 47.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SUBHOFFMAN/15SUBHOFFMAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SUBHOFFMAN/15SUBHOFFMAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 682}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SUBHOFFMAN/15SUBHOFFMAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 901, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SUBHOFFMAN/15SUBHOFFMAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1082, 'width': 952, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SUBHOFFMAN/15SUBHOFFMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SUBHOFFMAN/15SUBHOFFMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15SUBHOFFMAN/15SUBHOFFMAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Southern Living', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Southern Living', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoffman, Roy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mayes, Frances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:29:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/307805dc-27ee-5a24-a393-c4094eb0c092', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/307805dc-27ee-5a24-a393-c4094eb0c092'}, {'abstract': 'Coming-of-age tales about girls and their fraught relationships.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/books/review/inside-madeleine-by-paula-bomer.html', 'snippet': 'Coming-of-age tales about girls and their fraught relationships.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paula Bomer writes the kind of women’s fiction that critics like to call “raw.” Her stark portrayals of biological womanhood have been described as raw and angry, raw and darkly funny, brutally raw and urgent. The word is meant literally, to a degree: Her earlier books, “Nine Months” and “Baby,” feature birth scenes that spare no anatomical detail. But “raw” has also come to mean something specific in blurb-speak about quasi-confessional writing by women, and it’s not always fitting or fair. Half praise, half dis, it commends the struggle to embrace female ugliness while finding the work unsatisfactory. “Raw” work is visceral but also hard to digest — a kind term, in other words, for something half-baked.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15TORTORICI/15TORTORICI-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15TORTORICI/15TORTORICI-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 812}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15TORTORICI/15TORTORICI-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 757, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15TORTORICI/15TORTORICI-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1894, 'width': 1399, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15TORTORICI/15TORTORICI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15TORTORICI/15TORTORICI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/books/review/15TORTORICI/15TORTORICI-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nothing to Hide', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nothing to Hide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bomer, Paula', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-13T15:29:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dayna Tortorici', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dayna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tortorici', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/503bd2cd-6d1a-5200-8f63-7d0dbbef6fde', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/503bd2cd-6d1a-5200-8f63-7d0dbbef6fde'}, {'abstract': 'A married immigrant Londoner in his 70s is ready to come out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/books/review/mr-loverman-by-bernardine-evaristo.html', 'snippet': 'A married immigrant Londoner in his 70s is ready to come out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2012, at the age of 82, the indomitable stage and screen actor Christopher Plummer won his first Academy Award for his energetic portrayal of Hal Fields, a 75-year-old who comes out of the closet shortly after his wife’s death, in Mike Mills’s structurally complex romantic comedy “Beginners.” In the film, Hal’s full-throttled, last-call embrace of life inspires his son, Oliver, to fully embrace his own, while asking what exactly it takes to start life anew, and when, if ever, it’s too late.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'ra