': 'GAINESVILLE, Fla. — WE live in the age of grace and the age of futility, the age of speed and the age of dullness. The way we live now is not poetic. We live prose, we breathe prose, and we drink, alas, prose. There is prose that does us no great harm, and that may even, in small doses, prove medicinal, the way snake oil cured everything by curing nothing. But to live continually in the natter of ill-written and ill-spoken prose is to become deaf to what language can do.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/sunday-review/0615POETRYsub/0615POETRYsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/sunday-review/0615POETRYsub/0615POETRYsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 697}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/sunday-review/0615POETRYsub/0615POETRYsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 882, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/sunday-review/0615POETRYsub/0615POETRYsub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1764, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/sunday-review/0615POETRYsub/0615POETRYsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/15/sunday-review/0615POETRYsub/0615POETRYsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/sunday-review/0615POETRYsub/0615POETRYsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry: Who Needs It?', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Poetry: Who Needs It?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-14T18:30:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Logan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Logan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/65962735-ee05-522f-978d-0629e2bc0d7f', 'word_count': 984, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65962735-ee05-522f-978d-0629e2bc0d7f'}, {'abstract': 'Representative Eric Cantor, the House majority leader, lost his primary race, setting off a political frenzy. Militants in Iraq have also retaken areas of the country that Americans once held. Both events will dominate the conversation on the Sunday shows.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/14/sunday-breakfast-menu-june-15/', 'snippet': 'Representative Eric Cantor, the House majority leader, lost his primary race, setting off a political frenzy. Militants in Iraq have also retaken areas of the country that Americans once held. Both events will dominate the conversation on the Sund...', 'lead_paragraph': 'A primary race in Virginia rocked the political world when Representative Eric Cantor, the House majority leader, lost to his Tea Party-backed opponent David Brat, an economics professor. By the following day, Mr. Cantor announced that he would resign his leadership position by July 31. Mr. Cantor will appear on both ABC’s “This Week and CNN’s “State of the Union,” to give his opinion on why he lost his congressional seat and what went wrong with his campaign.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday Breakfast Menu, June 15', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-14T18:30:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elena Schneider', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schneider', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/641bd1df-48c2-58a1-9a7b-cdaf7cc7544b', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/641bd1df-48c2-58a1-9a7b-cdaf7cc7544b'}, {'abstract': 'Detainees have been released from Guantánamo for years. Why were Republicans outraged only with the prisoner swap for Sgt. Bowe Bergdahl?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/opinion/sunday/bergdahl-critics-didnt-howl-when-bush-freed-prisoners.html', 'snippet': 'Detainees have been released from Guantánamo for years. Why were Republicans outraged only with the prisoner swap for Sgt. Bowe Bergdahl?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In early 2003, military investigators traveled to Guantánamo Bay, Cuba, to interview one of the prison camp’s most valuable detainees, a Qaeda loyalist from Morocco named Abdullah Tabarak. According to multiple reports, Mr. Tabarak had been Osama bin Laden’s chief bodyguard and longtime confidant, and he gave himself up to help bin Laden elude capture shortly after the Sept. 11 attacks. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Milk Carton Guy', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Milk Carton Guy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bergdahl, Bowe R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tabarak, Abdullah', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisoners of War', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tabarak, Abdullah', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-14T18:35:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e07c5d0d-91b2-5381-a419-a3e26eaa162b', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e07c5d0d-91b2-5381-a419-a3e26eaa162b'}, {'abstract': 'The University of Tokyo, which produces many of Japan’s top politicians, doctors and even baseball team owners, is a lightweight when it comes to the nation’s most popular sport, baseball.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/sports/baseball/university-of-tokyos-baseball-failures-inspire-fans.html', 'snippet': 'The University of Tokyo, which produces many of Japan’s top politicians, doctors and even baseball team owners, is a lightweight when it comes to the nation’s most popular sport, baseball.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Itaru Kobayashi remembers what his baseball coach at the University of Tokyo used to tell the 