egins Sunday night on PBS.', 'lead_paragraph': '“If you’re chained up in a safe at the bottom of a shark tank,” a colleague advises early in “The Escape Artist,” what you do is “call for Houdini.” The Houdini he means is Will Burton (David Tennant, expressive as ever), London’s top-ranked junior criminal defense barrister, according to a recent poll, and well up the ladder toward queen’s counsel because of his ability to win any case. But his invincible legal acrobatics backfire when a handshake denied sends his latest client, the reclusive bird lover, sadist and psychopath Liam Foyle (Toby Kebbell), into a rage — leaving Will, his wife (Ashley Jensen) and their son (Gus Barry) targets of his revenge. Enter Will’s professional nemesis, Maggie Gardner (Sophie Okonedo, fresh off a Tony victory for best supporting actress in a play for “A Raisin in the Sun”), frothing to overtake her rival. David Wolstencroft, the mastermind behind the BBC spy series “MI-5,” wrote this two-part “Masterpiece Mystery!” thriller, beginning Sunday night on PBS, which plumbs Will’s justification for his job in all its ramifications. “Everyone deserves a defense,” he maintains. Even he.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/15/arts/15GUIDE1/15GUIDE1-superJum