or': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PGA Tour Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ko, Lydia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Lexi', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaymer, Martin', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-16T01:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79f29a37-ab56-598c-9181-18a05daf5ac9', 'word_count': 1082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79f29a37-ab56-598c-9181-18a05daf5ac9'}, {'abstract': 'What a deadly blast at a federal arsenal revealed about women laborers during the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/15/an-explosion-in-washington/', 'snippet': 'What a deadly blast at a federal arsenal revealed about women laborers during the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The timing could not have been more eerily ironic. On June 17, 1864, an official read a letter thanking the girls and women who worked at Washington’s federal arsenal for a donation to a monument for the 78 victims of a deadly 1862 arsenal blast in Pittsburgh. Then, just hours later, an explosion – like “a sudden flash of lightning,” in the words of one survivor – went off inside the arsenal’s 100-foot-long wooden laboratory.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Explosion in Washington', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-16T01:18:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Grady', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grady', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b3d4859-8273-5317-86ec-ee6cd7d95b86', 'word_count': 1287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b3d4859-8273-5317-86ec-ee6cd7d95b86'}, {'abstract': 'In soccer as in life, we Italians tend to do best when our backs are against the wall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/16/sports/never-boring-always-beautiful.html', 'snippet': 'In soccer as in life, we Italians tend to do best when our backs are against the wall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some people party too soon. I was in Brazil in spring 2006, and Brazil was in a festive mood. The World Cup in Germany was on the way, and Brazilians, rightly convinced they had the best team, were already celebrating their triumph. At one public event, I said: “Just calm down, please. Italy is going to win this one.” The audience chortled, saying: “Come on. Italian soccer is in the midst of its biggest scandal ever. Two championship titles have been revoked. The players are in shock, and the clubs are in disgrace!” That’s why, I said.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Never Boring, Always Beautiful', 'kicker': 'Italy', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Never Boring, Always Beautiful', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Severgnini, Beppe', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2014 (Soccer)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-16T01:21:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Beppe Severgnini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beppe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severgnini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30404032-5e1e-5dd7-a1e7-fee46f22e68c', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30404032-5e1e-5dd7-a1e7-fee46f22e68c'}, {'abstract': 'Brazilian soccer style transformed the “British and Apollonian” game into “Dionysian dance.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/16/sports/where-prose-turned-to-poetry.html', 'snippet': 'Brazilian soccer style transformed the “British and Apollonian” game into “Dionysian dance.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Brazil, soccer was initially played among elite clubs behind closed doors. Blacks were excluded. Outside the confines of formal championships, however, the sport was quickly taken up as a game played in abandoned lots, meadows and urban gaps. Balls were improvised, fields were improvised, and the game was fertile ground for the spirit of improvisation. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/16/sports/PLAYBRAZIL/PLAYBRAZIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/16/sports/PLAYBRAZIL/PLAYBRAZIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/16/sports/PLAYBRAZIL/PLAYBRAZIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/16/sports/PLAYBRAZIL/PLAYBRAZIL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1370, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/16/sports/PLAYBRAZIL/PLAYBRAZIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/16/sports/PLAYBRAZIL/PLAYBRAZIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/16/sports/PLAYBRAZIL/PLAYBRAZIL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Prose Turned to Poetry', 'kicker': 'Brazil', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where Prose Turned to Poetry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2014 (Soccer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-16T01:22:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By José Miguel Wisnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'José', 'middlename': 'Miguel', 'lastname': 'Wisnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f92e0a5-275c-5d5a-8f52-dc5df908ac83', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f92e0a5-275c-5d5a-8f52-dc5df908ac83'}, {'abstract': 'Isaias and Bonifacio Flores-Mendez were each sentenced to life in prison last month for smuggling hundreds of Mexican women across the border and forcing them into prostitution in and around New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/16/nyregion/life-sentences-for-two-sex-traffickers-who-preyed-on-mexican-immigrants.html', 'snippet': 'Isaias and Bonifacio Flores-Mendez were each sentenced to life in prison last month for smuggling hundreds of Mexican women across the border and forcing them into prostitution in and around New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a sprawling family business, employing drivers, dispatchers and doormen. There were “steerers” who passed out “chica” cards on the street to solicit customers. There was even a mechanic who swept vehicles for tracking devices that might have been surreptitiously placed by federal agents, prosecutors said.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/16/nyregion/16trafficking-01/16trafficking-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 543, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/16/nyregion/16trafficking-01/16trafficking-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, '