', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bharara, Preet', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flores-Mendez, Bonifacio', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flores-Mendez, Isaias', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-16T01:23:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9134bd9d-d1ff-5936-8425-23bea91dfe2a', 'word_count': 1277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9134bd9d-d1ff-5936-8425-23bea91dfe2a'}, {'abstract': 'Every team is simply trying to score goals while preventing its opponent from doing the same. But they all seem to go about it in distinct ways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/16/sports/how-we-play-the-game.html', 'snippet': 'Every team is simply trying to score goals while preventing its opponent from doing the same. But they all seem to go about it in distinct ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every team is simply trying to score goals while preventing its opponent from doing the same. But they all seem to go about it in distinct ways. To understand what is happening on the fields in Brazil at the World Cup, one must learn a bit about each country’s history, and literature, and music, and regionalism, and economy — not to mention bicycles and pottery. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How We Play the Game', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How We Play the Game', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-16T01:23:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af4bad3e-87a2-5f3a-a444-9e902a68c6be', 'word_count': 87, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af4bad3e-87a2-5f3a-a444-9e902a68c6be'}, {'abstract': 'South Carolina once sent a 14-year-old to the electric chair after a hasty prosecution that was riddled with misconduct. Now there is a push for exoneration.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/16/opinion/seeking-justice-for-george-stinney.html', 'snippet': 'South Carolina once sent a 14-year-old to the electric chair after a hasty prosecution that was riddled with misconduct. Now there is a push for exoneration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the first page of the official sentencing report for George Stinney Jr., the outline of a boy is visible in a short string of numbers. Age: 14. Height: 5-1. Weight: 95. His complexion is listed as Black, his religion as Baptist, his occupation as None. Next to Build one word is typed: Small.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/24/opinion/editorial-notebook-icon/editorial-notebook-icon-jumbo.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/24/opinion/editorial-notebook-icon/editorial-notebook-icon-superJumbo.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/24/opinion/editorial-notebook-icon/editorial-notebook-icon-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/24/opinion/editorial-notebook-icon/editorial-notebook-icon-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Boy’s Execution, 70 Years Later', 'kicker': 'Editorial Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Boy’s Execution, 70 Years Later', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stinney, George J Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-16T01:25:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Wegman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wegman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a253743-911a-51d2-806b-a8bbdc2aee83', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a253743-911a-51d2-806b-a8bbdc2aee83'}, {'abstract': 'SwiftKey’s app for Android, which the London start-up made available free last week, is used on almost 250 million handsets. Now the company hopes to develop a relationship with Apple.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/16/technology/a-smartphone-keyboard-app-that-anticipates-what-you-want-to-type.html', 'snippet': 'SwiftKey’s app for Android, which the London start-up made available free last week, is used on almost 250 million handsets. Now the company hopes to develop a relationship with Apple.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Typing on the glass of a smartphone can be a headache. The keyboards are tiny. Errors are easy to make, even on the simplest of words. And autocorrect, the function meant to fix typing mistakes, often introduces errors, switching words that have little to do with what people actually mean.', '