on_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/df69135d-87b9-5a3c-8751-07797a8b3f99', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/df69135d-87b9-5a3c-8751-07797a8b3f99'}, {'abstract': 'The discovery of intact eggs and pterosaur fossils in China suggests that the creatures nested in large groups.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/17/science/pterosaurs-flying-reptiles-were-a-social-lot.html', 'snippet': 'The discovery of intact eggs and pterosaur fossils in China suggests that the creatures nested in large groups.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scientists have discovered the first intact eggs from pterosaurs, the flying reptiles that lived alongside the dinosaurs. The eggs, along with dozens of pterosaur fossils, are from a site discovered in 2005 in northwestern China. In a new study in the journal Current Biology, the scientists report that the pterosaurs belong to a new genus and species, Hamipterus tianshanensis, dating to the Early Cretaceous, 145 million to 100 million years ago. They had wingspans of five to 11 feet.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/17/science/17OBOX1/17OBOX1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 440, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/17/science/17OBOX1/17OBOX1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 440}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/17/science/17OBOX1/17OBOX1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/17/science/17OBOX1/17OBOX1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1503, 'width': 2048