 a fun dad?” did prompt a number of amusing stories. We heard about dads who moonwalk, sing ZZ Top songs, play “name that potato,” dress up as the Grim Reaper on holidays and find various other ways to connect with their sons and daughters. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/18/health/18well_dads/18well_dads-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 232, 'width': 590, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/18/health/18well_dads/18well_dads-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 232, 'width': 590, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/18/health/18well_dads/18well_dads-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/18/health/18well_dads/18well_dads-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/18/health/18well_dads/18well_dads-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Putting Fun in Fatherhood', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Father's Day", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-17T20:45:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c38d0872-0e7b-5120-9b8b-6d92d354c968', 'word_count': 1287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c38d0872-0e7b-5120-9b8b-6d92d354c968'}, {'abstract': 'Many have criticized “Game of Thrones” for its depictions of female pain, but one writer says the show and others like it neglect female pleasure, too, ANNA NORTH writes.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/17/does-tv-ignore-female-sexuality/', 'snippet': 'Many have criticized “Game of Thrones” for its depictions of female pain, but one writer says the show and others like it neglect female pleasure, too, ANNA NORTH writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much criticism of “Game of Thrones” — and, indeed, of cable TV in general — has focused around its depictions of violence against women. But at Salon, Lili Loofbourow points to another problem: Much of television may offer not just too many scenes of brutality toward women but also too few designed to make them feel good.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/17/opinion/nyto-thrones/nyto-thrones-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/17/opinion/nyto-thrones/nyto-thrones-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/17/opinion/nyto-thrones/nyto-thrones-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/17/opinion/nyto-thrones/nyto-thrones-superJumbo.jpg', 'height