subject', 'value': 'Ethics and Official Misconduct', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Mail', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lerner, Lois', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House Oversight and Government Reform Committee', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T01:46:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Theodore Schleifer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Theodore', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schleifer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2da3dd85-893e-5d71-9a70-b29013c43b20', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2da3dd85-893e-5d71-9a70-b29013c43b20'}, {'abstract': 'The bill would deliver perhaps the most significant change yet under Mayor Bill de Blasio’s Vision Zero plan to eliminate traffic deaths in the city by 2024.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/18/nyregion/bill-to-reduce-speed-limit-to-25-mph-gains-favor.html', 'snippet': 'The bill would deliver perhaps the most significant change yet under Mayor Bill de Blasio’s Vision Zero plan to eliminate traffic deaths in the city by 2024.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A proposal to lower the New York City speed limit to 25 miles per hour cleared a critical hurdle on Tuesday, as a bill in the State Senate won the support of the de Blasio administration and some, though not all, traffic safety advocates.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bill to Reduce Speed Limit to 25 M.P.H. Gains Favor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bill to Reduce Speed Limit to 25 M.P.H. Gains Favor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speed Limits and Speeding', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T01:49:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c29681e-884c-5626-912a-d6b16b72f0d2', 'word_count': 576, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c29681e-884c-5626-912a-d6b16b72f0d2'}, {'abstract': 'The slow and steady withdrawal of stimulus has not been accompanied by a slow and steady improvement in economic conditions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/18/opinion/the-limits-of-the-fed.html', 'snippet': 'The slow and steady withdrawal of stimulus has not been accompanied by a slow and steady improvement in economic conditions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Federal Reserve is expected to conclude its two-day meeting on Wednesday afternoon with another $10 billion scale-back of its monthly bond purchases. At that pace, the bond-buying program — an effort to stimulate the economy by lowering long-term interest rates — will be over by December at the latest.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Limits of the Fed', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Limits of the Fed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quantitative Easing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T01:49:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/80d3794c-5d36-560c-b966-d43422a0319e', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80d3794c-5d36-560c-b966-d43422a0319e'}, {'abstract': '“Gertrude Stein Saints!” sets some of this author’s characters, real and invented, to music at Abrons Arts Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/18/theater/gertrude-stein-saints-sets-vivid-words-to-music.html', 'snippet': '“Gertrude Stein Saints!” sets some of this author’s characters, real and invented, to music at Abrons Arts Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Everybody’s Autobiography,” Gertrude Stein explained why she preferred saints to martyrs. “A really good saint does nothing,” she wrote. “Anybody is more interesting doing nothing than doing something.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Following in a Writer’s Rhythms', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Elliptical Rhythms, Echoed Onstage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stein, Gertrude', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Gertrude Stein Saints! (Play)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Theater Plastique', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Mellon University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABRONS ARTS CENTER', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T02:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fde14a24-1951-546e-abf9-bda5967a2412', 'word_count': 537, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fde14a24-1951-546e-abf9-bda5967a2412'}, {'abstract': 'Cherry Jones plays a character who runs a bed-and-breakfast that becomes an early shelter for battered women in the earnest, thoughtful drama “When We Were Young and Unafraid.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/18/theater/when-we-were-young-and-unafraid-with-cherry-jones.html', 'snippet': 'Cherry Jones plays a character who runs a bed-and-breakfast that becomes an early shelter for battered women in the earnest, thoughtful drama “When We Were Young and Unafraid.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The year is 1972, and a new age of feminism is dawning as women grope to find voices to match their evolving identities. Well, some of them are groping. The female characters in “When We Were Young and Unafraid,” Sarah Treem’s debate-driven new play at City Center Stage 1, appear to have little difficulty articulating their viewpoints or making sure that we know exactly where to place them on a shifting scale of political consciousness.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/18/arts/18WHEN/18WHEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge',