ject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T13:06:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear and Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/414cee20-caba-59f9-84da-a5ad6fd6060b', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/414cee20-caba-59f9-84da-a5ad6fd6060b'}, {'abstract': 'The economists charged with making the determination say it’s unclear if the economy is expanding or merely taking a pause.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/19/upshot/europe-may-be-in-a-recession-still.html', 'snippet': 'The economists charged with making the determination say it’s unclear if the economy is expanding or merely taking a pause.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is the European economy expanding, or is it in recession? Turns out, even the people whose job it is to decide aren’t sure. And their reasoning shows why the economic situation seems so perilous in Europe, even though its economy isn’t clearly shrinking. In fact, part of their hesitation to call the end of the recession stems from their fear that policy makers may not be sufficiently concerned about the state of the economy.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Europe May Be in a Recession (Still)', 'kicker': 'Euro Economics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Recession in Europe May Not Yet Be Over, Economists Say', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Irwin, Neil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Monetary Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T13:12:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Irwin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Irwin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a957dd0b-1e98-561f-b691-8bdcda4b8590', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a957dd0b-1e98-561f-b691-8bdcda4b8590'}, {'abstract': 'If you’re looking for high-energy fun, head to Union Square, where the high-flying acrobats from “Fuerza Bruta: Wayra” will splash around in giant pools over your head.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/theater/high-energy-fun-in-fuerza-bruta-wayra.html', 'snippet': 'If you’re looking for high-energy fun, head to Union Square, where the high-flying acrobats from “Fuerza Bruta: Wayra” will splash around in giant pools over your head.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Theater doesn’t really have an equivalent of the summer blockbuster. If anything, the home of the biggest, showiest spectacles — Broadway — is usually quiet during the hot months, with few new openings. But if you’re looking for high-energy fun and empty-headed action accessible to anyone, head to Union Square, where the high-flying acrobats from “Fuerza Bruta: Wayra” will splash around in giant pools over your head. From the makers of “De la Guarda,” this show, which opens at the Daryl Roth Theater on Friday, is an expanded version of a production that ran in New York from 2007 until January, with 20 new minutes added.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'c