that began in 2011 is hibernating.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT — Many people probably thought the euro zone recession ended about a year ago when output began growing again. Not so, a group of leading economists argued this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/business/international/19euecon-pic/19euecon-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 465, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/19/business/international/19euecon-pic/19euecon-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 465}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/business/international/19euecon-pic/19euecon-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 794, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/business/international/19euecon-pic/19euecon-pic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1588, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/business/international/19euecon-pic/19euecon-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/19/business/international/19euecon-pic/19euecon-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/business/international/19euecon-pic/19euecon-pic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Euro Zone, Recession Is Not Quite Over, Economists Say', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Monetary Union', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T13:48:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c389ad7a-9ca8-5c43-aa74-422ec7b7f9aa', 'word_count': 707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c389ad7a-9ca8-5c43-aa74-422ec7b7f9aa'}, {'abstract': 'Why party unity doesn’t necessarily protect presidential coalitions.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/18/the-democrats-united/', 'snippet': 'Why party unity doesn’t necessarily protect presidential coalitions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vox’s Matthew Yglesias, seconded by my colleague Paul Krugman, has written\xa0a seven-point response to my argument that the Democratic coalition is more fragile, and more dependent on Hillary Clinton’s unique strengths, than many people (mostly liberals) tend to think. Yglesias’s rebuttal focuses on the Democratic Party’s apparent lack of deep policy divisions, which he regards as the main reason why there’s so little opposition to Clinton at the moment. “It is impossible,” he writes, “to mount a coherent anti-Clinton campaign because there is no issue that divides the mass of Democrats.” There’s no equivalent of the intra-Democrat war over the Iraq War that gave us Dean v. Kerry and than Obama v. Clinton; the neoliberal/populist debate over Wall Street is muddled and complicated; there’s unanimity on the need to tackle inequality; the education reform debate is real but doesn’t matter enough at the federal level to shape presidential politics; and the party’s demographic diversity coexists with increasing ideological conformity. Then as his parting point, he notes that polarization and partisan rancor make it hard to imagine either political coalition truly dissolving any time soon: The collapses of the past, “where one party’s candidate gets utterly blown away … simply can’t happen under modern conditions.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Democrats United?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krugman, Paul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T13:49:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47b04606-90ee-5505-b4d7-3e2284cd7ddf', 'word_count': 1029, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47b04606-90ee-5505-b4d7-3e2284cd7ddf'}, {'abstract': 'The websites of Next Media, which publishes Apple Daily, an independent Hong Kong newspaper often critical of the Chinese government, were offline for hours in the largest Internet attack the company has experienced.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/18/pro-democracy-media-comp