': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/18/blogs/20140618-lens-veil-slide-GZ4I/20140618-lens-veil-slide-GZ4I-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/18/blogs/20140618-lens-veil-slide-GZ4I/20140618-lens-veil-slide-GZ4I-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/18/blogs/20140618-lens-veil-slide-GZ4I/20140618-lens-veil-slide-GZ4I-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pulling Back the Veil, or Keeping It On', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T21:07:52+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Blogs', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/36571dcb-a199-5746-b9fb-8716f019953c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/36571dcb-a199-5746-b9fb-8716f019953c'}, {'abstract': 'Some camps, in an effort to end not just the “fat talk,” but the mindset that leads to it, have put in place rules banning all body talk: positive, negative or in between.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/18/no-fat-talk-and-no-skinny-talk-either-at-some-camps/', 'snippet': 'Some camps, in an effort to end not just the “fat talk,” but the mindset that leads to it, have put in place rules banning all body talk: positive, negative or in between.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many children figure out young that one road to conversation with peers, older children and even parents is to talk appearance — theirs, or someone else’s. “She really shouldn’t wear those pants,” or even just “Did you notice his beard?” spark conversations (not always kind), and can lead, eventually, to the dubious bonding ritual that is negative self-talk: “No cookies for me! Not with these thighs!” “Oh, I know, my jeans are getting so tight!” Ninety-three percent of college women told one researcher they had engaged in such “fat talk,” and such talk comes with a cost, putting the focus on weight and appearance, and not in a positive way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No ‘Fat Talk’ and No Skinny Talk, Either, at Some Camps', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camps and Camping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T21:15:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f4ef8a5-6cd9-5688-bf83-1b67c42353f8', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f4ef8a5-6cd9-5688-bf83-1b67c42353f8'}, {'abstract': 'Leonard Grant, a.k.a. Uncle Murda, chronicled his life as a Brooklyn rap performer as a colleague, Ronald Herron, known as Ra Diggs, stands trial for three killings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/19/nyregion/uncle-murda-testifies-for-defense-in-ra-diggss-trial.html', 'snippet': 'Leonard Grant, a.k.a. Uncle Murda, chronicled his life as a Brooklyn rap performer as a colleague, Ronald Herron, known as Ra Diggs, stands trial for three killings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Studio time u