{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kameon, Judy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T22:12:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d88c7f3-b65c-5a63-a330-208532c1a05d', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d88c7f3-b65c-5a63-a330-208532c1a05d'}, {'abstract': 'Ameico, an importer of contemporary design, opens an unusual shop in New Milford, Conn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/19/garden/from-ma-bell-to-design-central.html', 'snippet': 'Ameico, an importer of contemporary design, opens an unusual shop in New Milford, Conn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ameico, an importer of contemporary design, has turned an abandoned telephone exchange building in New Milford, Conn., into an unlikely showcase. The 1917 structure, which had been boarded up for two decades, has been renovated and filled with a retail shop, called 29 Church. It sells clocks and watches from Braun, flatware by Konstantin Grcic for Serafino Zani, and classic posters by Bruno Munari and Enzo Mari for Danese. Upstairs, shoppers will find furniture and lighting from &Tradition, Santa & Cole, Max Bill and the Josef Albers Foundation. The 6,000-square-foot complex includes a library stocked with books on industrial design and will be open to the public for research. Plans are afoot to host industrial design conferences and exhibitions. “We have always felt that one of our missions is to educate consumers about what is good design,” said Peter Kahane, Ameico’s founder. Information: 888-350-8765 or ameico.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/garden/19OPEN/19OPEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 272, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/19/garden/19OPEN/19OPEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 272}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/garden/19OPEN/19OPEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 464, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/garden/19OPEN/19OPEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 928, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/garden/19OPEN/19OPEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/19/garden/19OPEN/19OPEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/garden/19OPEN/19OPEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Ma Bell to Design Central', 'kicker': 'Currents: Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Ma Bell to Design Central', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirst, Arlene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ameico Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Milford (Conn)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-18T22:13:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b067404-fe6b-55b3-9a18-8ff0c240dfd2', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b067404-fe6b-55b3-9a18-8ff0c240dfd2'}, {'abstract': 'A new feature of Design Miami/Basel is a show of intriguing objects that fall between art and design.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/19/garden/breaking-free-of-boundaries.html', 'snippet': 'A new feature of Design Miami/Basel is a show of intriguing objects that fall between art and design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Design Miami/Basel, a part of the semiannual Art Basel fair, opened on Tuesday in Switzerland with a new feature called “Design at Large.” Curated by Dennis Freedman, the creative director of Barneys New York, the program displays six large-scale installations stationed under an oculus. The works, a few of which have interactive or performance-based elements, aren’t intended to be commercial, Mr. Freedman said, adding, “None of these pieces could ever have been shown in a booth.” The group includes a tiny biomorphic plastic house, designed in 1965 by the French architect Jean Maneval, and Sheila Hicks’s interactive color lab, “Séance,” which consists of colorful fiber bundles that look like boulders Information: designmiami.com.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/garden/19SHOWS/19SHOWS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, '