in what looks to be a record-setting year.” The editorial calls on state governments to introduce measures that would make any attempt to bring a firearm into an airport a felony. “Unless states decide to classify bringing a gun into security as a felony, though, forgetful people will almost certainly continue to have the right to own, carry and misplace firearms because basic responsibility hasn’t been made a prerequisite for gun ownership.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T10:41:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c47e42b-519a-5dea-af11-7e684a3a2b8d', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c47e42b-519a-5dea-af11-7e684a3a2b8d'}, {'abstract': 'Corey Kilgannon writes the Character Study column, which appears weekly online and in print in the Sunday Metropolitan section for readers in the New York City region.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/06/19/in-new-york-on-the-hunt-for-characters/', 'snippet': 'Corey Kilgannon writes the Character Study column, which appears weekly online and in print in the Sunday Metropolitan section for readers in the New York City region.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sara Ashley O’Brien and Kimberly Ammiano, students at the Columbia University Graduate School of Journalism, followed Metropolitan reporter Corey Kilgannon as he interviewed people for his weekly Character Study column.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In New York, on the Hunt for Characters', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kimchi (South Korean Food)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kilgannon, Corey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rockaway Peninsula (Queens, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T10:59:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19ae1893-1ae6-580f-8f69-20268495a7f4', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19ae1893-1ae6-580f-8f69-20268495a7f4'}, {'abstract': 'The goal is to sustain team enthusiasm when people are working hard, often late at night and on weekends, to release a product they will probably have to redo repeatedly until it’s right.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/19/keeping-a-cash-strapped-start-up-on-track/', 'snippet': 'The goal is to sustain team enthusiasm when people are working hard, often late at night and on weekends, to release a product they will probably have to redo repeatedly until it’s right.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a start-up, there’s always too much to do and not enough time or money to do it. There are never enough people to fix what’s broken and never enough resources to develop the next strategic phase. For the first 12 months of Posse, I felt as if I was swimming as hard as I could, trying to avoid drowning. But I knew that if we were going to succeed I had to develop a rhythm to our development.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Keeping a Cash-Starved Start-Up on Track', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T11:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebekah Campbell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebekah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Campbell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4d67891-ffc8-5094-bcce-5d145ea4b281', 'word_count': 1188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4d67891-ffc8-5094-bcce-5d145ea4b281'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Marlene Dietrich travels on a French liner in 1939; the U.S. Senate votes in favor of civil rights in 1964.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/19/in-our-pages-june-20/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Marlene Dietrich travels on a French liner in 1939; the U.S. Senate votes in favor of civil rights in 1964.', 'lead_paragraph': '1939: Normandie Arrives Late', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/blogs/20iht-oldjune20/20iht-oldjune20-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 753, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/20/blogs/20iht-oldjune20/20iht-oldjune20-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 6