vents in Iraq, Ukraine and elsewhere seems to be that they will empower the Republican Party’s hawkish establishment at the expense of the party’s libertarian/anti-interventionist wing\xa0— strengthening the hawks’ critique of the Obama White House,\xa0bringing Bush-era voices back to the fore, and throwing new obstacles in Rand Paul’s already-difficult path to the nomination. And when you combine this scenario with Hillary Clinton’s none-too-subtle\xa0positioning of herself\xa0as a more interventionist figure than the current president, you have the possibility of the return of a kind of 2004-style hawkish center in American presidential politics, in which both Clinton and her eventual G.O.P. rival (be it a Cruz, a Rubio, a Bush, a Christie, whomever) would compete to out-tough the other on terrorism, Syria, Iran, ISIS, Putin, and so forth.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Unsettled Politics of Foreign Policy', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beck, Glenn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cheney, Dick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Megyn', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Rand', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox 