iddlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85dad3d1-0b56-5ea5-8d73-20c7d414e7da', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85dad3d1-0b56-5ea5-8d73-20c7d414e7da'}, {'abstract': 'Jeffery Renard Allen’s novel “Song of the Shank” draws on the real life of Thomas Wiggins, a blind piano prodigy and autistic savant born a slave in 19th-century Georgia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/books/review/song-of-the-shank-by-jeffery-renard-allen.html', 'snippet': 'Jeffery Renard Allen’s novel “Song of the Shank” draws on the real life of Thomas Wiggins, a blind piano prodigy and autistic savant born a slave in 19th-century Georgia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before the Chitlin’ Circuit, before the Cotton Club and eons before “Soul Train” or Black Entertainment Television (BET) put black talent on display, there was Congo Square in New Orleans. During Louisiana’s 18th-century French and Spanish colonial era, slaves were allowed to convene near the square on their Sundays off from work to sing, dance and play music — a practice that continued beyond the Louisiana Purchase. Word spread of the spectacle, and soon the slaves began to draw crowds of whites. Later the attempted suppression of “savage” African music by the Protestants helped transform Congo Square into a phenomenon sought by visitors from far and wide. You could argue that in that square, located in what is now Louis Armstrong Park, the American black as an entertainer was born. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JACKSONCOVER/22JACKSONCOVER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 464, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JACKSONCOVER/22JACKSONCOVER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 464}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JACKSONCOVER/22JACKSONCOVER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 791, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JACKSONCOVER/22JACKSONCOVER-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1583, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JACKSONCOVER/0622JACKSONCOVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JACKSONCOVER/0622JACKSONCOVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JACKSONCOVER/0622JACKSONCOVER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Command Performance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Command Performance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Allen, Jeffery Renard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T19:09:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mitchell S. Jackson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mitchell', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Jackson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'or