ich stories from their time in Iraq have been resurfacing in their minds. Several recalled ominous hints of what was to come, while others described the moment that pushed them to see just how deep-seated Iraq’s problems are.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/world/atwar_sominireflection/atwar_sominireflection-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/19/world/atwar_sominireflection/atwar_sominireflection-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 487}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/world/atwar_sominireflection/atwar_sominireflection-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 831, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/world/atwar_sominireflection/atwar_sominireflection-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1539, 'width': 1897, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/world/atwar_sominireflection/atwar_sominireflection-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/19/world/atwar_sominireflection/atwar_sominireflection-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/19/world/atwar_sominireflection/atwar_sominireflection-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Moments in Iraq Reporters Will Never Forget', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Iraqi Army', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Army', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baghdad (Iraq)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T20:25:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed295c18-3ff5-59ed-b439-498a58cdc34c', 'word_count': 1364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed295c18-3ff5-59ed-b439-498a58cdc34c'}, {'abstract': 'With her cellphone turned off, the author journeys to Rishikesh, India, where some people think yoga was born.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/travel/unplugging-in-the-unofficial-capital-of-yoga.html', 'snippet': 'With her cellphone turned off, the author journeys to Rishikesh, India, where some people think yoga was born.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a hot and dusty afternoon, about 30 hours into my Indian journey, when the bus driver dropped me off in a small town that he said was near my destination: Rishikesh, regarded as the unofficial world capital of yoga. Only a short rickshaw ride, it seemed, separated my fatigued self from inner Zen. Nearly two weeks of life with monks at an ashram awaited.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/travel/22-yoga-top/22-yoga-top-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/20