', 'snippet': 'The new majority whip voted against the debt ceiling increase.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Interest groups love keeping scorecards measuring the fidelity of lawmakers, but to judge the new leaders that House Republicans elected today all you really need to know is how they’d answer the question: Do you believe in governing by consensus or by destruction? Republicans today elected one of each type, which will probably lead to more tension, more obstruction, and less progress.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Steve Scalise, Obstructionist, Joins the House Leadership', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Kevin (1965- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scalise, Steve', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T21:49:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Firestone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firestone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19077b2f-2a83-5a96-9dc1-c90311fefa74', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19077b2f-2a83-5a96-9dc1-c90311fefa74'}, {'abstract': 'The Queens Museum uses its “Panorama” of New York as a launching point for “Bringing the World Into the World,” a playful look at ways to see reality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/20/arts/design/bringing-the-world-into-the-world-at-the-queens-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The Queens Museum uses its “Panorama” of New York as a launching point for “Bringing the World Into the World,” a playful look at ways to see reality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Queens Museum has a big advantage when it comes to the familiar curatorial tactic of using older works from its permanent collection to inspire an exhibition of contemporary art. At the museum’s figurative and actual heart lies one of the great visual wonders of New York: “The Panorama of the City of New York,” a nearly 10,000-square-foot miniaturization of every building, street, park and bridge in the five boroughs, complete with day-night lighting cycles and tiny planes zipping in and out of La Guardia and Kennedy along almost-invisible wires. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/arts/20QUEENSMUSEUM/20QUEENS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/20/arts/20QUEENSMUSEUM/20QUEENS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/arts/20QUEENSMUSEUM/20QUEENS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/arts/20QUEENSMUSEUM/20QUEENS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/arts/20QUEENSMUSEUM/20QUEENS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/20/arts/20QUEENSMUSEUM/20QUEENS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/arts/20QUEENSMUSEUM/20QUEENS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'If Seeing Is Believing, a Panorama of Truth', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'If Seeing Is Believing, a Panorama of Truth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Queens Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T21:55:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1866fad-44bf-56b4-8894-9ff831a6eea1', 'word_count': 1273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1866fad-44bf-56b4-8894-9ff831a6eea1'}, {'abstract': 'Hundreds of cultural events are planned across Europe and the United States to mark the 100th anniversary of World War I.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/arts/in-europe-and-the-us-cultural-reminders-of-world-war-i.html', 'snippet': 'Hundreds of cultural events are planned across Europe and the United States to mark the 100th anniversary of World War I.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — A century ago this month, the great powers of an earlier order began to unravel. World War I redrew the world map, ending the Hapsburg Empire and the Ottoman Empire and shifting the tectonic plates beneath Europe and the Middle East in ways that still resonate. From June 28, the date in 1914 when a young radical assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria in Sarajevo, setting off a chain of events that started the war, to Nov. 11, the date that the Allies and Germany signed a first armistice ending the war in 1918, hundreds of cultural events are planned across Europe and the United States to observe the war’s 100th anniversary. Here are a few of them.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Events That Ponder a Grim Anniversary', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Events That Ponder a Grim Anniversary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Donadio, Rachel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T21:56:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Donadio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donadio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5890e1fa-48dd-5a7d-8b52-eec8eb98854f', 'word_count': 1038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5890e1fa-48dd-5a7d-8b52-eec8eb98854f'}, {'abstract': 'The agency said it would lower or eliminate some fines for failing to disclose offshore accounts, while tightening other requirements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/20/business/international/irs-eases-some-rules-for-taxpayers-overseas.html', 'snippet': 'The agency said it would lower or eliminate some fines for failing to disclose offshore accounts, while tightening other requirements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Facing criticism that its crackdown on overseas tax cheats had disproportionately hurt small taxpayers guilty of innocent oversights, the Internal Revenue Service said it would revise its rules, lowering or eliminating some fines while tightening other requirements.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I.R.S. Eases Some Rules for Taxpayers Overseas', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I.R.S. Eases Some Rules for Taxpayers Overseas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Americans Abroad', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T21:56:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Knowlton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Knowlton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8e24174-aaab-5682-8370-d51b22da1723', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8e24174-aaab-5682-8370-d51b22da1723'}, {'abstract': 'Ralph Vaughan Williams’s “A Pastoral Symphony,” written after his service in France and Greece, is rarely performed but has been recorded with particular warmth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/arts/music/vaughan-williamss-a-pastoral-symphony-inspired-by-war.html', 'snippet': 'Ralph Vaughan Williams’s “A Pastoral Symphony,” written after his service in France and Greece, is rarely performed but has been recorded with particular warmth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ralph Vaughan Williams, the British composer long pigeonholed as a purveyor of bucolic calm, knew war, though he didn’t have to. When World War I began, he was 41, old enough to have avoided military service.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/arts/22PASTORAL1/22PASTORAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 852, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/arts/22PASTORAL1/22PASTORAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 852}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/arts/22PASTORAL1/22PASTORAL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 722, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/arts/22PASTORAL1/22PASTORAL1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1443, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/arts/22PASTORAL1/22PASTORAL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/arts/22PASTORAL1/22PASTORAL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/arts/22PASTORAL1/22PASTORAL1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sadness of Bugles as Soldiers Head Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Sadness of Bugles as Soldiers Head Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Ralph Vaughan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T21:56:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/01e29de2-40a4-5138-8be7-2a4cf5a767d2', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01e29de2-40a4-5138-8be7-2a4cf5a767d2'}, {'abstract': 'Two shows at the International Center of Photography counter some traditional stereotypes of Latin America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/20/arts/design/urbes-mutantes-at-the-international-center-of-photography.html', 'snippet': 'Two shows at the International Center of Photography counter some traditional stereotypes of Latin America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Latin American spring is about to turn into a Latin American summer in New York City art museums. Purely, it seems, by chance, a record number of institutions have recently opened significant shows of work from South America and the Caribbean. The International Center of Photography, as usual one step ahead of the curve, has two.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In an Ironic Lens, a Latin Myth Evaporates', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In an Ironic Lens, a Latin Myth Evaporates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Latin America', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Hollan