 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kilroys', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T23:37:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cea97be-38de-5433-8fb9-5a7dfe4cd16e', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cea97be-38de-5433-8fb9-5a7dfe4cd16e'}, {'abstract': 'In “Coherence,” directed by James Ward Byrkit, a party deteriorates when a comet passes over the Earth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/20/movies/coherence-traces-a-descent-into-madness.html', 'snippet': 'In “Coherence,” directed by James Ward Byrkit, a party deteriorates when a comet passes over the Earth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a commonplace that speculative fiction speaks to the anxieties percolating in its age. Such seems the case with the likes of “Edge of Tomorrow,” which kinks up its narrative by repeatedly cycling back to the past, suggesting that complex theories about space-time are now so mainstream that they’re the stuff of mass entertainment. The same might be said of the far more modest “Coherence,” although it’s also true that digital tools, which allow directors to shoot fast, cheap and sometimes sloppily, are also helping pry loose linear narrative’s hold on movies. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just Another Little Dysfunctional Dinner Party', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Just Another Little Dysfunctional Dinner Party', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Coherence (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byrkit, James Ward', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T23:38:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/59364e64-5b6b-5cec-aa00-80cb86d6cfe9', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59364e64-5b6b-5cec-aa00-80cb86d6cfe9'}, {'abstract': 'Barbara Knecht of Care for the Homeless responds to a Sunday Review article.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/20/opinion/the-maze-of-homelessness.html', 'snippet': 'Barbara Knecht of Care for the Homeless responds to a Sunday Review article.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Maze of Homelessness', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Maze of Homelessness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Welfare (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T23:38:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a622d66-874b-5305-b9a0-8e09ecb6fc71', 'word_count': 202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a622d66-874b-5305-b9a0-8e09ecb6fc71'}, {'abstract': 'Fritch, Tex., population 2,000, was already reeling from years of drought when a fire devastated the Panhandle community in May. Now residents are wondering whether it can be rebuilt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/20/us/after-fire-fritch-tex-fights-for-its-existence.html', 'snippet': 'Fritch, Tex., population 2,000, was already reeling from years of drought when a fire devastated the Panhandle community in May. Now residents are wondering whether it can be rebuilt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRITCH, Tex. — A month after a fire roared through this Panhandle town, all that remains of Sam Jones’s house is its concrete foundation, a row of charred pine trees and the underground storm shelter where he now lives.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/us/20ttWildfire/20ttWildfire-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/20/us/20ttWildfire/20ttWildfire-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/us/20ttWildfire/20ttWildfire-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/us/20ttWildfire/20ttWildfire-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/us/20ttWildfire/20ttWildfire-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/20/us/20ttWildfire/20ttWildfire-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/20/us/20ttWildfire/20ttWildfire-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After a Fire, a Dusty Town Hopes Not to Evaporate', 'kicker': 'Texas Tribune', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After a Fire, a Dusty Town Hopes Not to Evaporate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fritch (Tex)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drought', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-19T23:44:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edgar Walters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edgar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61b08dd0-dafe-5910-bcf2-a6c6ff56a4f4', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61b08dd0-dafe-5910-bcf2-a6c6ff56a4f4'}, {'abstract': 'In a report, the Railroad Commission of Texas rejected the idea that nearby drilling was the cause of a high content of methane in wells along the Brazos River.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/20/us/a-push-for-new-look-into-tainted-waters-source.html', 'snippet': 'In a report, the Railroad Commission of Texas rejected the idea that nearby drilling was the cause of a high content of methane in wells along the Brazos River.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEATHERFORD, Tex. — Steve Lipsky has nearly everything he needs on his 14-acre estate along the Brazos River, west of Fort Worth. The estate includes a guesthouse, a resort-style swimming pool and a seven-bathroom, 15,000-square-foot home where he lives with his wife and three children. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype':