ge: “Though the system is still new, millions of Americans are already being helped, and every success makes it that much harder for its political enemies to repeal or disable it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T10:58:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8045b23-0854-5707-8cf2-cf820e433e45', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8045b23-0854-5707-8cf2-cf820e433e45'}, {'abstract': 'The reasons for a pause in advancing tax plans are partly practical, but philosophical shifts have also played a role.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/21/upshot/the-unexpected-ceasefire-in-washingtons-tax-wars.html', 'snippet': 'The reasons for a pause in advancing tax plans are partly practical, but philosophical shifts have also played a role.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I grew up castrating hogs,” Joni Ernst tells voters in Iowa in her signature TV ad, vowing to “make ′em squeal” in Washington. But she’s not talking about cutting income tax rates.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/21/upshot/21up-harwood/21up-harwood-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/21/upshot/21up-harwood/21up-harwood-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None,