a sliver of the globe. Trouble is, most of that sliver — like 71 percent of Earth’s surface — is covered by water. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/0622EPSTEINNIXON/0622EPSTEINNIXON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 326, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/0622EPSTEINNIXON/0622EPSTEINNIXON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 326}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/0622EPSTEINNIXON/0622EPSTEINNIXON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 556, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/0622EPSTEINNIXON/0622EPSTEINNIXON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1112, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/0622EPSTEINNIXON/0622EPSTEINNIXON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/0622EPSTEINNIXON/0622EPSTEINNIXON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/0622EPSTEINNIXON/0622EPSTEINNIXON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Descent', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Descent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Epstein, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Free Diving', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nestor, James', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T14:32:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Epstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Epstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/398ce0ec-35b3-5b69-95a5-7fdfd0b25c68', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/398ce0ec-35b3-5b69-95a5-7fdfd0b25c68'}, {'abstract': 'A history of our desire to conquer, understand, embrace and ultimately save the Great Barrier Reef.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/books/review/the-reef-by-iain-mccalman.html', 'snippet': 'A history of our desire to conquer, understand, embrace and ultimately save the Great Barrier Reef.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our planet’s coral reefs offer nourishment and sanctuary to as many as nine million species, running the gamut of life, from single-cell organisms to whales. The Great Barrier Reef, shimmering beneath the turquoise shallows of Australia’s east coast, surpasses all other coral ecosystems in scale and fame. This colossal spine of life stretches for 1,400 miles, roughly the distance from Boston to Havana. Today the Great Barrier Reef seems obviously spectacular: a World Heritage Site visible to astronauts in outer space and acclaimed as one of the earth’s greatest natural wonders. Yet as Iain \xadMcCalman reveals in his enthralling book, “The Reef,” little more than 200 years ago no human had any inkling of its dimensions, far less any inkling of the complex science of its precarious vitality.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Down Under', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Down Under', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCalman, Iain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Barrier Reef (Australia)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reefs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T14:32:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Nixon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nixon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4c54afa-3f23-53bb-8685-b06c501bc796', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4c54afa-3f23-53bb-8685-b06c501bc796'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Lauren Francis-Sharma, Claire Kendal, Lisa See, Jennifer Weiner and Lauren Willig.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/books/review/lisa-sees-china-dolls-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Lauren Francis-Sharma, Claire Kendal, Lisa See, Jennifer Weiner and Lauren Willig.', 'lead_paragraph': '’TIL THE WELL RUNS DRY  By Lauren Francis-Sharma  Holt, $27. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/0622-SHORTLIST/0622-SHORTLIST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/0622-SHORTLIST/0622-SHORTL