e': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Keishin Armstrong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Keishin', 'lastname': 'Armstrong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c57f27a8-3eb1-5626-8812-d0e9a6c03a03', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c57f27a8-3eb1-5626-8812-d0e9a6c03a03'}, {'abstract': 'A collective portrait of three couples, including Ernest Hemingway and Martha Gellhorn, caught up in the Spanish Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/books/review/hotel-florida-by-amanda-vaill.html', 'snippet': 'A collective portrait of three couples, including Ernest Hemingway and Martha Gellhorn, caught up in the Spanish Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While many ran in terror from the mounting monstrosities of Spain’s civil war in the late 1930s, three couples — the bright young photographers Robert Capa and Gerda Taro, the writers Ernest Hemingway and Martha Gellhorn, and the devoted press officers Arturo Barea and Ilsa Kulcsar — jumped into the fray. As Amanda Vaill writes in “Hotel Florida,” they risked their lives to shape public opinion and sharpen political will, daring their way toward a new destiny.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JENKINS/0622-JENKINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 471, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JENKINS/0622-JENKINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 471}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22JENKINS/0622-JENKINS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 804, 'width': 1024, 'subType'