': [{'firstname': 'Dashka', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slater', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/091f9af1-c231-5611-b204-33b9d08fd0c1', 'word_count': 431, 'uri': 'nyt://article/091f9af1-c231-5611-b204-33b9d08fd0c1'}, {'abstract': 'Taking the bait car.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/magazine/so-you-think-you-can-steal.html', 'snippet': 'Taking the bait car.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I witnessed an odd incident: Two cars stopped, and the drivers began arguing. After a few moments of yelling, one driver got into the other’s car, and they drove off, leaving the second car unlocked with the engine running. I saw this very situation on a reality TV show called “Bait Car”: a vehicle is left for someone to steal while the police and cameramen wait. I yelled: “Bait car! Bait car!” as a warning for people nearby. When I told this story later, I was criticized for helping would-be thieves. But I am not comfortable with the show’s premise. Should I have said nothing? STEVEN FONG, SAN FRANCISCO', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/magazine/22ethicist/mag-22Ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/magazine/22ethicist/mag-22Ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/magazine/22ethicist/mag-22Ethicist-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 585, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/magazine/22et