'lastname': 'Bolick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cb53070-1781-5d01-afc8-47b72a62a1a7', 'word_count': 727, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cb53070-1781-5d01-afc8-47b72a62a1a7'}, {'abstract': 'The poet’s letters reveal a dedicated writer and ambitious man.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/books/review/the-letters-of-robert-frost-volume-1-1886-1920.html', 'snippet': 'The poet’s letters reveal a dedicated writer and ambitious man.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early fall of 1912, a blandly handsome, tousle-headed American schoolteacher arrived in London. Nearing 40, coming without introduction or much of a plan — except, as he later confessed, “to write and be poor” — he was making a last attempt to write himself into poetry. It would have taken mad willfulness to drag his wife and four children out of their \xadsettled New Hampshire life in a quixotic assault on the London literary scene. Still, he was soon spending a candlelit evening with Yeats in the poet’s curtained rooms, having come to the attention of that “stormy petrel” Ezra Pound, who lauded him in reviews back home. Little more than two years later, the schoolteacher sailed back, having published his first two books, “A Boy’s Will” (1913) and “North of Boston” (1914). He had become Robert Frost.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22LOGAN/22LOGAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22LOGAN/22LOGAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 899}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22LOGAN/22LOGAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 684, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22LOGAN/22LOGAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1368, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22LOGAN/22LOGAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22LOGAN/22LOGAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22LOGAN/22LOGAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Early Frost', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Early Frost', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frost, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T14:43:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Logan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Logan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d001b4d8-c0e8-58b3-b3ae-6fb2cf409583', 'word_count': 1646, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d001b4d8-c0e8-58b3-b3ae-6fb2cf409583'}, {'abstract': 'From his fiction and cultural criticism to his latest adventure on an aircraft carrier, Geoff Dyer has defied genres for decades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/books/review/geoff-dyers-another-great-day-at-sea-the-colour-of-memory-and-the-search.html', 'snippet': 'From his fiction and cultural criticism to his latest adventure on an aircraft carrier, Geoff Dyer has defied genres for decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Geoff Dyer came aboard the U.S.S. George H. W. Bush for his 14-day stay, he had his Ping-Pong paddle securely stowed but had misplaced his notebook. Too tall, spindly, self-described as “a leftover from a novel Graham Greene had decided not to write,” he had managed through a stubbornness born of sheer neurotic panic to wheedle his way, despite considerable and justified resistance from the United States Navy, into private quarters on the giant but very crowded vessel: the Vice-Presidential Room, no less. His report of his first day on board is a nonstop string of complaints: The noise is unbearable, he keeps knocking his head on the low ceilings and hatches, the spaghetti is cold and the lettuce inedible, the oil fumes are overwhelming, the temperature is like a blast furnace, the air is toxic, the ocean glitters but is spoiled by a sky greased with jet fuel. Dyer admits he is constitutionally incapable of adjusting to new environments; in fact, the only thing he’s grown accustomed to, over the years, is that he never gets used to things. This is the same man who, when asked by a friend (Alain de Botton) if he would like to spend some time writing in an interesting and relaxing environment, immediately replied: Put me on an American aircraft carrier. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22MARTIN/22MARTIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 431, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22MARTIN/22MARTIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 431}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22MARTIN/22MARTIN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22MARTIN/22MARTIN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1473, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22MARTIN/22MARTIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22MARTIN/22MARTIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22MARTIN/22MARTIN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Wordy Shipmate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Wordy Shipmate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dyer, Geoff', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T14:44:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clancy Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6e68e7a-2d77-5565-bb48-77266724574b', 'word_count': 1601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6e68e7a-2d77-5565-bb48-77266724574b'}, {'abstract': 'A couple return to Poland, 40 years after the war and their escape from the Warsaw ghetto.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/books/review/gwen-edelmans-train-to-warsaw.html', 'snippet': 'A couple return to Poland, 40 years after the war and their escape from the Warsaw ghetto.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In September 1939, more than 350,000 Jews lived in Warsaw. Six years later, only 11,500 were left. Most of Warsaw itself had been wiped from the face of the earth. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22Towers/0622-bks-Towers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22Towers/0622-bks-Towers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22Towers/0622-bks-Towers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 597, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22Towers/0622-bks-Towers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1050, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22Towers/0622-bks-Towers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22Towers/0622-bks-Towers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22Towers/0622-bks-Towers-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exile’s End', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exile’s End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edelman, Gwen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T14:44:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Towers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Towers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d17b4c5-b89d-5ec0-95be-b155433889b5', 'word_count': 661, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d17b4c5-b89d-5ec0-95be-b155433889b5'}, {'abstract': 'Diana Gabaldon’s “Written in My Own Heart’s Blood” — the eighth entry in her Outlander series of time-traveling romances — is No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, with a new TV show based on the series coming in August.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Diana Gabaldon’s “Written in My Own Heart’s Blood” — the eighth entry in her Outlander series of time-traveling romances — is No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, with a new TV show based on the series coming in August.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Turkey Stuffing: Diana Gabaldon’s hefty new novel, “Written in My Own Heart’s Blood” — the eighth entry in her Outlander series of time-traveling romances — enters the hardcover fiction list right at No. 1. These books describe the escapades of an 18th-century Scotsman and the World War II-era English nurse who fell into his world courtesy of a witches’ coven; writing in Salon in 1999, the critic Gavin McNett called it “the smartest historical sci-fi adventure-romance story ever written by a science Ph.D. with a background in scripting ‘Scrooge McDuck’ comics.” It’s been five years since the last Outlander title hit stores, and with a new Starz TV show based on the series coming in August, fans are primed for the latest installment. Gabaldon answered their questions recently on the website Goodreads. “People often say to me, ‘You write such strong female characters,’ ” she told one reader who praised the depiction of her heroine, “and I say, ‘Well, I don’t like stupid women.’ ” Gabaldon also cited her belief that fiction has three kinds of characters: mushrooms, hard nuts and onions. It’s a theory she’s expanded on at her official website. Onions, she explains, “develop slowly through the addition of multiple layers of personality, rather than popping up full-fledged as the ‘mushrooms’ do.” Hard nuts, meanwhile, play a merely functional role, making them difficult to animate. “Onions, mushrooms and nuts,” Gabaldon writes; “this is beginning to sound like an exotic recipe for turkey stuffing.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29list-img/29list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29list-img/29list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/0