, 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T17:39:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22d7fce6-d001-5833-867b-c189e1738349', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22d7fce6-d001-5833-867b-c189e1738349'}, {'abstract': 'In “Hard Choices,” Hillary Clinton takes the reader along on her journey representing the United States as President Obama’s top diplomat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/07/06/books/review/hillary-rodham-clintons-hard-choices.html', 'snippet': 'In “Hard Choices,” Hillary Clinton takes the reader along on her journey representing the United States as President Obama’s top diplomat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1969, the night before a Wellesley College senior named Hillary Rodham gave a commencement address that would draw national attention, she was introduced to Dean Acheson, the legendary former secretary of state who had come to campus for his granddaughter’s graduation. “I’m looking forward to hearing what you have to say,” Acheson told Rodham. At the time, many in the country were looking forward to hearing what Acheson had to say. He had just put the finishing touches on “Present at the Creation,” his landmark memoir that would come out a few months after his encounter with the young Rodham, providing a seminal portrait of his role in helping Harry S. Truman forge a new national security architecture at the outset of the Cold War.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/06/books/review/06baker-img/06baker-img-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', '