tle': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68743093-ea68-512d-ab30-fb6c3cf2dc68', 'word_count': 1012, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68743093-ea68-512d-ab30-fb6c3cf2dc68'}, {'abstract': 'The story behind the cover of this week’s Book Review.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/06/20/trumping-the-first-choice/', 'snippet': 'The story behind the cover of this week’s Book Review.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cover review this week wasn’t our original choice — we had an entirely different plan at the ready, a review of something else, already edited with the illustration assigned — and then the review of the novel “Song of the Shank” by Jeffery Renard Allen came in, less than a week before we had to go to press.Mitchell S. Jackson, whose first novel, “The Residue Years,” came out last year, had written a total rave, calling Allen’s book “the kind of imaginative work only a prodigiously gifted risk-taker could produce.” It was an interesting match between reviewer and author because, as the assigning editor Gregory Cowles explained, “Their novels are very different — Jackson’s was contemporary and seemingly autobiographical, while Allen’s is on the face of it neither of those. But both are voice-driven and beautifully written, and both play with contemporary ideas about family, race, class and the long legacy of slavery.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/18/blogs/bookreview622/bookreview622-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 650, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/18/blogs/bookreview622/bookreview622-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 650}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/18/blogs/bookreview622/book