 in pop music was a novel idea back in 1995, when “Smokey Joe’s Cafe: The Songs of Leiber and Stoller” made its debut. But it worked. The musical, based on the work of Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller, ran for nearly five years, showcasing such beloved tunes as “Dance With Me,” “Kansas City,” “Hound Dog” and “There Goes My Baby.” ', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/nyregion/22ARTSLI1/22ARTSLI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/nyregion/22ARTSLI1/22ARTSLI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/nyregion/22ARTSLI1/22ARTSLI1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/nyregion/22ARTSLI1/22ARTSLI1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/nyregion/22ARTSLI1/22ARTSLI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/nyregion/22ARTSLI1/22ARTSLI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/nyregion/22ARTSLI1/22ARTSLI1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Revue of Pop Songs as Catchy as Poison Ivy', 'kicker': 'Theater Review | Long Island', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Revue of Pop Songs as Catchy as Poison Ivy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobson, Aileen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walker, Chet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gateway, Performing Arts Center of Suffolk County', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Smokey Joe's Cafe: The Songs of Leiber and Stoller (Movie)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bellport (NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stoller, Mike', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leiber, Jerry', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T18:03:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aileen Jacobson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aileen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b674ede-596a-52bc-ad00-27290c1ae039', 'word_count': 754, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b674ede-596a-52bc-ad00-27290c1ae039'}, {'abstract': 'Is there still a one-state solution for Iraq?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/20/letting-go-of-sykes-picot/', 'snippet': 'Is there still a one-state solution for Iraq?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this week I quoted David Frum, usually more hawkish than I, on the theme that American interests in Iraq might be better pursued by looking for solid ground in, say, Kurdistan than by trying to help Iran stabilize the Sunni Triangle or replaying the Diem drama with our new batch of military advisers and Nouri al-Maliki. Now Jeffrey Goldberg, also usually more hawkish than I, has written a follow-up to the 2008 essay I cited in my Sunday column, on the Middle East’s potential post-Iraq War map, which circles around to a similar conclusion:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letting Go of Sykes-Picot', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kurds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'FRUM, DAVID', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldberg, Jeffrey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ignatius, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zakaria, Fareed', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T18:05:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1ae6971-b434-55e7-b7dc-5a1d879d60d8', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1ae6971-b434-55e7-b7dc-5a1d879d60d8'}, {'abstract': 'A full-floor apartment at the bronze-and-glass tower One Madison is the most expensive sale of the week.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/20/sold-for-11-75-million-with-a-tycoon-for-a-neighbor/', 'snippet': 'A full-floor apartment at the bronze-and-glass tower One Madison is the most expensive sale of the week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A full-floor residence at One Madison, the sylphlike bronze-and-glass tower at 23 East 22nd Street that has inspired a defection of high-rollers, including the media mogul Rupert Murdoch, from less avant-garde locales uptown, sold for $11.75 million and was the most expensive sale of the week, according to city records.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/realestate/22TICKET/22TICKET-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/realestate/22TICKET/22TICKET-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/realestate/22TICKET/22TICKET-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/realestate/22TICKET/22TICKET-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/realestate/22TICKET/22TICKET-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/realestate/22TICKET/22TICKET-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/realestate/22TICKET/22TICKET-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Ticket | Sold for $11.75 Million, With a Tycoon for a Neighbor', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tycoon for a Neighbor ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flatiron District (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-20T18:10:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Finn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/679baef3-7efe-5fcd-af05-1b055c1baec7', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/679baef3-7efe-5fcd-af05-1b055c1baec7'}, {'abstract': 'At the Long Wharf Theater in New Haven, Split Knuckle Theater mashes up the financial crisis and the tale of the intrepid Sir Ernest Shackleton.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/nyregion/endurance-at-the-long-wharf-theater-in-new-haven.html', 'snippet': 'At the Long Wharf Theater in New Haven, Split Knuckle Theater mashes up the financial crisis and the tale of the intrepid Sir Ernest Shackleton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was Kevin Spacey. There was a weird dream about an out-of-control copy machine. And there was the unraveling of the economy in 2008. All played a part in the creation of “Endurance,” a 90-minute theatrical mash-up in which grumbling insurance workers morph into chantey-singing polar explorers, office furniture subs for a doughty wooden icebreaker, and a light switch conveys audiences at the Long Wharf Theater from a stan