arge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/sports/yankees-1/yankees-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/sports/yankees-1/yankees-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/sports/yankees-1/yankees-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/sports/yankees-1/yankees-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/sports/yankees-1/yankees-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/sports/yankees-1/yankees-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Job in Peril, Yankees’ Nuno Wobbles Through a Start', 'kicker': 'Orioles 6, Yankees 1', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Job in Peril, Yankees’ Nuno Wobbles Through a Start', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nuno, Vidal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arangure, Jorge Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Orioles', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-21T20:42:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jorge Arangure Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jorge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arangure', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5261932-1904-5154-b891-81793317b310', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5261932-1904-5154-b891-81793317b310'}, {'abstract': 'Readers comment on my column about two 20-year-olds living worlds apart–and why we can address humanitarian problems both at home and abroad.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/21/should-america-solve-its-own-problems-first/', 'snippet': 'Readers comment on my column about two 20-year-olds living worlds apart–and why we can address humanitarian problems both at home and abroad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whenever I write about international issues, I always get pushback from readers who say that we should solve our own domestic problems before worrying about the rest of the world. As House Majority Leader Tom DeLay put it years ago, international humanitarianism puts “Ghana over Grandma.” I don’t buy that, and my Sunday column is a response to that way of thinking. But as my column notes, I think there’s sometimes also the opposite tendency, especially among university students–that is to think that it’s glamorous to fight illiteracy in Tanzania, but not to mentor at-risk students on the wrong side of the tracks in one’s own city. As far as I’m concerned, there are important needs both at home and abroad, and we needn’t solve one before beginning to tackle the other. The story I tell in this column is why I think that. So read the column and post your thoughts below.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should America Solve its Own Problems First?', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-21T20:57:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e584db6-fb58-521e-a128-bfd2a4b2bed0', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e584db6-fb58-521e-a128-bfd2a4b2bed0'}, {'abstract': 'Testifying at a closely followed antitrust trial, Jim Delany, the commissioner of the Big Ten, said the business of college sports places undue demands on college athletes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/sports/ncaabasketball/big-ten-commissioner-testifies-for-a-simpler-time.html', 'snippet': 'Testifying at a closely followed antitrust trial, Jim Delany, the commissioner of the Big Ten, said the business of college sports places undue demands on college athletes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OAKLAND, Calif. — Mark Emmert, the N.C.A.A. president, drew a crowd to federal court here last week, as journalists and lawyers packed a courtroom and a second overflow room to witness his defense of amateur college sports. When Emmert finished his testimony early Friday, much of the crowd was gone. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/sports/22ncaa-1/22ncaa-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 835, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/sports/22ncaa-1/22ncaa-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 835}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/sports/22ncaa-1/22ncaa-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, '