cial)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fitzsimmons, Emma G', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wakefield (Bronx, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Porterfield, Keshon (Murder Victim)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-22T15:52:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Correal and Emma G. Fitzsimmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Correal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Fitzsimmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c94014e-37ba-5f9e-aaf9-e85243c7cc87', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c94014e-37ba-5f9e-aaf9-e85243c7cc87'}, {'abstract': 'Critics of monarchy say it is an antiquated institution too extravagant for cash-strapped countries to maintain, writes JAKE FLANAGIN. Yet many people throughout the world are reluctant to let it go.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/are-monarchs-worth-the-money/', 'snippet': 'Critics of monarchy say it is an antiquated institution too extravagant for cash-strapped countries to maintain, writes JAKE FLANAGIN. Yet many people throughout the world are reluctant to let it go.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Spain’s King Juan Carlos abdicated his throne in June, he received resounding praise for his leadership through the country’s troubled transition to democracy in the 20th century. But many critics in constitutional monarchies throughout the world continue to ask the question: Is the tradition of royalty too luxurious and costly to maintain?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are Royals Worth the Money?', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-22T15:58:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff33a420-170e-5fdf-bbd3-d92947ca8807', 'word_count': 829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff33a420-170e-5fdf-bbd3-d92947ca8807'}, {'abstract': 'Carlos Mare has spent the past three decades transforming graffiti by sculpting and welding stainless steel into art for exhibition in galleries here and abroad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/23/nyregion/mastering-the-art-of-three-dimensional-graffiti.html', 'snippet': 'Carlos Mare has spent the past three decades transforming graffiti by sculpting and welding stainless steel into art for exhibition in galleries here and abroad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Clanging metal, whirring drills and strains of Chet Baker wafted through the air, nine stories above the busy intersection of St. Nicholas Avenue and West 155th Street in Harlem. Carlos Mare sweated and huffed as he bent a stainless steel arrow over his knee. He tucked it into a mirror-shiny tangle of curves and lines that hung from a wall, stepped back and waved his arms, as if he were conducting an orchestra.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/23/nyregion/23side/23side-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/23/nyregion/23side/23side-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption':