nal', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Theodore Schleifer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Theodore', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schleifer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17a8da71-45d3-5aef-9a92-aef6fb6fa794', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17a8da71-45d3-5aef-9a92-aef6fb6fa794'}, {'abstract': 'Mexico’s politicians are pushing through unpopular legislation, while its people are glued to the World Cup.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/23/opinion/fooling-mexican-fans.html', 'snippet': 'Mexico’s politicians are pushing through unpopular legislation, while its people are glued to the World Cup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The day before the Mexican soccer team’s thrilling underdog tie with the World Cup favorite, Brazil, last week, the lead editorial of the news site SinEmbargo was titled, “Ready for your Clamato and Gatorade?” — common hangover remedies. “In about three weeks, when you wake from your World Cup dreams,” the editors wrote, “remember that when the soccer fest began, the country was on the verge of monumental decisions. If upon waking, you realize that the country’s energy reserves have been cheaply sold off or whatever else, don’t bother protesting because this is a chronicle foretold.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/23/opinion/0623OPEDselman/0623OPEDselman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 650, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/23/opinion/0623OPEDselman/0623OPEDselman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 650}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/23/opin