an unexpected — and rather meta — performance. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Most Rare Vision: Shakespeare in China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Most Rare Vision: Shakespeare in China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qin, Amy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Actors' Gang", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "A Midsummer Night's Dream (Play)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-22T22:18:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Qin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Qin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3e6b237-23fa-5391-9104-5837253f2406', 'word_count': 936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3e6b237-23fa-5391-9104-5837253f2406'}, {'abstract': 'A 24-year-old California man died after attending opening night of the Electric Daisy Carnival in Las Vegas on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/23/arts/music/man-dies-in-las-vegas-at-electric-daisy-carnival.html', 'snippet': 'A 24-year-old California man died after attending opening night of the Electric Daisy Carnival in Las Vegas on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 24-year-old California man died after attending opening night of the Electric Daisy Carnival in Las Vegas on Friday. The Las Vegas Sun reported that the man, Montgomery Tsang of San Leandro, collapsed in the parking lot of the Las Vegas Motor Speedway, where the festival was being held. He was taken to a hospital, where he died early Saturday morning. No cause of death had been determined as of Sunday afternoon, Jennifer Jacobs, senior coroner investigator at the Clark County Coroner’s Office, said. The festival’s organizers, Insomniac Events, estimate that approximately 134,000 people attended on Friday. About 250 medical calls were made that night, five of which resulted in trips to the hospital, according to the Las Vegas police. A spokeswoman for Insomniac, Jennifer Forkish, said in a statement on Saturday that “one death is one too many,” and asked festivalgoers to help keep one another safe on the remaining two nights. Saturday’s was not the first death tied to an Electric Daisy event. In 2010, a 15-year-old girl died of a drug overdose at the festival in Los Angeles. The next year, a 19-year-old man died at an Electric Daisy event in Dallas, and in 2012 two people died off site after attending the festival in Las Vegas.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Man Dies in Las Vegas at Electric Daisy Carnival', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Man Dies in Las Vegas at Electric Daisy Carnival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsang, Montgomery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-22T22:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba3cfb8a-7e83-5e9b-a34c-fab4a5deacdb', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba3cfb8a-7e83-5e9b-a34c-fab4a5deacdb'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (13) THE ESCAPE ARTIST “Everyone deserves a defense,” says Will Burton (David Tennant, above), who is London’s top-ranked junior barrister, according to a recent poll, and well up the ladder toward Queen’s Counsel because of his ability to win any case. But his invincible legal acrobatics backfire when a handshake denied sends his latest client, the reclusive bird lover and psychopath Liam Foyle (Toby Kebbell), into a rage — leaving Will, his wife (Ashley Jensen) and their son (Gus Barry) the targets of revenge. Enter Will’s nemesis, Maggie Gardner (Sophie Okonedo), frothing to overtake her rival. David Wolstencroft, the mastermind behind the BBC spy series “MI-5,” wrote this two-part “Masterpiece Mystery!” thriller, shown here in its entirety. (Part 2 will be broadcast on Channel 49 at 9.) “For the Tennant completist, it’s mandatory viewing,” Mike Hale wrote in The New York Times.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-22T22:28:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/066451b3-e27c-5dba-83dd-1378adbe8902', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/066451b3-e27c-5dba-83dd-1378adbe8902'}, {'abstract': 'Philosophy’s project to more fully represent the diversity of human history and culture faces serious challenges.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/22/converging-on-inclusiveness/', 'snippet': 'Philosophy’s project to more fully represent the diversity of human history and culture faces serious challenges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like many institutions that have become more concerned with equality in the past few decades, academic philosophy today aims to be more inclusive. In general, university departments are now striving to consider the experiences and concerns of a broader range of people than have traditionally played the social and professional role of the philosopher. This makes sense. In an increasingly global intellectual landscape, the removal of barriers to entry for previously excluded groups of people and schools of thought is productive and fair.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-thestone/opinionator-pog-thestone-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-thestone/opinionator-pog-thestone-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-thestone/opinionator-pog-thestone-jumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-thestone/opinionator-pog-thestone-superJumb