s', 'value': 'Jensen, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Takata Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Honda Motor Co Ltd', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mazda Motor Corp', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler Group LLC', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bayerische Motorenwerke AG', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-23T10:35:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi and Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdc92053-71e4-5ade-9611-e188bcbe4314', 'word_count': 1142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdc92053-71e4-5ade-9611-e188bcbe4314'}, {'abstract': 'Opinions on a rigged market, Syrian rebels, safety in the skies, the I.R.S. curiosity and Irish recovery.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/23/morning-views-20/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on a rigged market, Syrian rebels, safety in the skies, the I.R.S. curiosity and Irish recovery.', 'lead_paragraph': '“There’s no escaping the conclusion that the stock market is not a level playing field where all investors, large and small, have an equal shot at a fair deal,” writes The New York Times editorial board. “The Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations recently held a hearing on “maker-taker” pricing in which stock exchanges pay rebates to brokers for sending them buy-and-sell orders. The practice has been around for years, as a growing number of exchanges — there are now 11 public exchanges in the United States — have battled for business. What is new is the compelling evidence that the rebates are corrupting.” Brokers are technically supposed to find the best stock prices for their clients, but new research shows that they are “routinely sending orders to venues that paid the highest rebates.” The board calls on financial regulators to monitor executions, for the good of the American economy and maintaining fair competition: “Securities regulators clearly need to better enforce the best execution requirements on brokers, combined with better disclosure on brokers’ routing decisions and the rebates they earn. If Congress won’t provide more resources for enforcement, rebates need either to be passed along to the customer or eliminated altogether.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-23T10:42:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90369602-fe3b-55ab-b9dc-6db04c3bed14', 'word_count': 726, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90369602-fe3b-55ab-b9dc-6db04c3bed14'}, {'abstract': 'Milan', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2014/06/23/fashion/