or': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Castlight Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health Care Cost Institute', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PricewaterhouseCoopers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-24T11:59:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/99d93517-86e2-54a8-9f6f-afe442c61860', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99d93517-86e2-54a8-9f6f-afe442c61860'}, {'abstract': 'Long, convoluted sentences are a disservice to harried readers. We don’t have to use See-Spot-run syntax, but we should do the hard work of ironing out our sentences so readers don’t have to.', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/24/tangled-passages-9/', 'snippet': 'Long, convoluted sentences are a disservice to harried readers. We don’t have to use See-Spot-run syntax, but we should do the hard work of ironing out our sentences so readers don’t have to.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As we’ve often noted, long, convoluted sentences are a disservice to harried readers, who may be scanning the paper on the subway or checking their phone in the checkout line. We don’t have to use See-Spot-run syntax, but we should do the hard work of ironing out our sentences so readers don’t have to.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tangled Passages', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-24T12:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b4015d6-42ec-5050-b6f2-5f61473347cc', 'word_count': 1430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b4015d6-42ec-5050-b6f2-5f61473347cc'}, {'abstract': 'A regulator is close to picking a corporate monitor for Credit Suisse. | The dismissal of American Apparel’s chief raises corporate-governance questions. | The Supreme Court erects new hurdles to class actions for securities fraud. | Three traders leave Steven A. Cohen’s family office.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/06/24/morning-agenda-monitor-for-credit-suisse-named/', 'snippet': 'A regulator is close to picking a corporate monitor for Credit Suisse. | The dismissal of American Apparel’s chief raises corporate-governance questions. | The Supreme Court erects new hurdles to class actions for securities fraud. | Three traders...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Neil Barofsky, an old foe of the 2008 bank bailouts, has managed to secure a familiar role: watchdog for one of the world’s biggest banks, Ben Protess and Jessica Silver-Greenberg write in DealBook. New York State’s financial regulator is said to be close to picking Mr. Barofsky, a former federal prosecutor, as the corporate monitor for Credit Suisse after the bank’s guilty plea to helping American clients evade taxes. Credit Suisse agreed to hire a monitor as part of a related settlement. Separately, a monitor was also installed late last year at BNP Paribas, the giant French bank suspected of violating sanctions provisions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Monitor for Credit Suisse Named', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-24T12:04:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_