ces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Akram, Fares', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rudoren, Jodi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-24T23:50:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fares Akram and Jodi Rudoren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fares', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Akram', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jodi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rudoren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61b321e5-26cf-5c38-a007-fa3eb63359b2', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61b321e5-26cf-5c38-a007-fa3eb63359b2'}, {'abstract': 'Morality aside, the name causes the team financial harm.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/opinion/redskins-is-bad-business.html', 'snippet': 'Morality aside, the name causes the team financial harm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATLANTA, Ga. — THE moral arguments against the Washington Redskins’ team name, which is considered offensive to American Indians, are by now well known, especially in the wake of last week’s decision by the United States Patent and Trademark Office to strip the team of trademark protections.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Redskins’ Is Bad Business', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Redskins’ Is Bad Business', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Redskins', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewis, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trademarks and Trade Names', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tripathi, Manish', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Native Americans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Organizational', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-24T23:54:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Lewis and Manish Tripathi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Manish', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tripathi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/12643bd7-31f6-5d4a-95ea-c916f39c2620', 'word_count': 721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12643bd7-31f6-5d4a-95ea-c916f39c2620'}, {'abstract': 'The Education Department said that it would evaluate growth in students with disabilities over time and will compare their test scores with those of students not designated with special needs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/us/shift-in-law-on-disability-and-students-shows-lapses.html', 'snippet': 'The Education Department said that it would evaluate growth in students with disabilities over time and will compare their test scores with those of students not designated with special needs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fewer than a third of states and territories now comply with federal disability law under a change announced Tuesday in the way the Department of Education evaluates how well public schools educate students with disabilities.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shift in Law on Disability and Students Shows Lapses', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'States Falter in New Policy on Disability and Students', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Special Education', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duncan, Arne', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-24T23:55:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db1e40aa-25a8-59f9-ba51-fd3311c6242e', 'word_count': 675, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db1e40aa-25a8-59f9-ba51-fd3311c6242e'}, {'abstract': 'Martin Klizan started strong against No. 1 Rafael Nadal in the first round of Wimbledon, but backed off his aggressive game plan in a four-set loss.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/sports/tennis/when-facing-rafael-nadal-at-wimbledon-a-hammer-is-required.html', 'snippet': 'Martin Klizan started strong against No. 1 Rafael Nadal in the first round of Wimbledon, but backed off his aggressive game plan in a four-set loss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — There was only one way that Martin Klizan was going to defeat Rafael Nadal on Center Court at Wimbledon on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Nadal Is the Opponent at Wimbledon, a Hammer Is Required', 'kicker': 'Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klizan, Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T00:05:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': "By Craig O'Shannessy", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Shannessy", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36959fe1-7aca-5441-97eb-e374d64621e6', 'word_count': 670, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36959fe1-7aca-5441-97eb-e374d64621e6'}, {'abstract': 'How can the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria tell whether a person is a Sunni or a Shiite? From accounts, it seems they often ask a list of questions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/world/middleeast/questioning-helps-rebels-tell-sunnis-from-shiites.html', 'snippet': 'How can the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria tell whether a person is a Sunni or a Shiite? From accounts, it seems they often ask a list of questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — Whether a person is a Shiite or a Sunni Muslim in Iraq can now be, quite literally, a matter of life and death.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Questions Rebels Use to Tell Sunni From Shiite', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Questions Rebels Use to Tell Sunni From Shiite', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shiite Muslims', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubin, Alissa Johannsen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sunni Muslims', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T00:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alissa J. Rubin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alissa', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Rubin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f04ab14-d50e-5ac7-a9d8-2be974b8d1a2', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f04ab14-d50e-5ac7-a9d8-2be974b8d1a2'}, {'abstract': 'A proposal to use money that provides clean water in New York on a new bridge across the Hudson needs more scrutiny.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/opinion/the-tappan-zee-bridge-loan.html', 'snippet': 'A proposal to use money that provides clean water in New York on a new bridge across the Hudson needs more scrutiny.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it rains hard in New York, outdated municipal waste treatment plants often fail, sending untreated sewage into state waterways. Upgrading old plants and building new ones has long depended heavily on the federal Clean Water State Revolving Fund. It is capitalized with state and federal money, makes loans to needy municipalities and is constantly under pressure from budget-conscious legislators on Capitol Hill.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tappan Zee Bridge Loan', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Tappan Zee Bridge Loan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Facilities Corp (NYS)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Clean Water State Revolving Fund (CWSRF)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tappan Zee Bridge', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers and Sewage', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T00:17:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/323cb641-f364-54d0-9196-3ae7ef9a3abd', 'word_count': 331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/323cb641-f364-54d0-9196-3ae7ef9a3abd'}, {'abstract': 'Small labels contend that consolidation by the major record companies has left them squeezed in negotiations with the online music services that now account for a majority of their revenue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/business/media/small-music-labels-see-youtube-battle-as-part-of-war-for-revenue.html', 'snippet': 'Small labels contend that consolidation by the major record companies has left them squeezed in negotiations with the online music services that now account for a majority of their revenue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The booze flowed freely in New York last week at the Libera Awards, a three-year-old alternative Grammys for the world of independent music, as awards went to prestige indie acts like Arcade Fire and Arctic Monkeys and executives at small labels congratulated one another about what a great year it was for their music.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/25/business/25INDIE/25INDIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 442, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/25/business/25INDIE/25INDIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 442}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/25/business/25INDIE/25INDIE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/25/business/25INDIE/25INDIE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1430, 'width': 1941, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/25/business/25INDIE/25INDIE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/25/business/25INDIE/25INDIE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/25/business/25INDIE/25INDIE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Indie Music’s Digital Drag', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Indie Music’s Digital Drag', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audio Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Royalties', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T00:19:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1773a6cb-e8bf-5275-851f-83ce60a7d5a1', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1773a6cb-e8bf-5275-851f-83ce60a7d5a1'}, {'abstract': 'A Harvard scholar who wanted to be buried in Iran, a request that angered hard-liners there, has been cremated by his family in Boston, the Iranian news media reported Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/world/middleeast/harvard-scholars-body-cremated-after-request-for-burial-in-iran-falters.html', 'snippet': 'A Harvard scholar who wanted to be buried in Iran, a request that angered hard-liners there, has been cremated by his family in Boston, the Iranian news media reported Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Richard Nelson Frye, a renowned Harvard scholar who wanted to be buried in Iran, a request that angered Iranian hard-liners, has been cremated by his family in Boston, the Iranian news media reported Tuesday. The Iranian Students’ News Agency quoted Dr. Frye’s Iranian colleague Turaj Daryaii as saying the family had waited more than two months for official Iranian permission to bury him in Isfahan, the ancient capital of the Persian Empire, before deciding to cremate him on June 8. It was not clear what the family intended to do with his ashes. Dr. Frye, who died on March 27 at age 94, had dedicated his career to Iran’s culture and language, and he had written the burial request into his will. He was widely regarded in Iran as a friend, even among some conservatives, but his death coincided with growing resentment by Iranian hard-liners over signs of reconciliation with the United States after decades of estrangement. A month after Dr. Frye died, the leader of Friday Prayer in Isfahan denounced his burial request, calling him a spy and a robber of cultural artifacts. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran: Harvard Scholar’s Body Cremated After Request for Burial in Iran Falters', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran: Harvard Scholar’s Body Cremated After Request for Burial in Iran Falters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cremation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frye, Richard Nelson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T00:20:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/039b6b69-8338-5d6b-be8b-c5c2cc33db89', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/039b6b69-8338-5d6b-be8b-c5c2cc33db89'}, {'abstract': 'France’s highest administrative court, the Conseil d’État, ruled Tuesday that a feeding tube could legally be removed from a 38-year-old man in a persistent vegetative state, whose care has been part of a long, bitter public fight involving the country’s end-of-life legislation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/world/europe/france-court-rules-on-end-of-life-case.html', 'snippet': 'France’s highest administrative court, the Conseil d’État, ruled Tuesday that a feeding tube could legally be removed from a 38-year-old man in a persistent vegetative state, whose care has been part of a long, bitter public fight involving the co...', 'lead_paragraph': 'France’s highest administrative court, the Conseil d’État, ruled\xa0Tuesday\xa0that a feeding tube could legally be removed from a 38-year-old man in a persistent vegetative state, whose care has been part of a long, bitter public fight involving the country’s end-of-life legislation. Before a 2008 accident left the man, Vincent Lambert, a former psychiatric nurse, paralyzed and unresponsive, he had repeatedly said he did not wish to be kept alive in such a condition, according to his wife and several family members, who have sought for him to be allowed to die. Given that, the court found that the feeding tube that has kept Mr. Lambert alive could be considered “unreasonable” medical “obstinacy,” and could be legally removed. Mr. Lambert’s parents, who oppose that, have appealed to the European Court of Human Rights, and Mr. Lambert will be kept alive during the months or years of that court’s examination of the case. French law prohibits any medical acts explicitly intended to end a patient’s life, but authorizes doctors to withdraw treatment or administer lethal doses of painkillers under certain circumstances.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'France: Court Rules on End-of-Life Case', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'France: Court Rules on End-of-Life Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Life-Sustaining Support Systems, Withdrawal Of', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Court of Human Rights', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T00:25:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sayare', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sayare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0528b59a-fe27-56d9-9eb1-9be568206b5a', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0528b59a-fe27-56d9-9eb1-9be568206b5a'}, {'abstract': 'The state has watched legalization take effect in Colorado, and sees children’s access as a concern.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/us/washington-state-to-require-approval-of-packaging-for-edible-marijuana-products.html', 'snippet': 'The state has watched legalization take effect in Colorado, and sees children’s access as a concern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — Washington State will require its approval of all packaging for edible marijuana products, Gov. Jay Inslee said Tuesday, a pre-emptory move to ban cartoons, toy images and any other labels that might appeal to children.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/25/us/MARIJUANA/MARIJUANA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/25/us/MARIJUANA/MARIJUANA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image