Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citizen of the State of New York', 'kicker': 'Room for Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T10:45:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccdb2aea-3291-5ab9-abf6-14f5ae29420c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccdb2aea-3291-5ab9-abf6-14f5ae29420c'}, {'abstract': 'Opinions on a lending bank, American hypocrisy, phone hacking, early parole and militarized police.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/morning-views-22/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on a lending bank, American hypocrisy, phone hacking, early parole and militarized police.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Some Tea Party members have decided that the United States must shut down the Export-Import Bank of the United States,” a federal agency that helps American businesses sell their goods abroad, writes The New York Times’s editorial board. “The bank provides loans and loan guarantees to foreign businesses to help them buy American products and services.” The board is critical in particular of the efforts of Representative Jeb Hensarling, Republican of Texas, who is “leading the charge” against the bank and appears oblivious to the fact that “if the United States dismantled the bank unilaterally, as some lawmakers are advocating, American companies could lose billions of dollars in overseas orders and decide to move their operations to other countries that provide generous export financing.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T10:46:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name':