19bc-5886-a703-d2b6dce397b4'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/classical-playlist-verdi-schubert-michael-gordon-and-more/', 'snippet': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VERDI: ‘Don Carlo’Jon Vickers, Gré Brouwenstijn, Tito Gobbi and other singers.The Covent Garden Orchestra and Opera Chorus, Carlo Maria Giulini, conductor(Andromeda; three CDs)This live 1958 recording of Verdi’s “Don Carlo” from the Royal Opera in London, though not an ideal performance, is great to have. Of particular interest is Jon Vickers in the title role. Here, Vickers is early in his career before it became clear that he would be the great dramatic tenor of his generation, bringing a powerhouse voice and expressive intensity to roles like Wagner’s Tristan and Verdi’s Otello. This is Vickers in a more youthful, lyric mode, but still singing with chilling power and ardency, despite occasional moments of strain in his high range. The cast boasts Gré Brouwenstijn as Elisabetta, Tito Gobbi as Rodrigo, Fedora Barbieri as Eboli, and Boris Christoff as Filippo II. The great Carlo Maria Giulini conducts a majestic account of the score. The performance comes through cleanly on this remastered recording. (Anthony Tommasini)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/classical-vickers/classical-vickers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/classical-vickers/classical-vickers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/classical-vickers/classical-vickers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumb