ds': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T20:52:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Rojas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rojas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/594d1dc8-46e3-52e4-8a4c-bd4dfdcc90c3', 'word_count': 928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/594d1dc8-46e3-52e4-8a4c-bd4dfdcc90c3'}, {'abstract': 'Designers showed shirt-and-neckwear combinations that utilized the same humorous pattern.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/25/marc-jacobs-ferragamo-z-zegna-milan-fashion-week-mens-wear-trends/', 'snippet': 'Designers showed shirt-and-neckwear combinations that utilized the same humorous pattern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are so many competing guides, tutorials and rules out there for creating the right shirt-and-tie combination that it all seems a bit arbitrary and overcomplicated. Judging from what we’ve seen at the spring/summer 2015 Milan and Pitti Uomo shows, there might a be a less confusing (and more cheeky) approach to try: shirts and ties that implement the same humorous print. Massimiliano Giornetti, the creative director of Salvatore Ferragamo, paired a tie with a matching shirt emblazoned with grazing giraffes. Marc Jacobs, whose collection featured cool interpretations of pastels and flamingo imagery, showed a shirt and tie, both in a pink fern print, under a dusty rose checked suit that somehow still maintained an edge. The Z Zegna show, which was at Pitti Uomo in Florence this season, displayed a smart balance of athletic wear and fine Italian tailoring. One example was a pentagonal polka dot shirt with an elasticated hem, worn with a matching tie and trainers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag