_date': '2014-06-25T22:23:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Tortorello', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tortorello', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c0d55e4-ba2b-513c-bb66-94dfaaccee63', 'word_count': 2239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c0d55e4-ba2b-513c-bb66-94dfaaccee63'}, {'abstract': 'The actress’s handmade stone-encrusted renovation may take the rest of her life to complete. Gaudí would be impressed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/garden/melissa-leos-theater-in-the-round.html', 'snippet': 'The actress’s handmade stone-encrusted renovation may take the rest of her life to complete. Gaudí would be impressed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STONE RIDGE, N.Y. — From the road, Melissa Leo’s 200-year-old farmhouse would seem to be standard issue for Ulster County, with all the usual trappings: on the porch, a hammock, some wind chimes, a few director’s chairs, an armless mannequin wearing a flowery dress and a baseball cap, her arms strewed about her feet; up above, an enormous peace sign (Woodstock is less than 20 miles away).', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Melissa Leo’s Theater in the Round', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theater in the Round', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, Penelope', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leo, Melissa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Catskills (NYS Area)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T22:23:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Penelope Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Penelope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5443ab3-db07-5f74-88f6-b67f94036c56', 'word_count': 2148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5443ab3-db07-5f74-88f6-b67f94036c56'}, {'abstract': 'ESPN’s World Cup coverage has brought attention to the show “Men in Blazers,” hosted by two Englishmen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/arts/television/men-in-blazers-a-whimsical-soccer-show-on-espn.html', 'snippet': 'ESPN’s World Cup coverage has brought attention to the show “Men in Blazers,” hosted by two Englishmen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve been watching ESPN’s nightly World Cup wrap-up show, you have probably seen them — not beneath the swaying palm trees on the Rio de Janeiro beachfront set with the rest of the on-air talent, but squished together in an eight-by-five-foot room. Two scruffy, balding Britons, clutching a miniature banana as a make-believe microphone, riffing on the day’s games. Maybe you’ve even heard them describe the Brazilian star Neymar as “a Japanese manga character come to life,” or the Portuguese forward Cristiano Ronaldo as “a petulant show pony.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/26BLAZERSjp/26BLAZERSjp-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/26BLAZERSjp/26BLAZERSjp-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/26BLAZERSjp/26BLAZERSjp-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/26BLAZERSjp/26BLAZERSjp-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 1276, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/26BLAZERSjp/26BLAZERSjp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/26BLAZERSjp/26BLAZERSjp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/arts/26BLAZERSjp/26BLAZERSjp-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At World Cup, Two Unknowns Are Surprise Stars', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At World Cup, Two Unknowns Are Surprise Stars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Men in Blazers (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ESPN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2014 (Soccer)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T22:23:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Mahler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0d847ec-08c6-57ab-9fc8-eddbe4356fbc', 'word_count': 992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0d847ec-08c6-57ab-9fc8-eddbe4356fbc'}, {'abstract': 'A paper screen by David Weeks offers many uses and limitless dimensions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/garden/stretching-to-infinity-and-beyond.html', 'snippet': 'A paper screen by David Weeks offers many uses and limitless dimensions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since 1996, the New York designer David Weeks has been turning out furniture, lighting, toys and even wallpaper with gorilla heads. His latest project, the Infinity Screen, is a series of interlocking four-by-six-inch paper modules that can grow to any dimension. The piece came about when the W Hotel in Fort Lauderdale, Fla., commissioned Mr. Weeks to design a light installation that required no electricity. He used die-cut paper coated in shiny Mylar to create a fixture that resembled a chandelier. When the project was done, he decided to explore other uses for the technique. “It was an exercise in form,” he said. The panels ($250 a square yard) are made of card stock paper and can be used as a room divider or window covering. A stainless-steel mounting bracket is $150 per 36-inch length. Mr. Weeks will produce the screen in other materials or in printed versions for custom projects. Information: 212-966-3433 or davidweeksstudio.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRGOODS1/26CURR5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 304, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRGOODS1/26CURR5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 304}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRGOODS1/26CURR5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 518, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRGOODS1/26CURR5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1037, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRGOODS1/26CURR5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRGOODS1/26CURR5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRGOODS1/26CURR5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stretching to Infinity and Beyond', 'kicker': 'Currents: Goods', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stretching to Infinity and Beyond', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirst, Arlene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weeks, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T22:23:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3f56fb9-ae1a-5ab5-9de9-6918eb44b2c0', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3f56fb9-ae1a-5ab5-9de9-6918eb44b2c0'}, {'abstract': '“Cape Cod Modern: Midcentury Architecture and Community on the Outer Cape” tells of a cache of remarkable buildings and their brainy bohemian occupants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/garden/rustic-raw-and-thoroughly-modern.html', 'snippet': '“Cape Cod Modern: Midcentury Architecture and Community on the Outer Cape” tells of a cache of remarkable buildings and their brainy bohemian occupants.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Cape Cod Modern: Midcentury Architecture and Community on the Outer Cape,” by Peter McMahon and Christine Cipriani (Metropolis Books, $33), reveals one of the East Coast’s best-kept architectural secrets: an enclave of disarmingly unpretentious houses, many inspired by chicken coops and oyster houses. Often made with scavenged wood mixed with raw lumber straight from the yard, the buildings were designed by Marcel Breuer, Eero Saarinen, Serge Chermayeff, Maurice Smith and serious amateurs. In this rich cultural setting, left-leaning figures including Mary McCarthy, Edmund Wilson, Dwight Macdonald and Noam Chomsky dined on plywood tables, bathed nude in brisk waters on principle and exercised elastic morals among the unpainted two-by-fours. It was a summer camp for consenting, distinctly un-Puritanical intellectuals. And the party needn’t be over. Many of the houses are in the Cape Cod National Seashore conservation park, with some recently restored for summer rental and for artists in the off-season.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRBOOKS/26CURR3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 746, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRBOOKS/26CURR3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 746}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRBOOKS/26CURR3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 824, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRBOOKS/26CURR3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1489, 'width': 1197, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRBOOKS/26CURRBOOKS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRBOOKS/26CURRBOOKS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/garden/26CURRBOOKS/26CURRBOOKS-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rustic, Raw and Thoroughly Modern', 'kicker': 'Currents: Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rustic, Raw and Thoroughly Modern', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cape Cod National Seashore', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McMahon, Peter (1957- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cipriani, Christine', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Cape Cod Modern: Midcentury Architecture and Community on the Outer Cape (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-25T22:23:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Giovannini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giovannini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b269ffc0-541e-5646-8ec1-d9a8c5994a5b', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b269ffc0-541e-5646-8ec1-d9a8c5994a5b'}, {'abstract': 'Niche Modern’s Crystalline Collection includes four blown-glass pendant lamps in eight hues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/garden/a-burst-of-vivid-colors.html', 'snippet': 'Niche Modern’s Crystalline Collection includes four blown-glass pendant lamps in eight hues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Niche Modern’s Crystalline Collection of blown-glass pendant lamps includes four shapes that vaguely allude to molecular structures. “Deep down in the core of my being, I love order and mathematics,” said Jeremy Pyles, who founded the company with his wife, Mary Welch. But the main reason that the group burst onto the scene in a flash of eight mostly vivid colors, including a blue inspired by the palette of the Hotel Condesa in Mexico City, had to do with their bulbs. Niche Modern’s existing lamps offered few options for low-energy bulbs, or for the 240-volt varieties used overseas, where the company has been brightening a growing number of hotels and restaurants. The Crystalline series will accommodate both models of the stylish Plumen compact fluorescent light bulb, as well as Niche Modern’s specialty incandescent bulb. The collection is available through July 15 at Lumens.com and following that through Niche Modern. From $550. Information: nichemodern.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia':