hangs from his neck with a nylon cord. But the young man is barely suppressing a grin, and the tableau was staged as part of a university graduation celebration.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/world/26sino-harbin/26sino-harbin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 412, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/26/world/26sino-harbin/26sino-harbin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 412}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/world/26sino-harbin/26sino-harbin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/world/26sino-harbin/26sino-harbin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1408, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/world/26sino-harbin/26sino-harbin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/26/world/26sino-harbin/26sino-harbin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/26/world/26sino-harbin/26sino-harbin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Graduates’ Red Guard Photos Cast Doubt on What They Learned', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cultural Revolution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bo Xilai', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mao Zedong', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beijing News, The', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'China Daily', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sina Weibo', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chongqing (China)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harbin (China)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T10:15:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kiki Zhao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kiki', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zhao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64e99056-686e-5ab1-8c26-9529afd53af0', 'word_count': 573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64e99056-686e-5ab1-8c26-9529afd53af0'}, {'abstract': 'Opinions on cellphone privacy, cheap solar, legalizing marijuana and uncounted immigrants.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/morning-views-23/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on cellphone privacy, cheap solar, legalizing marijuana and uncounted immigrants.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The nine justices of the Supreme Court — whose average age is 68 — often admit to being unfamiliar with modern technology, if not befuddled by it,” writes The New York Times editorial board. “Yet in a gratifyingly sweeping ruling on Wednesday, the court\xa0embraced\xa0a central reality of the digital age and protected cellphones from being searched without a warrant during an arrest, except in rare circumstances.” The board is wholly supportive of the ruling. Despite government arguments to the contrary, “the expectation of privacy in a phone’s contents\xa0outweighs\xa0the immediate needs of law enforcement.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T10:44:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d420ea50-7a98-510c-b9c9-116ffed71bf6', 'word_count': 552, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d420ea50-7a98-510c-b9c9-116ffed71bf6'}, {'abstract': 'Google’s software will soon reach ever further into our lives, sitting on just about every other device you encounter — and the company’s far-reaching ambitions may now stand as its biggest hurdle.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/daily-report-is-googles-new-technology-more-creepy-than-helpful/', 'snippet': 'Google’s software will soon reach ever further into our lives, sitting on just about every other device you encounter — and the company’s far-reaching ambitions may now stand as its biggest hurdle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One way to think of Google is as an extremely helpful, all-knowing, hyper-intelligent executive assistant. Already, it can remind you about your flight, open up your boarding pass when you get to the airport and offer you driving directions to your hotel when you land.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report