: None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de4c5ddd-5890-5121-921d-6bd41db8e859', 'word_count': 261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de4c5ddd-5890-5121-921d-6bd41db8e859'}, {'abstract': 'And it’s not just humans. Monkeys, too, seem to believe in the “hot hand” beloved of gamblers and basketball players, scientists say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/science/the-science-of-hot-hand.html', 'snippet': 'And it’s not just humans. Monkeys, too, seem to believe in the “hot hand” beloved of gamblers and basketball players, scientists say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From time to time, athletes get on a streak. Suddenly, the basketball goes through the net every time, or a batter gets a hit in every game. This blissful condition is often known as the hot hand, and players have come to believe it is real — so much so that they have made it a part of their strategy for winning games.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/01/science/01zimmer/01zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/07/01/science/01zimmer/01zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/01/science/01zimmer/01zimmer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 625, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/01/science/01zimmer/01zimmer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1198, 'width': 1962, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'