ne, 'print_headline': 'Marine Life: Disco Clam’s Secret: Mirror Balls on Its Lip', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clams', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Journal of the Royal Society Interface', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T20:27:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4c8b6c3-d1c7-5163-8666-f4a5b5deb0ce', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4c8b6c3-d1c7-5163-8666-f4a5b5deb0ce'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans hailed the decision on recess appointments as a repudiation of what they called President Obama’s abuse of his constitutional power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/us/politics/justices-decision-opens-fresh-debate-on-limits-of-presidential-power.html', 'snippet': 'Republicans hailed the decision on recess appointments as a repudiation of what they called President Obama’s abuse of his constitutional power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Thursday’s decision by the Supreme Court to curb President Obama’s ability to make recess appointments opened a new debate in the nation’s capital about the proper limits of presidential power in an era of intense partisan gridlock.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Decision by Justices Opens a New Debate on the Limits of Presidential Power', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Decision by Justices Opens a New Debate on the Limits of Presidential Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Orders and Memorandums', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T20:30:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b74d890-75cb-5e9e-9893-1a2886f9f8f2', 'word_count': 971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b74d890-75cb-5e9e-9893-1a2886f9f8f2'}, {'abstract': 'A novel in dialogue between a man and a series of people he has kidnapped and chained up in a decommissioned military base.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/dave-eggerss-your-fathers-where-are-they-and-the-prophets-do-they-live-forever.html', 'snippet': 'A novel in dialogue between a man and a series of people he has kidnapped and chained up in a decommissioned military base.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Political novels are full of pitfalls, particularly for a novelist with strong political leanings. Writers may craft their world however they like, so they need not deal with the complexities of reality if it inconveniences their narrative. Ayn Rand’s philosophy can work in “Atlas Shrugged,” for example, because it’s a fantasyland of her creation. A better approach can be found in “The Brothers Karamazov,” in which Dostoyevsky makes his ideological opponents his most articulate characters. Great writers force themselves to confront the challenges the world can throw against what they believe. The poet Tom Sleigh explains this as “the difference between exploring a political emotion, say, rather than a political conviction.” Whereas a political emotion is concerned with the lived experience of how politics impact people’s lives, a political conviction is primarily didactic — it “weaves no web, traps no chaotically buzzing flies — it’s hygienic, and easily put aside when the moment of outrage or conversational animus has passed.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Klay/0629-bks-Klay-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 706, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Klay/0629-bks-Klay-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 706}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Klay/0629-bks-Klay-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 871, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Klay/0629-bks-Klay-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1760, 'width': 1496, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Klay/0629-bks-Klay-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Klay/0629-bks-Klay-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Klay/0629-bks-Klay-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Troubled Inquisitor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Troubled Inquisitor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Your Fathers, Where Are They? And the Prophets, Do They Live Forever? (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eggers, Dave', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T20:32:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Klay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c39fe65-9fda-50d9-b88b-8e32d6c23289', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c39fe65-9fda-50d9-b88b-8e32d6c23289'}, {'abstract': 'For six years, Alice Goffman saw America’s penal system through the eyes of young black men in Philadelphia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/alice-goffmans-on-the-run.html', 'snippet': 'For six years, Alice Goffman saw America’s penal system through the eyes of young black men in Philadelphia.', 'lead_paragraph': '“On the Run” is, first and foremost, a remarkable feat of reporting. Its author, Alice Goffman, a young sociologist, had an ethnography assignment for an undergraduate class at the University of Pennsylvania, and she, the daughter of the renowned sociologist Erving Goffman (1922-82), didn’t take it lightly. She hung out with an older African-American food service worker at the university, and one thing led to another. Before long, she had moved into an apartment in a poor, largely black neighborhood in Philadelphia, her housemate a young man whose family lived down the block. Goffman became such a part of the fabric of the community that she was harassed by the police, witnessed someone getting pistol-whipped, was even set up on a blind date. And all the while she was furiously taking notes, trying to make sense of what at first glance appeared to be utter chaos going on around her. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KOTLOWITZ/22KOTLOWITZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 788, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KOTLOWITZ/22KOTLOWITZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 788}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KOTLOWITZ/22KOTLOWITZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 780, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KOTLOWITZ/22KOTLOWITZ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1959, 'width': 1491, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KOTLOWITZ/22KOTLOWITZ-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KOTLOWITZ/22KOTLOWITZ-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KOTLOWITZ/22KOTLOWITZ-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Deep Cover: Alice Goffman’s ‘On the Run’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deep Cover', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kotlowitz, Alex', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goffman, Alice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T20:33:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Kotlowitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kotlowitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3970680a-b877-5962-824c-973802119b8a', 'word_count': 1675, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3970680a-b877-5962-824c-973802119b8a'}, {'abstract': 'The bloated democracies of the West need to become as lean as their autocratic competitors, this manifesto urges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/the-fourth-revolution-by-john-micklethwait-and-adrian-wooldridge.html', 'snippet': 'The bloated democracies of the West need to become as lean as their autocratic competitors, this manifesto urges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Somewhere along the long, bumpy road of human evolution — between the emergence of Davos Man and the TEDification of everything — a new breed of journalist emerged, specially adapted to the corporatized landscape of the modern media biome. Call him Aspen Man, after his proclivity for Ideas. (Aspen Woman exists, too, but the breed skews male.) Like all journalists, Aspen Man feeds upon stories, studies and statistics, but his unique genius lies in his ability to transform the resulting partly digested mix into brain candy for well-heeled aficionados of Malcolm Gladwell and Thomas Friedman. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Brooks/0629-bks-Brooks-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 542, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Brooks/0629-bks-Brooks-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 542}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Brooks/0629-bks-Brooks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 926, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Brooks/0629-bks-Brooks-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1852, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Brooks/0629-bks-Brooks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Brooks/0629-bks-Brooks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Brooks/0629-bks-Brooks-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height