Carney (“Once”) brings his latest film about musicians, “Begin Again,” to the big screen. In this video, he discusses a musical sequence featuring Keira Knightley and Mark Ruffalo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'John Carney Narrates a Scene From ‘Begin Again’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carney, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knightley, Keira', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruffalo, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T21:22:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/057f63f9-6f08-5879-883e-7015eeb59d10', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/057f63f9-6f08-5879-883e-7015eeb59d10'}, {'abstract': 'Joe Manganiello, an actor-turned-director, was inspired by his role in “Magic Mike” to make a documentary about the Dallas strip club La Bare.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/movies/la-bare-directed-by-joe-manganiello-of-magic-mike.html', 'snippet': 'Joe Manganiello, an actor-turned-director, was inspired by his role in “Magic Mike” to make a documentary about the Dallas strip club La Bare.', 'lead_paragraph': '“La Bare” takes its title from the club it chronicles, a male strip joint in Dallas. The name proves unfortunately apt for a rambling, superficial documentary that straddles the line between exposé and infomercial. Directed by the actor Joe Manganiello (who was inspired by his role in Steven Soderbergh’s “Magic Mike,” set in the world of male revues), this celebratory film argues that male stripping is more strenuous than its female counterpart: It requires a full-fledged act and an athlete’s regimen. We learn a bit about the stagecraft, such as how audience volunteers are selected and why underpants with flies are forbidden.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/LABARE/LABARE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/LABARE/LABARE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/LABARE/LABARE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/LABARE/LABARE-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/LABARE/LABARE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/LABARE/LABARE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/LABARE/LABARE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hey, Taking Off My Clothes Is Hard Work', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'La Bare', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'La Bare (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manganiello, Joe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T21:22:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Kenigsberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenigsberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1334b34d-aa03-598f-ae13-8494facc66af', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1334b34d-aa03-598f-ae13-8494facc66af'}, {'abstract': 'Boston Ballet’s first of two 50th-anniversary programs at Lincoln Center presents works by William Forsythe, José Martinez and Alexander Ekman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/arts/dance/boston-ballet-visits-lincoln-center.html', 'snippet': 'Boston Ballet’s first of two 50th-anniversary programs at Lincoln Center presents works by William Forsythe, José Martinez and Alexander Ekman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Celebrating the close of its 50th-anniversary season, Boston Ballet has come to Lincoln Center with two programs at the David H. Koch Theater. The second program, which opens on Friday, includes two masterpieces of different stages of radical modernism, Vaslav Nijinsky’s 1912 “Afternoon of a Faun” and George Balanchine’s 1972 “Symphony in Three Movements.” The first program, which opened on Wednesday, is all postmodern, all chic, all wiseguy, all full of theater games, all laden with production concepts and entirely loathsome. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27BALLET/BALLET-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27BALLET/BALLET-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27BALLET/BALLET-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27BALLET/BALLET-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27BALLET/BALLET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27BALLET/BALLET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27BALLET/BALLET-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Artifice, Historical Transcendence and Cactus Plants', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Artifice, Historical Transcendence and Cactus Plants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T21:26:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7c7efe4-c5ac-5a74-bd30-52c1f5f63204', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7c7efe4-c5ac-5a74-bd30-52c1f5f63204'}, {'abstract': 'Smith Henderson’s debut novel, “Fourth of July Creek,” follows a social worker in Montana in 1980 investigating a survivalist’s treatment of his son.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/books/fourth-of-july-creek-by-smith-henderson.html', 'snippet': 'Smith Henderson’s debut novel, “Fourth of July Creek,” follows a social worker in Montana in 1980 investigating a survivalist’s treatment of his son.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The unforgettable hero of “Fourth of July Creek” is Pete Snow, a social worker charged with rescuing troubled children in the wilds of northern Montana. At work, he is preternaturally kind and helpful, even in the face of unbearable situations. In this rugged country, way off the grid, it seems there’s no horror he hasn’t seen, from “newly suicided fathers and their wreckage” to “the mother who calls your office wondering if you could take her child, God is telling her to kill him, you better hurry.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27JPBOOKHENDERSON/27JPBOOKHENDERSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27JPBOOKHENDERSON/27JPBOOKHENDERSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/arts/27JPBOOKHENDERSON/27JPBOOKHENDERSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo'