 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let the Mass Destruction Begin', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bay, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wahlberg, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tucci, Stanley', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grammer, Kelsey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Transformers: Age of Extinction (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T21:44:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cee3e0aa-bf08-5a81-8be5-128549ba087c', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cee3e0aa-bf08-5a81-8be5-128549ba087c'}, {'abstract': 'The international music festival, which has taken place in January since 2003, is expanding to offer a day of free programming on July 27.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/globalfest-to-make-its-summer-debut/', 'snippet': 'The international music festival, which has taken place in January since 2003, is expanding to offer a day of free programming on July 27.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eleven musical acts, three film screenings, family-friendly performances and an underground bass stage will mark Globalfest’s summer debut, at Lincoln Center on July 27, organizers announced on Thursday.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Globalfest to Make Its Summer Debut', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Globalfest in the Summer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T21:56:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cc803c2-a707-5d39-8ddb-586ced7f50a8', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cc803c2-a707-5d39-8ddb-586ced7f50a8'}, {'abstract': 'ANNA ALTMAN writes the Supreme Court’s decision may mean the end of Aereo, but not, according to many, of innovation in TV.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/26/court-rules-against-aereo-but-not-against-innovation/', 'snippet': 'ANNA ALTMAN writes the Supreme Court’s decision may mean the end of Aereo, but not, according to many, of innovation in TV.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Wednesday morning, the Supreme Court handed down a 6-3 ruling that it was unlawful for Aereo, a television streaming service, to capture and stream broadcast television signals to subscribers. The court found that Aereo’s service violated the Copyright Act by not securing permission from broadcasters to enact “public performances” of their programs. This result is a largely seen as a boon to networks and a threat to start-ups, particularly those that use cloud-based technology to allow file sharing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Court Rules Against Aereo, but Not Against Innovation', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blodget, Henry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brustein, Joshua', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diller, Barry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Aereo Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-26T21:56:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Altman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5b8df48-3193-53f1-8413-f61710ad1286', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5b8df48-3193-53f1-8413-f61710ad1286'}, {'abstract': 'Advice on saying hello in an awkward situation, responding to high school graduation announcements, coping with a boyfriend’s Facebook family and the trip to the barbershop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/fashion/Say-Hi-but-Dont-Pry.html', 'snippet': 'Advice on saying hello in an awkward situation, responding to high school graduation announcements, coping with a boyfriend’s Facebook family and the trip to the barbershop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My husband and I were sitting in the waiting room of a fertility clinic, when I recognized a woman who was a friend from college 18 years ago. I hadn’t seen or spoken with her since. Would it have been O.K. to say hello to her in this situation? Could I have asked if she’s married and what she’s up to, the way I would any other friend? How about explaining that we were not there for IVF treatment but for a follow-up, since we lucked out and got pregnant on our own?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'lega