a mix of religious sects into a stable nation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — A picture of Gertrude Bell, the British diplomat and spy, still hangs on a wall in the Alwiya Club, the Baghdad clubhouse for the social elite that she established, and black-and-white photographs of her can be found in the collections of the city’s old families.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27GERTRUDE1/27GERTRUDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 423, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27GERTRUDE1/27GERTRUDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 423}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27GERTRUDE1/27GERTRUDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27GERTRUDE1/27GERTRUDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1444, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27GERTRUDE1/27GERTRUDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27GERTRUDE1/27GERTRUDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27GERTRUDE1/27GERTRUDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For British Spy in Iraq, Affection Is Strong but Legacy Is Unfulfilled', 'kicker': 'The Great War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For British Spy in Iraq, Affection Is Strong, but Legacy Is Unfulfilled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arango, Tim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Gertrude', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T00:31:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Arango', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arango', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc44c74b-d79c-53cf-88ef-f16216aadb2c', 'word_count': 1395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc44c74b-d79c-53cf-88ef-f16216aadb2c'}, {'abstract': 'A century after World War I began, German leadership in Europe is both desired and resented, an ambivalence keenly felt by both the Germans and their wary neighbors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/world/europe/in-germany-world-war-i-still-resonates.html', 'snippet': 'A century after World War I began, German leadership in Europe is both desired and resented, an ambivalence keenly felt by both the Germans and their wary neighbors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KIEL, Germany — On June 28, 1914, Kaiser Wilhelm II was preparing to indulge in a favorite pastime: racing his yacht Meteor at a regatta that is still held each year in this seafaring stronghold. When he learned of the assassination in Sarajevo of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, the heir to the Hapsburg Empire, he hastened to Berlin. But he quickly returned to this Baltic Sea port, on which he lavished so much money and time in his frantic race to match or outdo British naval superiority.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/world/GERMANY/GERMANY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/03/world/GERMANY/GERMANY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/world/GERMANY/GERMANY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/world/GERMANY/GERMANY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/world/GERMANY/GERMANY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/03/world/GERMANY/GERMANY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/world/GERMANY/GERMANY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Militarism and Humiliation Cast Shadow on Germany', 'kicker': 'The Great War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Militarism and Humiliation Cast a Century-Long Shadow Over Germany', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilhelm II, Emperor of Germany', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kiel (Germany)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T00:32:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Smale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6765dc1c-2415-5124-8a3f-0a1976fff08c', 'word_count': 1525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6765dc1c-2415-5124-8a3f-0a1976fff08c'}, {'abstract': 'World War I destroyed kings, kaisers, czars and sultans; it demolished empires; it introduced chemical weapons; it brought millions of women into the work force.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/world/europe/world-war-i-brought-fundamental-changes-to-the-world.html', 'snippet': 'World War I destroyed kings, kaisers, czars and sultans; it demolished empires; it introduced chemical weapons; it brought millions of women into the work force.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ZONNEBEKE, Belgium — To walk the orderly rows of headstones in the elegant graveyards that hold the dead of World War I is to feel both awe and distance. With the death of the last veterans, World War I, which began 100 years ago, has moved from memory to history. But its resonance has not faded — on land and geography, people and nations, and on the causes and consequences of modern war.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The War to End All Wars? Hardly. But It Did Change Them Forever.', 'kicker': 'The Great War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Conflict That Shaped the Modern World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erlanger, Steven', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Belgium', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flanders (Belgium)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ypres (Belgium)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cemeteries', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T00:32:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e71faf75-625d-5a29-bdf8-1a61997b5c25', 'word_count': 1656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e71faf75-625d-5a29-bdf8-1a61997b5c25'}, {'abstract': 'A century after hundreds of thousands died around Ypres, their remains are still being found, and shells are still exploding.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/world/europe/belgians-share-their-land-with-world-war-i-reminders.html', 'snippet': 'A century after hundreds of thousands died around Ypres, their remains are still being found, and shells are still exploding.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YPRES, Belgium — The padlocked cage beside the driveway on the Butaye family farm near this town in western Belgium is almost full of rusting bombs again. Since January, Stijn Butaye has collected 46 mortar shells on his family’s 100 acres, World War I munitions he found among the sugar beet and potato fields, sometimes with the help of his metal detector.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Belgians Share Their Land With War’s Reminders', 'kicker': 'The Great War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daley, Suzanne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ypres (Belgium)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Belgium', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flanders (Belgium)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Verdun (France)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T00:32:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzanne Daley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/287c7642-0460-553a-b691-17ec92569952', 'word_count': 1555, 'uri': 'nyt://article/287c7642-0460-553a-b691-17ec92569952'}, {'abstract': 'Nationalist and sectarian passions continue to haunt Bosnia, which was ravaged by a civil war just two decades ago and is even now the scene of dueling efforts to define Gavrilo Princip’s legacy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/world/europe/in-sarajevo-gavrilo-princip-set-off-world-war-i.html', 'snippet': 'Nationalist and sectarian passions continue to haunt Bosnia, which was ravaged by a civil war just two decades ago and is even now the scene of dueling efforts to define Gavrilo Princip’s legacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SARAJEVO, Bosnia and Herzegovina — As the 100th anniversary approaches, the clusters of visitors have thickened at the street-corner museum in Sarajevo’s old town that stands where Gavrilo Princip claimed his place in history on June 28, 1914, firing the shots that killed Archduke Franz Ferdinand and his wife, Sophie, and setting off World War I.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Sarajevo, Divisions That Drove an Assassin Have Only Begun to Heal', 'kicker': 'The Great War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Sarajevo, Divisions That Drove Assassin Have Yet to Heal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burns, John F', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferdinand, Franz (1863-1914)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Princip, Gavrilo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sarajevo (Bosnia and Herzegovina)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bosnia and Herzegovina', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Serbia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Croatia', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T00:32:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f22f6186-a9b6-53ae-af52-696d91449a15', 'word_count': 1859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f22f6186-a9b6-53ae-af52-696d91449a15'}, {'abstract': 'Divided loyalties among the citizenry during World War I are still seen in today’s struggle with Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/world/europe/world-war-i-battle-in-ukraine-echoes-through-the-decades.html', 'snippet': 'Divided loyalties among the citizenry during World War I are still seen in today’s struggle with Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOUNT MAKIVKA, Ukraine — In a conflict that cost millions of lives, destroyed four empires and introduced Europe to the horrors of chemical weapons and total war, the battle on a pine-covered mountain in what is today western Ukraine was a modest affair. A few hundred soldiers died, and the outcome was so muddled that all sides claimed victory.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27MAKIVKA1/27MAKIVKA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27MAKIVKA1/27MAKIVKA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27MAKIVKA1/27MAKIVKA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27MAKIVKA1/27MAKIVKA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27MAKIVKA1/27MAKIVKA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27MAKIVKA1/27MAKIVKA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/world/27MAKIVKA1/27MAKIVKA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Battle in Ukraine Echoes Through the Decades', 'kicker': 'The Great War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T00:32:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Higgins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Higgins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/920a8c89-940c-5c8d-9a72-813313678dbf', 'word_count': 1742, 'uri': 'nyt://article/920a8c89-940c-5c8d-9a72-813313678dbf'}, {'abstract': 'The Germans were pushing toward Paris in 1918 when untested American troops helped stop them at the Marne River in a pivotal World War I battle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/world/europe/world-war-i-second-battle-of-the-marne.html', 'snippet': 'The Germans were pushing toward Paris in 1918 when untested American troops helped stop them at the Marne River in a pivotal World War I battle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHÂTEAU-THIERRY, France — Fifty miles to Paris. That was all that separated a hardened German Army from, perhaps, the end of the Great War. By early 1918, the Germans were rolling through northern France, and French commanders feared they were planning a decisive attack against the 