bo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28kenya2/28kenya2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28kenya2/28kenya2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28kenya2/28kenya2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kenya’s Future Clouds as Tensions Rise and Tourists Flee', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kenya’s Future Clouds as Tensions Rise and Tourists Flee', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gettleman, Jeffrey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Odinga, Raila', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kenyatta, Uhuru Muigai', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T04:05:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Gettleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gettleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7da951e7-142f-5ff8-99f7-897e784f0d3a', 'word_count': 1207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7da951e7-142f-5ff8-99f7-897e784f0d3a'}, {'abstract': 'When 200 or so young New Yorkers converge in Battery Park City on June 30 for the first day of a camp run by Asphalt Green, there is one thing they will not have in common with the manager Ms. Acosta: She never went to camp as a child.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/nyregion/niki-acosta-camp-boss-covers-all-the-bases.html', 'snippet': 'When 200 or so young New Yorkers converge in Battery Park City on June 30 for the first day of a camp run by Asphalt Green, there is one thing they will not have in common with the manager Ms. Acosta: She never went to camp as a child.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When 200 or so young New Yorkers converge in Battery Park City on June 30 for the first day of a camp run by Asphalt Green, there is one thing they will not have in common with the manager Niki Acosta: She never went to camp as a child. “I went to Y.M.C.A. programs, but it was only weekly, or sporadically,” Ms. Acosta, 28, said the other day in her office, where she was immersed in preparations. Now in her second year at the nonprofit’s downtown outpost (there is also a 5.5-acre campus on the Upper East Side), she said, she has been “gearing up for the first day of camp since September of last year.” Ms. Acosta lives in one half of a two-family house in Woodside, Queens, with a poodle named Otis.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ROUTINE2/29ROUTINE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ROUTINE2/29ROUTINE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ROUTINE2/29ROUTINE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ROUTINE2/29ROUTINE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ROUTINE2/29ROUTINE2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ROUTINE2/29ROUTINE2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ROUTINE2/29ROUTINE2-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Niki Acosta: Camp Boss Covers All the Bases', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Camp Boss Covers All the Bases', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camps and Camping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Asphalt Green', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Acosta, Niki', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T04:49:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00ffbbe8-63db-5327-8378-325c03380e9f', 'word_count': 792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00ffbbe8-63db-5327-8378-325c03380e9f'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/arts/television/whats-on-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (Starz) BLUE JASMINE (2013) Cate Blanchett, above, won an Oscar for her role as Jasmine French, a Park Avenue matron fallen on hard times after her husband, Hal (Alec Baldwin), lands in prison for bilking his clients out of millions — leaving her with little more than her pearls, white Chanel jacket, Hermès bag and monogrammed Louis Vuitton luggage. Destitute, in a manner of speaking, Jasmine flies first-class to San Francisco to begin life anew with her sister, Ginger (Sally Hawkins), whose scrappy existence and brutish men (Andrew Dice Clay and Bobby Cannavale) Jasmine can barely stomach without self-medicating with vodka and Xanax. Then she meets a diplomatic type (Peter Sarsgaard) with an appreciation for her decorating talents and designer threads, and starts to see light at the end of her terribly dark tunnel. With intimations of Ruth Madoff and Blanche DuBois, this shattered woman is “brilliantly brought to quivering life by Ms. Blanchett,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times, in Woody Allen’s “most sustained, satisfying and resonant film since ‘Match Point.’\xa0” What did Jasmine know about her husband’s unscrupulous business dealings, and when did she know it? “These questions come to haunt ‘Blue Jasmine,’ and as the past catches up with the present, they help drive this moving, sometimes funny film toward its shattering end,” Ms. Dargis added.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T05:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename