moved from the Studio 54 theater in Midtown on Thursday and arrested after disrupting a performance of the musical “Cabaret.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shia LaBeouf Arrested for Disorderly Conduct at Studio 54', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LaBeouf, Shia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Studio 54', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T05:30:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13025b69-df81-557b-9687-72a082221163', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13025b69-df81-557b-9687-72a082221163'}, {'abstract': 'The Communist Party newspaper said the practice, which has received a recent burst of publicity, has no medical value.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/peoples-daily-takes-firm-stance-against-urine-consumption/', 'snippet': 'The Communist Party newspaper said the practice, which has received a recent burst of publicity, has no medical value.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If imbibing urine is not your cup of tea, People’s Daily\xa0— the solemn voice of the Chinese Communist Party\xa0— is with you on that one. On Friday, the newspaper took time off from its usual encomiums to party leaders to warn people against drinking their own pee.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'People’s Daily Takes Firm Stance Against Urine Consumption', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Complementary Medicine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "People's Daily", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tianjin (China)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T05:31:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2311a3b-dcef-51ff-a613-cae145b7bc55', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2311a3b-dcef-51ff-a613-cae145b7bc55'}, {'abstract': 'One book introduces readers to some of the signature trials and towering jurists of the Federal District Court in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/nyregion/first-if-no-longer-foremost.html', 'snippet': 'One book introduces readers to some of the signature trials and towering jurists of the Federal District Court in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is called the nation’s Mother Court because it was created even before the United States Supreme Court, in 1789, when New York City was the nation’s capital. (In truth, though, the court for what became the Southern District of New York was first only because the opening of the federal court in New Jersey was delayed after a jurist became indisposed.) In “The Mother Court: Tales of Cases That Mattered in America’s Greatest Trial Court” (American Bar Association Publishing), James D. Zirin, a trial lawyer and former federal prosecutor, engagingly introduces readers to some of the signature trials and towering jurists of the Federal District Court in Manhattan. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 991, 'width': 1480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29BOOKSHELF/29BOOKSHELF-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'First if No Longer Foremost', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'First if No Longer Foremost', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corman, Avery', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx 