ps://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/morning-views-24/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on buffer zones, recess appointments, a response to the Iraq crisis, the Common Core, and the food industry.', 'lead_paragraph': '“In the world as Supreme Court Justice Antonin Scalia imagines it, anti-abortion advocates resemble Eleanor McCullen, a self-described ‘mother and grandmother’ who stands outside a reproductive-health clinic in Boston and attempts to gently ‘counsel’ approaching women out of getting an abortion,” writes The New York Times Editorial Board, in response to the Court’s decision to strike down a Massachusetts law that established buffer zones around abortion clinics. The editorial says the ruling “ignores what actually happens on the ground,” including “a long and sometimes violent history of protest at reproductive-health clinics.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jindal, Bobby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maliki, Nuri Kamal al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T11:10:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Altman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b676fd88-7873-5ef4-9097-17995f35bee1', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b676fd88-7873-5ef4-9097-17995f35bee1'}, {'abstract': 'As Australia prepares a new search for the missing Malaysia Airlines jet, a report notes that it could have fallen in an area up to 19 times as large as the new search zone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/28/world/asia/report-underscores-lingering-doubts-in-search-for-missing-plane.html', 'snippet': 'As Australia prepares a new search for the missing Malaysia Airlines jet, a report notes that it could have fallen in an area up to 19 times as large as the new search zone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia — As Australia prepares a yearlong search for the jet carrying Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 in an area of ocean floor the size of Croatia or West Virginia, a government report underscores the lingering uncertainty about where the plane went down, noting that it might have fallen in an area of the southern Indian Ocean up to 19 times as large as the new search area.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Report Underscores Lingering Doubts in Search for Missing Plane', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Report Underscores Lingering Doubts in Search for Missing Plane', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradsher, Keith', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Malaysia Airlines Flight 370', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aviation Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Malaysia Airlines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T11:12:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/885f3933-770d-5994-a784-20d2e99b7e72', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/885f3933-770d-5994-a784-20d2e99b7e72'}, {'abstract': 'We worked for good in Morsi’s Egypt. Must we be “disappeared” by the military?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/28/opinion/khaled-al-qazzaz-disappeared-by-egypts-military.html', 'snippet': 'We worked for good in Morsi’s Egypt. Must we be “disappeared” by the military?', 'lead_paragraph': 'TORA, Egypt — In a few days’ time, I will complete 365 days of imprisonment, more than half spent in solitary confinement and under severe limitations in the maximum-security Scorpion wing of Tora prison in Cairo. I have spent the past year thinking about what drove me to where I am today. I have also been thinking about an explanation for why politicians, human rights activists and the media have largely been silent about my case.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Is the World Silent?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Imprisoned by Egypt’s Military', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qazzaz, Khaled al-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T11:37:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Khaled Al-Qazzaz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Khaled', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Al-Qazzaz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8eabb342-4b2f-5df5-a8fa-b0facce31e86', 'word_count': 815, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8eabb342-4b2f-5df5-a8fa-b0facce31e86'}, {'abstract': 'The abrupt downfall of Wan Qingliang stands out from many other recent graft investigations because Mr. Wan still held a position of considerable power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/28/world/asia/chinas-anticorruption-campaign-moves-to-a-powerful-party-seat.html', 'snippet': 'The abrupt downfall of Wan Qingliang stands out from many other recent graft investigations because Mr. Wan still held a position of considerable power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — The Communist Party chief of Guangzhou, one of China’s most populous and wealthiest cities, has been held for investigation by the party’s anticorruption agency, state-run media announced on Friday, one day after he admonished city officials to embrace President Xi Jinping’s campaign against lax discipline.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28CHINA/28CHINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28CHINA/28CHINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28CHINA/28CHINA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28CHINA/28CHINA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1392, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28CHINA/28CHINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28CHINA/28CHINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/world/28CHINA/28CHINA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China’s Anticorruption Campaign Unseats a Powerful Party Chief in Guangzhou', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China’s Anticorruption Campaign Unseats a Powerful Party Chief in Guangzhou', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wan Qingliang', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xi Jinping', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guangzhou (China)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Commission for Discipline Inspection (Communist Party of China)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Politburo Standing Committee of the Communist Party of China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T11:41:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc03d83e-d594-52b4-9171-65167306c119', 'word_count': 600, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc03d83e-d594-52b4-9171-65167306c119'}, {'abstract': 'BNP Paribas is set to plead guilty to criminal charges and pay an $8.9 billion penalty. | Brokerage firms shunned Barclays’ trading pool on Thursday. | The federal government is auctioning off nearly 30,000 Bitcoins. | Argentina appeared to defy a judge’s order on debt payments.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/morning-agenda-8-9-billion-penalty-for-bnp/', 'snippet': 'BNP Paribas is set to plead guilty to criminal charges and pay an $8.9 billion penalty. | Brokerage firms shunned Barclays’ trading pool on Thursday. | The federal government is auctioning off nearly 30,000 Bitcoins. | Argentina appeared to defy a...', 'lead_paragraph': 'BNP TO PLEAD GUILTY AND PAY $8.9 BILLION FINE \xa0|\xa0 BNP Paribas is set to plead guilty to criminal charges and pay an $8.9 billion penalty to state and federal authorities, Ben Protess and Jessica Silver-Greenberg write in DealBook. The move would be the culmination of a widespread investigation into the French bank for transferring billions of dollars on behalf of Sudan and other countries blacklisted by the United States. The plea deal, which authorities hope to announce on Monday, will include a statement of facts that outlines how BNP, France’s largest bank, hid the names of Sudanese clients when processing transactions through its American operations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: $8.9 Billion Penalty for BNP', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T11:51:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5cbecd5-d9f2-5770-b32f-54ad4ce6050e', 'word_count': 1942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5cbecd5-d9f2-5770-b32f-54ad4ce6050e'}, {'abstract': '“Vicious,” a British series soon to be broadcast on PBS, follows two partners in a gay relationship of nearly 50 years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/arts/television/vicious-on-pbs-follows-two-gay-characters.html', 'snippet': '“Vicious,” a British series soon to be broadcast on PBS, follows two partners in a gay relationship of nearly 50 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As he descends a staircase in the opening scene of “Vicious,” a new comedy making its PBS debut on Sunday, Ian McKellen overhears his lover, played by Derek Jacobi, reacting to bad news he received on the telephone. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/arts/29STEREOTYPES1/29STEREOTYPES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/arts/29STEREOTYPES1/29STEREOTYPES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/arts/29STEREOTYPES1/29STEREOTYPES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/arts/29STEREOTYPES1/29STEREOTYPES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 