ub_date': '2014-06-27T14:35:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b057a12-3f7e-55a4-b2f6-6d577dba7bfe', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b057a12-3f7e-55a4-b2f6-6d577dba7bfe'}, {'abstract': 'A response to Thomas Edsall’s critique of the welfare system’s rightward shift.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/reform-conservatism-on-work-and-poverty/', 'snippet': 'A response to Thomas Edsall’s critique of the welfare system’s rightward shift.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Turning back to this week’s official theme, reform conservatism’s liberal critics, I want to discuss a recent column from my colleague Thomas Edsall that, without mentioning our quasi-movement by name, raises one of the sharpest objections to our program of any left-of-center piece I’ve seen. Where reform conservatives tend to argue for a welfare system and tax code organized more explicitly around work and family, Edsall’s piece makes the case that\xa0we’ve already tried something like that approach\xa0— have been trying it, in fact, since the early 1990s\xa0— and that it has worked out pretty badly, failing completely at its stated work-promoting aims while making life increasingly desperate for the very poor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reform Conservatism on Work and Poverty', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank':