mes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/letters-carters-woman-question.html', 'snippet': 'Readers react to recent reviews of “The Redeemer” and “Call to Action,” Boris Fishman’s “A Replacement Life” and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carter’s Woman Question', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: Carter’s Woman Question', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T14:53:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f5b3643-e526-5938-9610-210c629e2997', 'word_count': 849, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f5b3643-e526-5938-9610-210c629e2997'}, {'abstract': 'John Banville is one of several authors to write mystery novels under a pseudonym in recent years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/starting-from-scratch.html', 'snippet': 'John Banville is one of several authors to write mystery novels under a pseudonym in recent years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Galbraith’s “The Silkworm” is reviewed this week, and with all due respect to Galbraith, the extent of his success may be due entirely to J. K. Rowling. The mystery is the second written by the Harry Potter creator under the pseudo\xadnym. “I was yearning to go back to the beginning of a writing career in this new genre, to work without hype or expectation and to receive totally unvarnished feedback,” she wrote on her website last year after being unmasked. Oh, well.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29openbook/29openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 265, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29openbook/29openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 265}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29openbook/29openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29openbook/29openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29openbook/29openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29openbook/29openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29openbook/29openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Starting From Scratch', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Starting From Scratch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T14:53:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08f9399b-9101-5fa2-a537-5bd66763b0d0', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08f9399b-9101-5fa2-a537-5bd66763b0d0'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORTY-ONE FALSE STARTS: Essays on Artists and Writers,  by Janet Malcolm. (Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $16.) Whether she’s observing artists and writers present (David Salle, Thomas Struth) or past (Edith Wharton, the Bloomsbury group), what resonates most here is Malcolm’s cool intelligence and psychoanalytic knack for noticing. The title essay, a 1994 profile of Salle, starts over 41 times, parodying Salle’s own violent juxtaposition of images.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T14:54:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/3811c2c2-799c-545a-a4dd-eea83d69f3dd', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3811c2c2-799c-545a-a4dd-eea83d69f3dd'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SONG OF THE SHANK,  by Jeffery Renard Allen. (Graywolf, paper, $18.) Allen’s masterly novel blends the personal story of the enslaved autistic piano prodigy Thomas Wiggins with the history of the Civil War and Reconstruction.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T14:54:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/49918674-1115-5afb-b516-b94a5cd69b40', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49918674-1115-5afb-b516-b94a5cd69b40'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Mark Harris, Donna Kornhaber, Noah Isenberg and Steven Awalt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/five-came-back-by-mark-harris-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Mark Harris, Donna Kornhaber, Noah Isenberg and Steven Awalt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FIVE CAME BACK  A Story of Hollywood and the Second World War  By Mark Harris  Penguin Press, $29.95. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29SHORT/0629-bks-SHORT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29SHORT/0629-bks-SHORT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 150}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url