ruled Morocco. Orwell, who himself had earlier been a colonial British official in Burma, went on: “How long before they turn their guns in the other direction?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22COHEN/22COHEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22COHEN/22COHEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22COHEN/22COHEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 659, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22COHEN/22COHEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1318, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22COHEN/22COHEN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22COHEN/22COHEN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22COHEN/22COHEN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'L’Étranger', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'L’Étranger', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hussey, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T15:03:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mitchell Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mitchell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7682bf02-da25-5c88-b774-ca53312925eb', 'word_count': 1157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7682bf02-da25-5c88-b774-ca53312925eb'}, {'abstract': 'Meryl Gordon recounts the life of Huguette Clark, who went from Jazz Age socialite to hospital-bound recluse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/meryl-gordons-phantom-of-fifth-avenue.html', 'snippet': 'Meryl Gordon recounts the life of Huguette Clark, who went from Jazz Age socialite to hospital-bound recluse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the end, she loved watching SpongeBob and the Smurfs, bidding (by proxy) on antique French dolls and writing enormous checks to her nurse, sometimes two in one day. When she died in 2011 at 104, Huguette Clark was flush with property — apartments totaling 42 rooms on Fifth Avenue, a 23-acre estate in Santa Barbara and a 22-room mansion in New Canaan, Conn., that she had never bothered to \xadfurnish. (In 1961, her Santa Barbara caretaker quit because he was suffering from the isolation: Clark had reportedly not visited the place since 1953.) Yet despite such splendid real estate, Clark had chosen to spend the final two decades of her life in a series of modest hospital rooms attended by a small circle of extremely well-paid retainers, including her doctor, her nurse, her accountant and her lawyer.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22GREEN/22GREEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 882, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22GREEN/22GREEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 882}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22GREEN/22GREEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22GREEN/22GREEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1393, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22GREEN/22GREEN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22GREEN/22GREEN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22GREEN/22GREEN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fortune’s Daughter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fortune’s Daughter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, Penelope', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, Meryl', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Huguette M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T15:04:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Penelope Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Penelope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a1c9d27-a0df-5299-b024-18bd8844a671', 'word_count': 1382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a1c9d27-a0df-5299-b024-18bd8844a671'}, {'abstract': 'Are we building worse than we used to?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/27/these-ages-of-shoddy/', 'snippet': 'Are we building worse than we used to?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Henry Petroski, an engineer who’s also a fine writer, has a lament about the decline of good construction in today’s Times. It’s a great read; I’m trying to figure out whether I believe in his premise.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'These Ages of Shoddy', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T15:13:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71ffba14-5e51-53f7-95c4-cd003ab8d946', 'word_count': 406, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71ffba14-5e51-53f7-95c4-cd003ab8d946'}, {'abstract': 'A series of fires in a New England town exposes tensions between summer and year-round residents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/the-arsonist-by-sue-miller.html', 'snippet': 'A series of fires in a New England town exposes tensions between summer and year-round residents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the middle of the night, a jet-lagged woman, unable to sleep, goes for a walk along a country road in the New England town where her family has summered for generations. Frankie Rowley, newly returned from years of relief work in Africa, is certainly lost in the middle of her life’s journey, and in a dark night, if not precisely in a dark wood. Unsure of her place in the world, unsure whether she has a place in the world, Frankie has come to Pomeroy, N.H., for a life-reassessment disguised as a visit to her parents, who have decided to live full time in their summer house following her father’s retirement from teaching. It’s only after a car passes Frankie on the road, its headlights sweeping past in a disorienting arc, that she realizes the smell of smoke has been in the air for some time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KORELITZ/22KORELITZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KORELITZ/22KORELITZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 747}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KORELITZ/22KORELITZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 823, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KORELITZ/22KORELITZ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1915, 'width': 1539, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KORELITZ/22KORELITZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KORELITZ/22KORELITZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/22/books/review/22KORELITZ/22KORELITZ-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Neighborhood Watch', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Neighborhood Watch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Korelitz, Jean Hanff', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Sue', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T15:14:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jean Hanff Korelitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': 'Hanff', 'lastname': 'Korelitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/58c580f8-02d5-5f7b-aad6-cfedc6b150b0', 'word_count': 744, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58c580f8-02d5-5f7b-aad6-cfedc6b150b0'}, {'abstract': 'J. K. Rowling, whose “Silkworm” (written as Robert Galbraith) is No. 2 on the hardcover fiction list, has found herself caught up in a couple of recent controversies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/07/06/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'J. K. Rowling, whose “Silkworm” (written as Robert Galbraith) is No. 2 on the hardcover fiction list, has found herself caught up in a couple of recent controversies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inner Bloke: Back in 2006, when J. K. Rowling was at work on her seventh and final Harry Potter novel, an unnamed publishing insider told The Daily Express in Britain that “she will soon be in a position where she doesn’t have to write if she doesn’t want to, and if she does, she can write whatever she chooses.” Here’s the update, eight years and three non-Potter books later: Rowling wants to write. But she could do without the distractions of fame; that’s why she adopted the pseudonym Robert Galbraith for her Cormoran Strike crime series, also projected to last seven volumes. (“The Silkworm,” the second entry in the series, enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 2, while its predecessor, “The Cuckoo’s Calling,” is No. 8 on the trade paperback list.) “When I ‘unmasked’ myself to my editor,” Rowling says on the official Robert Galbraith website, “one of the first things he said was ‘I never would have thought a woman wrote that.’ Apparently I had successfully channeled my inner bloke!” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/06/books/review/06list-img/06list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 455, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/06/books/review/06list-img/06list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 455, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/06/books/review/06list-img/06list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/07/06/books/review/06list-img/06list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/06/books/review/06list-img/06list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T15:17:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/927031e1-cd6e-5b2f-80e7-307363edea1f', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/927031e1-cd6e-5b2f-80e7-307363edea1f'}, {'abstract': 'After her loss on Friday to Barbora Zahlavova Strycova, Li Na has now twice been one of the fastest top players to the exits in the last two Grand Slam events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/28/sports/tennis/li-na-ousted-at-wimbledon-novak-djokovic-advances.html', 'snippet': 'After her loss on Friday to Barbora Zahlavova Strycova, Li Na has now twice been one of the fastest top players to the exits in the last two Grand Slam events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — After leading out of the gates at the beginning of the year, Li Na has now twice become one of the fastest top players to the exits at the last two Grand Slam events.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/sports/28wimbledon1/28wimbledon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/28/sports/28wimbledon1/28wimbledon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/sports/28wimbledon1/28wimbledon1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/sports/28wimbledon1/28wimbledon1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/sports/28wimbledon1/28wimbledon1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/28/sports/28wimbledon1/28wimbledon1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/28/sports/28wimbledon1/28wimbledon1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Li Na Ousted at Wimbledon; Novak Djokovic Advances', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Li Na Ousted at Wimbledon; Novak Djokovic Advances', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Li Na', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strycova, Barbora Zahlavova', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T15:19:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2b18555-c4cd-548c-94ce-50d8819d0422', 'word_count': 535, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2b18555-c4cd-548c-94ce-50d8819d0422'}, {'abstract': 'A nonlinear look at 150 years of baseball history, with an eye to the social, cultural and political forces behind the game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/the-devils-snake-curve-by-josh-ostergaard.html', 'snippet': 'A nonlinear look at 150 years of baseball history, with an eye to the social, cultural and political forces behind the game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sport of deciphering baseball — of finding embedded within the history, structure, statistics and conventions of our national pastime some secret cultural code that might cast light on our cryptic national character — was born in 1954. Seven years earlier, Jackie Robinson had signed with the Brooklyn Dodgers to break the sport’s color line, finally giving baseball the conditio sine qua non for any democratic claims it might make. Two years before that, American servicemen in Europe had celebrated Germany’s surrender by gleefully retrofitting the Nuremberg stadium into a ballpark, holding regular games where Hitler had recently rallied the Nazis.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Turrentine/0629-bks-Turrentine-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 465, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Turrentine/0629-bks-Turrentine-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 465}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Turrentine/0629-bks-Turrentine-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 794, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Turrentine/0629-bks-Turrentine-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1588, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Turrentine/0629-bks-Turrentine-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Turrentine/0629-bks-Turrentine-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/0629-bks-Turrentine/0629-bks-Turrentine-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Between Innings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Between Innings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ostergaard, Josh', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-27T15:20:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Turrentine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Turrentine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/30c3c262-773d-5f0b-ae42-c2818b12b9ce', 'word_count': 1311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30c3c262-773d-5f0b-ae42-c2818b12b9ce'}, {'abstract': 'With Wrigley Field celebrating its centennial, George F. Will takes a deeply personal look at his hapless Chicago Cubs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/books/review/george-f-wills-a-nice-little-place-on-the-north-side.html', 'snippet': 'With Wrigley Field celebrating its centennial, George F. Will takes a deeply personal look at his hapless Chicago Cubs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Between 1921 and 1970, the Chicago Bears won eight N.F.L. championships as tenants of Wrigley Field. The Yankees and Tigers have clinched the World Series there, but the Cubs, who have played in the park since 1916, never have. In fact, they haven’t won a World Series since 1908, a stretch of futility longer than that of any other North American sports franchise. What’s the problem? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29MCMANUS/29MCMANUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29MCMANUS/29MCMANUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29MCMANUS/29MCMANUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29MCMANUS/29MCMANUS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1401, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/books/review/29MCMANUS/29MCMANUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_nam