': 'nyt://interactive/3187317f-ede4-5314-9bd7-41cf791eeb65'}, {'abstract': 'A retrospective of Eugene Speicher at the Samuel Dorsky Museum of Art in New Paltz, N.Y., examines his career in the context of American realism, but also as an artist who spent months each year in nearby Woodstock.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/nyregion/a-review-of-along-his-own-lines-a-retrospective-of-new-york-realist-eugene-speicher-in-new-paltz.html', 'snippet': 'A retrospective of Eugene Speicher at the Samuel Dorsky Museum of Art in New Paltz, N.Y., examines his career in the context of American realism, but also as an artist who spent months each year in nearby Woodstock.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fortunes in the art world — and particularly those defined by the market — change quickly. Take Eugene Speicher (1883-1962), a painter whom Esquire magazine called America’s most important living painter in 1936, but whose name few people know today (and even fewer can pronounce: “Spiker”). “Along His Own Lines: A Retrospective of New York Realist Eugene Speicher,” organized by Valerie Ann Leeds at the Samuel Dorsky Museum of Art\xa0in New Paltz, N.Y., seeks to redress this, examining Mr. Speicher’s career in the context of American realism, but also as an artist who spent months each year in nearby Woodstock.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ARTSWE/29ARTSWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 557, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ARTSWE/29ARTSWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 557}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/nyregion/29ARTSWE/29ARTSWE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 950, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'le