one}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a4e54a4-e873-535e-a37a-baa1d9166f91', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a4e54a4-e873-535e-a37a-baa1d9166f91'}, {'abstract': 'It’s not their fault. It’s all in the timing of brain development.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/29/opinion/sunday/why-teenagers-act-crazy.html', 'snippet': 'It’s not their fault. It’s all in the timing of brain development.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ADOLESCENCE is practically synonymous in our culture with risk taking, emotional drama and all forms of outlandish behavior. Until very recently, the widely accepted explanation for adolescent angst has been psychological. Developmentally, teenagers face a number of social and emotional challenges, like starting to separate from their parents, getting accepted into a peer group and figuring out who they really are. It doesn’t take a psychoanalyst to realize that these are anxiety-provoking transitions.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/sunday-review/0629COVER/0629COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/29/sunday-review/0629COVER/0629COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/sunday-review/0629COVER/0629COVER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/sunday-review/0629COVER/0629COVER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/sunday-review/0629COVER/0629COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/29/sunday-review/0629COVER/0629COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/29/sunday-review/0629COVER/0629COVER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Teenagers Act Crazy', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Teenagers Act Crazy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Anxiety and Stress', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friedman, Richard A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-28T18:30:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard A. Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b1d05da-f172-57d0-a464-bcbafe541344', 'word_count': 1945, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b1d05da-f172-57d0-a464-bcbafe541344'}, {'abstract': 'For all its lead character’s flaws, it’s the best depiction of a hospital on TV.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/28/what-nurse-jackie-gets-right-about-the-e-r/', 'snippet': 'For all its lead character’s flaws, it’s the best depiction of a hospital on TV.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lot of nurses hate the Showtime series “Nurse Jackie,” which will wrap up its sixth season on Sunday evening. Actually, to be precise, they hate the show’s lead character, the emergency department nurse Jackie Peyton. There’s much to dislike: She is an adulterer, a liar, an unreliable mother and, most significantly, addicted to prescription painkillers. (That’s hardly giving anything away, but be warned: spoilers ahead.)', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-bedside/opinionator-pog-bedside-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-bedside/opinionator-pog-bedside-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-bedside/opinionator-pog-bedside-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What ‘Nurse Jackie’ Gets Right About the E.R.', 'kicker': 'Bedside', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What ‘Nurse Jackie’ Gets Right About the E.R.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': '