d War I — and it reverberates on every continent today, JAKE FLANAGIN writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To many in the United States and, indeed, around the globe, World War I is a historical afterthought, a foggy precursor to World War II.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Great War Still Echoes', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-29T18:52:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/531052ab-3ff2-5792-a787-9a13ba119699', 'word_count': 932, 'uri': 'nyt://article/531052ab-3ff2-5792-a787-9a13ba119699'}, {'abstract': 'The decisive goal in the Netherlands’ victory over Mexico was set up by Arjen Robben’s fall — some might say dive — under a challenge from Mexico’s captain, Rafael Márquez. Klaas-Jan Huntelaar scored on the penalty kick.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/30/sports/worldcup/world-cup-2014-netherlands-tops-mexico-with-2-goals-in-final-minutes.html', 'snippet': 'The decisive goal in the Netherlands’ victory over Mexico was set up by Arjen Robben’s fall — some might say dive — under a challenge from Mexico’s captain, Rafael Márquez. Klaas-Jan Huntelaar scored on the penalty kick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORTALEZA, Brazil — Mexico’s coach, Miguel Herrera, has become an icon during this World Cup for his impassioned exhortations in front of the team bench. Herrera has stomped and stamped, whirled and whipped, flailed and frothed over everything from referee decisions to near misses to, most notably, goals scored by his players. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New Yor