aiis-primary.html', 'snippet': 'Neil Abercrombie’s decision to defy one of the most popular politicians in Hawaii history has come back to rock the governor and the Democratic Party.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WAIANAE, Hawaii — For over 50 years, Senator Daniel K. Inouye, a Democrat, was the closest thing modern-day Hawaii had to political royalty: He began representing the state in Washington two years before its most famous citizen was even born. As he lay dying in 2012, Mr. Inouye asked the governor, Neil Abercrombie, to appoint Representative Colleen Hanabusa, a protégée and the daughter of a gas station owner here, to fill his seat.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/JP-HAWAII-1/JP-HAWAII-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/30/us/JP-HAWAII-1/JP-HAWAII-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/JP-HAWAII-1/JP-HAWAII-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/JP-HAWAII-1/JP-HAWAII-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/JP-HAWAII-1/JP-HAWAII-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/30/us/JP-HAWAII-1/JP-HAWAII-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/JP-HAWAII-1/JP-HAWAII-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Disregarded Request From a Beloved Senator Shakes Up Hawaii’s Primary', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Disregarded Request From a Beloved Senator Shakes Up Hawaii’s Primary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endorsements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hawaii', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABERCROMBIE, NEIL', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ige, David', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hanabusa, Colleen', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Inouye, Daniel K', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schatz, Brian', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-30T00:52:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d8e1ae8-7a86-5edd-8cd1-b04e9e15cd15', 'word_count': 1185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d8e1ae8-7a86-5edd-8cd1-b04e9e15cd15'}, {'abstract': 'An online museum displaying more than 300 pieces of Bruce Springsteen memorabilia is to officially open its figurative doors on Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/30/arts/music/online-museum-for-springsteen.html', 'snippet': 'An online museum displaying more than 300 pieces of Bruce Springsteen memorabilia is to officially open its figurative doors on Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An online museum displaying more than 300 pieces of Bruce Springsteen memorabilia is to officially open its figurative doors on Monday. The virtual museum, blindedbythelight.com, is the product of five years of planning, eight years of amassing a collection of thousands of items and more than three decades of adoration for the Boss by the site’s founder, Michael Crane. Plans are to add 30 to 50 items a month to the site, where they will tell a story that fans might not otherwise see, Mr. Crane said by phone on Sunday from Israel. “For a Bruce fan, this gives you insight into your hero and the way he grew up,” he said. Among the items is an eighth-grade report card, above right, featuring mostly low grades, including D’s in composition, physical education and music. The young Springsteen “didn’t have a great time in Catholic school,” Mr. Crane said. “He wasn’t allowed to be who he wanted to be and to express himself.” Mr. Crane said his favorite item is a photo of Mr. Springsteen and his father on a 1972 visit to Mexico. “Bruce has described their relationship as tumultuous,” Mr. Crane said, “so it’s cool to see Bruce as just a kid laughing with his dad.” Admission is $9.99, which allows a month of access to the site, the ability to download a font that replicates Mr. Springsteen’s handwriting style, the use of a ticket and memorabilia exchange, and entry to a monthly raffle. Mr. Springsteen has no formal involvement with the site.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/arts1/arts1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 219, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/arts1/arts1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 219, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/arts1/arts1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/arts1/arts1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/arts1/arts1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Online Museum for Springsteen', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Online Museum for Springsteen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Springsteen, Bruce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'blindedbythelight.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crane, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-30T00:53:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/108ae7e3-0453-57ad-8ed4-745c16456b76', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/108ae7e3-0453-57ad-8ed4-745c16456b76'}, {'abstract': 'Lawmakers rebuff an attempt to enforce a law banning insider trading by members of Congress and their aides.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/30/opinion/house-hypocrisy-on-insider-trading.html', 'snippet': 'Lawmakers rebuff an attempt to enforce a law banning insider trading by members of Congress and their aides.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was quite the ballyhooed reform of Capitol Hill ethics — an explicit ban on insider trading by members of Congress and their aides enacted two years ago in a bipartisan wave of approval by the Senate and House. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Hypocrisy on Insider Trading', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'House Hypocrisy on Insider Trading', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-30T00:56:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0a47b4e-d7c1-56e5-a936-d830bce015d6', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0a47b4e-d7c1-56e5-a936-d830bce015d6'}, {'abstract': 'The plan opened the door to greater use of telemedicine and could alleviate the doctor shortage, a growing problem as millions of people gain insurance coverage under the Affordable Care Act.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/30/us/medical-boards-draft-plan-to-ease-path-to-out-of-state-and-online-treatment.html', 'snippet': 'The plan opened the door to greater use of telemedicine and could alleviate the doctor shortage, a growing problem as millions of people gain insurance coverage under the Affordable Care Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Officials representing state medical boards across the country have drafted a model law that would make it much easier for doctors licensed in one state to treat patients in other states, whether in person, by videoconference or online.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/DOCTORS/DOCTORS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 845, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/30/us/DOCTORS/DOCTORS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 845}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/DOCTORS/DOCTORS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 727, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/DOCTORS/DOCTORS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/DOCTORS/DOCTORS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/30/us/DOCTORS/DOCTORS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/us/DOCTORS/DOCTORS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Medical Boards Draft Plan to Ease Path to Out-of-State and Online Treatment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Medical Boards Draft Plan to Ease Path to Out-of-State and Online Treatment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pear, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Videophones and Videoconferencing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federation of State Medical Boards', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-30T00:58:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da3f5418-2771-5eee-b065-7510539e49e7', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da3f5418-2771-5eee-b065-7510539e49e7'}, {'abstract': 'Novelists with ties to Africa are gaining prominence in the publishing world as they become more cosmopolitan, and America does, too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/30/arts/new-wave-of-african-writers-with-an-internationalist-bent.html', 'snippet': 'Novelists with ties to Africa are gaining prominence in the publishing world as they become more cosmopolitan, and America does, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than a decade ago, when the young Nigerian writer Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie was struggling to get her first novel, “Purple Hibiscus,” published, an agent told her that things would be easier “if only you were Indian,” because Indian writers were in vogue. Another suggested changing the setting from Nigeria to America. Ms. Adichie didn’t take this as commentary on her work, she said, but on the timidity of the publishing world when it came to unknown writers and unfamiliar cultures, especially African ones. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/WRITERS1/WRITERS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/WRITERS1/WRITERS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/WRITERS1/WRITERS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/WRITERS1/WRITERS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/WRITERS1/WRITERS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/30/arts/WRITERS1/WRITERS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 't