le', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Longo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Longo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5ef430d-c083-52a1-b73f-bbafa6370659', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5ef430d-c083-52a1-b73f-bbafa6370659'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Malesky, an associate professor of political economy at Duke University, provides insights into the recent fraught relations between the two Asian neighbors.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/30/q-and-a-edmund-malesky-on-vietnam-and-china/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Malesky, an associate professor of political economy at Duke University, provides insights into the recent fraught relations between the two Asian neighbors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Edmund J. Malesky, an associate professor of political economy at Duke University, has spent years studying the politics of Vietnam. More recently, he has been working on a governance project involving China. His scholarship on the two countries, both ruled by Communist parties with close ties, has given him a basis for a comparative perspective on the nations’ politics and political economies. In early May, a Chinese state-owned oil company moved an exploratory oil rig into waters off the disputed', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A.: Edmund Malesky on Vietnam and China', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of Vietnam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-30T08:26:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 