 {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Draghi, Mario', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-06-30T10:26:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d0f522e-e8d0-5957-8149-caa1e68aa83f', 'word_count': 649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d0f522e-e8d0-5957-8149-caa1e68aa83f'}, {'abstract': 'Opinions on New Jersey schemes, a meticulous trial, a viral outbreak and Russian profanity.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/06/30/morning-views-25/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on New Jersey schemes, a meticulous trial, a viral outbreak and Russian profanity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times editorial board writes that\xa0Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey “has yet to show sufficient interest in managing the public’s money in ways that benefit the public.” “He has used dubious strategies to avoid raising taxes,” the board adds, citing multiple instances that have hurt the state. A recent one involves the diversion of funds from a Port Authority of New York and New Jersey Hudson River tunnel project to rebuild the deteriorating Pulaski Skyway; it’s being investigated by the\xa0Securities and Exchange Commission and the office of the Manhattan district attorney, Cyrus Vance Jr.\xa0The board says, “Mr. Christie has presented himself nationally as a straight-talking politician who can work with Democrats and attract voters from both parties. That image is taking a beating.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-06-30T10:54:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/507bd723-17be-5ae6-9719-f97708ea260d', 'word_count': 602, 'uri': 'nyt://article/507bd723-17be-5ae6-9719-f97708ea260d'}, {'abstract': 'The new fashion director and chief fashion critic for The Times and International New York Times tells us what readers can expect from her in the Styles section.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/06/30/vanessa-friedman-on-the-evolving-role-of-fashion-journalism/', 'snippet': 'The new fashion director and chief fashion critic for The Times and International New York Times tells us what readers can expect from her in the Styles section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vanessa Friedman, formerly The Financial Times’s first fashion editor, started as fashion director and chief fashion critic for The Times and The International New York Times one month ago. She told us a bit about how she started writing about fashion and what readers can expect from her in the Styles section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/blogs/Friedman/Friedman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/27/blogs/Friedman/Friedman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/27/blogs/Friedman/Friedman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jum